\ shell.4 - a minimal POSIX-flavored shell built on RelF.
\
\ Load on top of an already-bootstrapped kernel.img:
\   S" shell.4" INCLUDED
\   SH
\
\ *** IMPORTANT for anyone editing this file: use only "\" line
\ *** comments for anything spanning more than one physical line.
\ *** A "(...)" comment that spans multiple physical lines can
\ *** silently corrupt parsing once enough code precedes it earlier
\ *** in this file, surfacing as a cascade of unrelated "Undefined
\ *** word" errors when the file is next loaded - confirmed
\ *** empirically in Iteration 33 (see PROGRESS.md), where an
\ *** unmodified, pre-existing six-line "(...)" comment broke only
\ *** after unrelated new code was added earlier in the file; 200
\ *** trivial filler word definitions inserted before it reproduced
\ *** the identical failure in an otherwise pristine copy. Single-line
\ *** "(...)" comments, however long, are fine. If you see this
\ *** symptom (unrelated "Undefined word" errors right after an edit
\ *** that "should" be safe), check for multi-line "(...)" comments
\ *** before assuming a logic bug in whatever was just changed.

\ Scope (v0.1), deliberately limited - see PROGRESS.md for the phased
\ plan this fits into:
\   - word-splitting on whitespace only (no quoting/escaping)
\   - PATH-searched external commands via FORK/EXECVE/WAITPID
\   - builtins: cd, exit, export, pwd
\ NOT implemented yet (planned as separate, later increments):
\   pipes, redirection, $VAR expansion, $(...) command substitution,
\   control structures, functions, job control, quoting of any kind.

\ ---- sizes ----

256 CONSTANT LINE-MAX
64  CONSTANT MAX-ARGS

VARIABLE ARGS-OVERFLOW?
\ Set whenever a word had to be dropped because ARGV is full, and
\ reported once per line by RUN-LINE. Until Iteration 154 hitting
\ MAX-ARGS was completely silent: 70 arguments came back as 62 plus a
\ spurious empty one, and `set -- <70 words>; echo $#` printed 9. The
\ command then RAN, with a quietly different argument list than the
\ script asked for, which is the worst of the available behaviours.
\ Iteration 90 gave diagnostics to SET-SHVAR, SET-FUNC and the
\ positional-parameter stack but never reached this path.
256 CONSTANT PATH-MAX

\ ---- buffers (fixed, allocated once - no dynamic sizing in v0) ----

\ LINE-MAX is the size the line buffers START at: they grow to fit the
\ line being read (see READ-LINE-INTO).
LINE-MAX BUFFER: LINE-BUF

512 CONSTANT NORM-MAX
NORM-MAX BUFFER: NORM-BUF
MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: NORM-WORDS   \ each word's start offset
MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: NORM-WLENS   \ and its length, in NORM-BUF
VARIABLE NORM-WCOUNT
VARIABLE NORM-WSTART      \ start of the word currently open, or -1
\ Declared here rather than beside NORMALIZE-OPERATORS: TOKENIZE
\ reads the normalized line straight out of this buffer (Iteration
\ 111), and this file is one linear source where a word must be
\ defined before it is used.
MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: ARGV

\ ==================================================================
\ Newline
\ ==================================================================
\ This kernel's own CR emits CRLF (13 then 10), which is right for a
\ Forth console and wrong for a shell: every builtin that printed a
\ line - pwd, command -v, readonly -p, every diagnostic - emitted a
\ stray carriage return, so the output differed from every other
\ shell by one byte per line. Found by diffing command.sh against
\ bash, where the ONLY remaining difference was "\r".
\
\ Redefined here rather than spelled "10 EMIT" at each site: every
\ later definition in this file then gets it automatically, and
\ nothing outside shell.4 is affected.
: CR ( --- )  10 EMIT ;

: ARGV@ ( i --- c-addr )  CELLS ARGV + @ ;
PATH-MAX BUFFER: PATHBUF
PATH-MAX BUFFER: CWDBUF
64 BUFFER: ENVNAMBUF

VARIABLE ARGC          ( number of tokens in the current command line )
VARIABLE BG-PID   \ pid of the most recent background command, for $!
VARIABLE LAST-STATUS   ( exit status of the last foreground command, decoded to a plain 0-255 code regardless of whether it came from a builtin or WAITPID )


\ !XT / @XT (storing an execution token as a START-relative offset,
\ so it survives being saved into an image) live in shadow.4, which
\ this file already requires for SHADOW{ itself.

VARIABLE SUPPRESS-EXEC?  \ true when an enclosing if's own then/else
                          \ branch has already determined this whole
                          \ region shouldn't execute anything (either
                          \ because its own condition didn't permit
                          \ this branch, or because an even further
                          \ enclosing if was itself already
                          \ suppressed). A suppressed region still
                          \ needs to be *read* line by line, so a
                          \ nested if/while inside it correctly
                          \ consumes its own then/body/fi rather than
                          \ having its "fi" mistaken for the enclosing
                          \ if's own - it just must not actually run
                          \ anything while doing so. Checked at every
                          \ point that would otherwise execute a
                          \ command (DO-ASSIGN, RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE)
                          \ rather than only gating whether
                          \ RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL is invoked at all, which
                          \ is what let a nested if inside a false
                          \ branch go unparsed and corrupt the
                          \ enclosing if's own fi/else detection -
                          \ found by testing directly, not by
                          \ inspection.
0 LAST-STATUS !        ( defensive: don't rely on a VARIABLE's initial content )

VARIABLE RETURN-PENDING?  \ true from the moment a "return" builtin
                           \ runs (see DISPATCH) until the innermost
                           \ still-executing function's own
                           \ RUN-FUNC-BODY notices and resets it.
                           \ Checked alongside SUPPRESS-EXEC? in
                           \ RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE, so it short-
                           \ circuits any further command in the same
                           \ logical line regardless of ';'/'&&'/'||'
                           \ structure - all of those eventually run
                           \ their own piece through
                           \ RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE, the one place
                           \ this needs to be checked, the same reason
                           \ SUPPRESS-EXEC? is checked only there
                           \ (and DO-ASSIGN) rather than at every
                           \ splitting word individually.
VARIABLE FUNC-DEPTH        \ how many RUN-FUNC-BODY calls are
                           \ currently on the way down (0 at the top
                           \ level) - "return" outside any function is
                           \ diagnosed rather than silently ignored or
                           \ left to corrupt later execution.

VARIABLE LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING?  \ true from the moment "break" or
                           \ "continue" runs (see DISPATCH) until the
                           \ current loop iteration's own
                           \ DO-WHILE-BODY notices and resets it.
                           \ Checked in the same three places
                           \ RETURN-PENDING? is checked - the plain
                           \ RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE guard, so nothing
                           \ further in the same logical line runs
                           \ either - PLUS both RUN-FUNC-BODY's and
                           \ DO-WHILE-BODY's own replay loops, since a
                           \ loop body can call a function, and that
                           \ function might be the one that actually
                           \ calls break/continue meant for the
                           \ *loop*, several call frames further out.
                           \ Unlike RETURN-PENDING?, this flag is only
                           \ ever reset by DO-WHILE-BODY, never by
                           \ RUN-FUNC-BODY - a function's own body
                           \ stops early when it sees this set (it has
                           \ to, the same as for a return), but must
                           \ leave the flag itself alone so it keeps
                           \ propagating outward through however many
                           \ nested function calls separate the
                           \ break/continue from the loop iteration
                           \ that actually needs to see it.
VARIABLE LOOP-BREAK?       \ true only if it was specifically "break"
                           \ (not "continue") that set
                           \ LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? - survives past
                           \ DO-WHILE-BODY's own reset of that flag
                           \ (which only marks the *iteration* as
                           \ over), so the outer while/for loop
                           \ (DO-WHILE/DO-FOR-ITERATE) can check it
                           \ right after each DO-WHILE-BODY call and
                           \ decide whether to stop iterating
                           \ entirely (break) or just proceed to the
                           \ next iteration as usual (continue, or a
                           \ body that simply ran to completion).
VARIABLE LOOP-DEPTH        \ how many while/for loops are currently
                           \ active (0 at the top level) - like
                           \ FUNC-DEPTH, lets break/continue outside
                           \ any loop be diagnosed. Never needs >R/R>
                           \ nesting the way FUNC-DEPTH's own
                           \ analogues do for recursion: a loop body
                           \ (or a function called from one) can't yet
                           \ contain another while/for construct at
                           \ all (see the existing multi-line-
                           \ construct limitation), so two loops can
                           \ never be simultaneously in progress.

0 RETURN-PENDING? !
0 FUNC-DEPTH !
0 LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? !
0 LOOP-BREAK? !
0 LOOP-DEPTH !

\ ==================================================================
\ C-string helpers
\ EXECVE/GETENV/SETENV/CHDIR all speak NUL-terminated C strings, not
\ Forth's own length-prefixed convention - these bridge the two.
\ ==================================================================

: CSTRLEN ( c-addr --- u )
  DUP BEGIN DUP C@ WHILE CHAR+ REPEAT SWAP - ;

\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ Writing to stderr.
\
\ Forth's `."` and TYPE go to stdout, which is wrong for every
\ diagnostic a shell produces: POSIX puts them on fd 2, and a script
\ that redirects stdout must still see its errors. Defined this early
\ because RUN-CHILD needs them, ~2400 lines above where the
\ interactive prompt (the other fd 2 writer) lives.
\
\ Note that shell.4's OLDER diagnostics - "cd: no such directory",
\ "shell: syntax error: ...", "alias: too many aliases" and the rest -
\ still use `."` and so still go to stdout. Moving them is a separate
\ change with its own test churn, recorded in PROGRESS.md rather than
\ folded in here.
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE SH-NL 10 C,   \ a single newline byte. An empty S" " does not
                     \ compile in this kernel - the definition fails
                     \ to complete and the word comes back undefined -
                     \ so a byte constant is how to write exactly one
                     \ newline and nothing else.

: ERR-TYPE ( c-addr u --- )   2 WRITE-FILE DROP ;
: ERR-CSTR ( c-addr --- )     DUP CSTRLEN ERR-TYPE ;
: ERR-NL   ( --- )            SH-NL 1 ERR-TYPE ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Reading a line of any length
\ ==================================================================
\ Lines are read into GROWING buffers (pool.4's BUF-ENSURE): a reader
\ is given a buffer's descriptor and an offset rather than an address,
\ because the buffer may move while it reads - and the old block stays
\ readable until the top-level loop frees it, since code holds
\ addresses into these buffers. LINE-MAX is only the size a buffer
\ starts at (Iteration 249).
\
\ Until then every line had to fit 256 bytes. A longer one was cut and
\ its tail read as the next line, so `echo <250 characters>Z echo
\ INJECTED` ran a command nobody wrote (Iteration 154's finding, made
\ safe in 248 and lifted here). What is left is a ceiling well past any
\ real script, LINE-HARD-MAX: a line longer than that is reported, read
\ to its end and discarded - status 2, nothing of it runs - so feeding
\ the shell a binary file costs a message, not the memory.

1048576 CONSTANT LINE-HARD-MAX

VARIABLE LONG-LINE?   \ was the last line read too long?

: LINE-TOO-LONG ( --- )
  S" shell: line too long" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
  2 LAST-STATUS !  -1 LONG-LINE? ! ;

LINE-MAX BUFFER: DRAIN-BUF

: DRAIN-LINE ( fid --- )
\ Read and discard up to and including the next newline.
  BEGIN
    DRAIN-BUF LINE-MAX 2 PICK READ-LINE DROP   ( fid u flag )
    SWAP LINE-MAX = AND 0=
  UNTIL DROP ;

VARIABLE GL-OFF
VARIABLE GL-BODY
VARIABLE GL-FID
VARIABLE GL-N
VARIABLE GL-K
VARIABLE GL-ANY

: GL-ROOM ( --- c-addr k )
\ Room for at least 64 more characters after what has been read, plus
\ one a caller may use to terminate the line: where it is, and how many
\ characters to ask for.
  GL-OFF @ GL-N @ + 65 + GL-BODY @ BUF-ENSURE
  GL-BODY @ @ GL-OFF @ + GL-N @ +
  GL-BODY @ CELL+ @ GL-OFF @ - GL-N @ - 1- ;

: READ-LINE-INTO ( off body fid --- u flag )
\ Read one line from fid into the growing buffer described at body,
\ starting at offset off. u excludes the newline; flag is false only at
\ end of file. READ-LINE never reads past the newline, so when a read
\ fills the room it was given, the rest of the line is still waiting:
\ grow and read again. A read that fills it exactly is followed by one
\ that returns just the newline.
  SHADOW{ GL-OFF GL-BODY GL-FID | GL-N GL-K GL-ANY }
  0 LONG-LINE? !
  BEGIN
    GL-ROOM DUP GL-K ! GL-FID @ READ-LINE DROP   ( u flag )
    GL-ANY @ OR GL-ANY !
    DUP GL-N @ + GL-N !
    GL-K @ =                                     ( full? )
    GL-OFF @ GL-N @ + LINE-HARD-MAX > IF
      \ Drain only if the line is still going: a read that stopped short
      \ has already taken the newline, and draining then swallowed the
      \ NEXT line - and, as it happened, the rest of the script.
      IF GL-FID @ DRAIN-LINE THEN
      LINE-TOO-LONG 0 -1 EXIT
    THEN
  WHILE REPEAT
  GL-N @ GL-ANY @ ;

VARIABLE AG-OFF
VARIABLE AG-BODY
VARIABLE AG-N

: ACCEPT-INTO ( off body --- u )
\ The same from the terminal, a KEY at a time with ACCEPT's editing:
\ backspace and DEL remove a character, CR and LF end the line. The
\ kernel's ACCEPT cannot be used - it reads the whole line and drops
\ what does not fit, so nothing is left to grow into.
  SHADOW{ AG-OFF AG-BODY | AG-N }
  0 LONG-LINE? !
  BEGIN
    KEY
    DUP 8 = OVER 127 = OR IF
      DROP AG-N @ IF AG-N @ 1- AG-N ! THEN
    ELSE DUP 10 = OVER 13 = OR IF
      DROP LONG-LINE? @ IF LINE-TOO-LONG 0 ELSE AG-N @ THEN EXIT
    ELSE
      AG-OFF @ AG-N @ + LINE-HARD-MAX < IF
        AG-OFF @ AG-N @ + 2 + AG-BODY @ BUF-ENSURE
        AG-BODY @ @ AG-OFF @ + AG-N @ + C!
        AG-N @ 1+ AG-N !
      ELSE
        DROP -1 LONG-LINE? !   \ past the ceiling: read on to the newline
      THEN
    THEN THEN
  0 UNTIL ;

VARIABLE PARSE-N

: PARSE-DECIMAL ( c-addr u --- n )
  \ Parses a simple non-negative decimal integer from (c-addr u) - no
  \ sign handling, matching how exit codes are conventionally
  \ unsigned small integers. An explicit multiply/accumulate loop
  \ rather than the standard >NUMBER, to keep this self-contained and
  \ easy to verify directly, matching EMIT-DECIMAL's own reasoning
  \ elsewhere. u can be 0 (e.g. `exit ""`), guarded the same way as
  \ Iteration 15's crash-hardening fixes - this kernel's DO/LOOP
  \ doesn't treat start=limit as zero iterations. Moved here (from
  \ much later in the file originally) in Iteration 35, so arithmetic
  \ expansion's own numeric-lookup logic - defined much earlier than
  \ this word's original position - can call it directly.
  0 PARSE-N !
  DUP 0 > IF
    0 DO
      DUP I + C@ 48 -
      PARSE-N @ 10 * + PARSE-N !
    LOOP
    DROP
  ELSE
    2DROP
  THEN
  PARSE-N @ ;

: TYPE0 ( c-addr --- )
  DUP CSTRLEN TYPE ;

\ ==================================================================
\ String builder: append bytes/strings into a buffer via a cursor.
\ B-RESET points the cursor at a buffer; B-CHAR/B-CSTR/B-STR append;
\ B-FINISH NUL-terminates. Used for building "dir/cmd" PATH candidates
\ without any stack-juggling - a plain, explicit cursor is much harder
\ to get wrong than shuffling multiple addresses/lengths on the stack
\ (this session's testing of the new primitives found repeated
\ stack-order mistakes doing exactly that kind of shuffling).
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE BUILD-P
VARIABLE B-SRC

: B-RESET ( addr --- )  BUILD-P ! ;
: B-CHAR ( c --- )  BUILD-P @ C! BUILD-P @ 1+ BUILD-P ! ;
: B-CSTR ( c-addr --- )
  BEGIN DUP C@ DUP WHILE B-CHAR CHAR+ REPEAT 2DROP ;
: B-STR ( c-addr u --- )
  \ u can be 0 (e.g. TRY-DIR called for an empty PATH component,
  \ which POSIX treats as meaning "."), guarded for the same reason
  \ as HAS-SLASH? above.
  SWAP B-SRC !
  DUP 0 > IF
    0 DO B-SRC @ I + C@ B-CHAR LOOP
  ELSE
    DROP
  THEN ;
: B-FINISH ( --- )  0 B-CHAR ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Forth-string equality (used for matching builtin names)
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE SEQ-A
VARIABLE SEQ-ALEN
VARIABLE SEQ-B
VARIABLE SEQ-BLEN
VARIABLE SEQ-I

: STR= ( c-addr1 u1 c-addr2 u2 --- f )
  SHADOW{ SEQ-A SEQ-ALEN SEQ-B SEQ-BLEN | SEQ-I }
  SEQ-ALEN @ SEQ-BLEN @ <> IF 0 EXIT THEN
  BEGIN SEQ-I @ SEQ-ALEN @ < WHILE
    SEQ-A @ SEQ-I @ + C@
    SEQ-B @ SEQ-I @ + C@
    <> IF 0 EXIT THEN
    SEQ-I @ 1+ SEQ-I !
  REPEAT
  -1 ;

\ Compare a Forth string against a NUL-terminated C string (used for
\ command-name matching, since ARGV entries are C strings post-tokenize).
: STR0= ( c-addr1 u1 c-addr2 --- f )
  DUP CSTRLEN STR= ;

: CSTR= ( c-addr1 c-addr2 --- f )  \ compares two NUL-terminated C
                                     \ strings (STR0= compares a
                                     \ length-prefixed one against a
                                     \ NUL-terminated one - this is for
                                     \ two NUL-terminated ones, e.g.
                                     \ shell-variable names below).
  SWAP DUP CSTRLEN ROT STR0= ;

VARIABLE SCN-SRC
VARIABLE SCN-LEN
VARIABLE SCN-DEST
VARIABLE SCN-MAX

: SAFE-COPY-NUL ( src-addr u dest-addr dest-max --- ok? )
  \ Copies u bytes from src-addr into dest-addr and NUL-terminates
  \ the result, but only if u+1 fits within dest-max - otherwise
  \ leaves dest-addr untouched and returns false, rather than
  \ truncating or overflowing a fixed-size slot. Explicit variables
  \ rather than stack-juggling for all four arguments, matching this
  \ shell's own established preference (see e.g. CMDSUB-* words).
  SHADOW{ SCN-SRC SCN-LEN SCN-DEST SCN-MAX }
  SCN-LEN @ 1+ SCN-MAX @ > IF 0 EXIT THEN
  SCN-SRC @ SCN-DEST @ SCN-LEN @ MOVE
  0 SCN-DEST @ SCN-LEN @ + C!
  -1 ;

VARIABLE SCC-SRC
VARIABLE SCC-DEST
VARIABLE SCC-MAX

: SAFE-CSTR-COPY ( src-addr dest-addr dest-max --- ok? )
  \ Same as SAFE-COPY-NUL, but src-addr is already a NUL-terminated
  \ C string (its own length is computed here) rather than a
  \ separately-given (addr u) pair.
  SHADOW{ SCC-SRC SCC-DEST SCC-MAX }
  SCC-SRC @ SCC-SRC @ CSTRLEN SCC-DEST @ SCC-MAX @ SAFE-COPY-NUL ;

\ A variable's value is still limited to SHVAR-VALUE-MAX - 1 characters.
\ Until Iteration 249 an over-long one was silently not copied, so the
\ destination kept whatever it held: `a=first; b=<300 characters>` set
\ b to "first", and `read` did the same. Now it is reported, the value
\ is EMPTY, and the command's status is 2. VALUE-FAILED? tells the
\ callers that would otherwise overwrite that status.
VARIABLE VALUE-FAILED?

: VALUE-TOO-LONG ( --- )
  S" shell: value too long" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
  2 LAST-STATUS !  -1 VALUE-FAILED? ! ;

: VALUE-COPY ( src u dest max --- )
\ SAFE-COPY-NUL for a value: never leaves a stale one behind.
  OVER >R SAFE-COPY-NUL R> SWAP IF DROP ELSE 0 SWAP C! VALUE-TOO-LONG THEN ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Shell-local variables: POSIX "shell parameters" distinct from the
\ OS environment - a standalone "NAME=value" line (see ASSIGNMENT-
\ EQPOS/DO-ASSIGN further down) sets one of these without exporting
\ it, so it expands via $NAME/${NAME} (see LOOKUP-VAR, used by
\ EXPAND-VAR below) but a child process never sees it - unlike
\ `export NAME=value`, which sets both this table and calls SETENV
\ (see DO-EXPORT). A simple linear table (name/value pairs copied
\ into fixed-size buffers, not just pointers - ARGV entries point
\ into LINE-BUF, which gets overwritten on the very next line) -
\ matches how this shell already avoids hashing/dynamic structures
\ everywhere else (ARGV, the PATH search, etc).
\ ==================================================================

32  CONSTANT MAX-SHVARS
64  CONSTANT SHVAR-NAME-MAX
256 CONSTANT SHVAR-VALUE-MAX
MAX-SHVARS SHVAR-NAME-MAX * BUFFER: SHVAR-NAMES
MAX-SHVARS SHVAR-VALUE-MAX * BUFFER: SHVAR-VALUES
MAX-SHVARS BUFFER: SHVAR-RO   \ readonly flag per slot
VARIABLE SHVAR-COUNT
0 SHVAR-COUNT !

: SHVAR-NAME-SLOT ( i --- addr )   SHVAR-NAME-MAX * SHVAR-NAMES + ;
: SHVAR-VALUE-SLOT ( i --- addr )  SHVAR-VALUE-MAX * SHVAR-VALUES + ;

VARIABLE FS-I

: FIND-SHVAR ( name-addr --- i | -1 )  \ linear scan; name-addr is a
                                         \ NUL-terminated C string.
  SHADOW{ | FS-I }
  BEGIN FS-I @ SHVAR-COUNT @ < WHILE
    DUP FS-I @ SHVAR-NAME-SLOT CSTR= IF
      DROP FS-I @ EXIT
    THEN
    FS-I @ 1+ FS-I !
  REPEAT
  DROP -1 ;

: GET-SHVAR ( name-addr --- value-addr | 0 )
  FIND-SHVAR
  DUP -1 = IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  SHVAR-VALUE-SLOT ;

VARIABLE SV-NAME
VARIABLE SV-VALUE
VARIABLE SV-IDX

: SET-SHVAR ( name-addr value-addr --- )  \ both NUL-terminated C
                                            \ strings. Updates an
                                            \ existing slot if the
                                            \ name is already present,
                                            \ otherwise appends a new
                                            \ one - silently does
                                            \ nothing if the table is
                                            \ full, or if either
                                            \ string doesn't fit its
                                            \ slot (SAFE-CSTR-COPY's
                                            \ own bounds check),
                                            \ matching this shell's
                                            \ sparse error handling
                                            \ elsewhere. Captures both
                                            \ arguments into variables
                                            \ immediately, rather than
                                            \ carrying them through the
                                            \ FIND-SHVAR/SHVAR-COUNT
                                            \ stack logic below them -
                                            \ the first version of this
                                            \ word didn't, and ended up
                                            \ feeding SAFE-CSTR-COPY
                                            \ the wrong address (found
                                            \ by testing directly: the
                                            \ name and value slots came
                                            \ out swapped).
  SHADOW{ SV-NAME SV-VALUE | SV-IDX }
  SV-NAME @ FIND-SHVAR
  DUP -1 = IF
    DROP
    SHVAR-COUNT @ MAX-SHVARS < 0= IF
      \ GOALS.md's memory policy: a full fixed table must never fail
      \ silently. This used to just EXIT, so the 33rd variable was
      \ never set and every later use of it expanded to empty -
      \ wrong output rather than an error (found in Iteration 44,
      \ diagnosed only now).
      ." shell: too many shell variables" CR
      EXIT
    THEN
    SHVAR-COUNT @ DUP 1+ SHVAR-COUNT !
    DUP SHVAR-RO + 0 SWAP C!        \ new slots start writable
  THEN
  SV-IDX !
  \ A readonly variable refuses assignment, per POSIX. Checked here
  \ rather than in each caller, so every path that can set a variable
  \ (plain assignment, export, ${VAR:=word}, a for loop's own control
  \ variable) is covered by one test.
  SV-IDX @ SHVAR-RO + C@ IF
    ." shell: readonly variable" CR
    EXIT
  THEN
  SV-NAME @ SV-IDX @ SHVAR-NAME-SLOT SHVAR-NAME-MAX SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  SV-VALUE @ DUP CSTRLEN SV-IDX @ SHVAR-VALUE-SLOT SHVAR-VALUE-MAX VALUE-COPY ;

: REMOVE-SHVAR ( name-addr --- )  \ shifts the last entry into the
                                    \ removed slot rather than
                                    \ shifting every following entry
                                    \ down - order doesn't matter for
                                    \ a lookup-only table. A no-op if
                                    \ the name isn't present.
  FIND-SHVAR
  DUP -1 = IF DROP EXIT THEN
  DUP SHVAR-COUNT @ 1- = IF
    DROP SHVAR-COUNT @ 1- SHVAR-COUNT !
    EXIT
  THEN
  SHVAR-COUNT @ 1- SHVAR-NAME-SLOT
  OVER SHVAR-NAME-SLOT SHVAR-NAME-MAX MOVE
  SHVAR-COUNT @ 1- SHVAR-VALUE-SLOT
  OVER SHVAR-VALUE-SLOT SHVAR-VALUE-MAX MOVE
  DROP
  SHVAR-COUNT @ 1- SHVAR-COUNT ! ;

DEFER POS-DIGIT-CALL   \ patched once POS-PARAM-SLOT exists, below

: LOOKUP-VAR ( name-addr --- value-addr | 0 )  \ shell-local storage
                                                 \ first (covers every
                                                 \ assigned variable,
                                                 \ exported or not),
                                                 \ falling back to
                                                 \ GETENV (covers a
                                                 \ variable this shell
                                                 \ never itself
                                                 \ assigned but
                                                 \ inherited from its
                                                 \ own environment,
                                                 \ e.g. $HOME).
  \ A single digit names a positional parameter, not a shell variable.
  \ Handled here rather than at each call site so every braced form
  \ gets it at once - ${2}, ${3:+x}, ${#2}, ${2#pat} - where before
  \ only the bare $2 spelling worked, because EXPAND-VAR special-cased
  \ digits and EXPAND-BRACED-VAR went straight to this word.
  DUP POS-DIGIT-CALL ?DUP IF SWAP DROP EXIT THEN
  DUP GET-SHVAR DUP 0= IF DROP GETENV ELSE SWAP DROP THEN ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Positional parameters ($1-$9, $#, $@, $*) - a script's own
\ command-line arguments at the top level, or a function's own call
\ arguments within its body (see SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-ARGV/
\ SAVE-POS-PARAMS/RESTORE-POS-PARAMS, used by RUN-FUNC-BODY). Scope
\ limit: single-digit access only ($1-$9, not ${10} and beyond) -
\ POS-PARAM-COUNT is itself clamped to MAX-POS-PARAMS, so $# won't
\ report a true count past 9 either; a real, if less common, gap this
\ project may want to close later given the payoff (${10}+ needs
\ EXPAND-VAR's own ${NAME} braced path to accept a decimal name, not
\ just POSIX portable name characters).
\ ==================================================================

9 CONSTANT MAX-POS-PARAMS
256 CONSTANT POS-PARAM-MAX
32 CONSTANT MAX-POS-PARAM-DEPTH

MAX-POS-PARAMS POS-PARAM-MAX * BUFFER: POS-PARAMS
VARIABLE POS-PARAM-COUNT

MAX-POS-PARAM-DEPTH MAX-POS-PARAMS POS-PARAM-MAX * * BUFFER: POS-PARAMS-SAVE
MAX-POS-PARAM-DEPTH CELLS BUFFER: POS-PARAM-COUNT-SAVE

: POS-PARAM-SLOT ( i --- addr )  \ i is 0-based: $1 is slot 0, $9 is
                                   \ slot 8.
  POS-PARAM-MAX * POS-PARAMS + ;

: POS-PARAMS-SAVE-SLOT ( depth --- addr )
  MAX-POS-PARAMS POS-PARAM-MAX * * POS-PARAMS-SAVE + ;

: POS-PARAM-COUNT-SAVE-SLOT ( depth --- addr )
  CELLS POS-PARAM-COUNT-SAVE + ;

VARIABLE SPP-I

: POS-DIGIT-LOOKUP ( name-addr --- value-addr | 0 )
  \ "1".."9" name a positional parameter. Anything else - including
  \ "0", which is the script name rather than a parameter - is not
  \ ours, and an out-of-range digit expands to empty like an unset
  \ variable.
  DUP CSTRLEN 1 <> IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  C@ 48 -
  DUP 1 < IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP 9 > IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  1-
  DUP POS-PARAM-COUNT @ < 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  POS-PARAM-SLOT ;

' POS-DIGIT-LOOKUP IS POS-DIGIT-CALL

: SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-ARGV ( --- )
  \ Populates POS-PARAMS/POS-PARAM-COUNT from the *current* global
  \ ARGV/ARGC, starting at ARGV[1] (skipping ARGV[0], the function
  \ name itself) - called by RUN-FUNC-BODY right as a function call
  \ begins, while the global ARGV still holds the calling line (before
  \ the function's own first body line is replayed and overwrites it).
  SHADOW{ | SPP-I }
  BEGIN
    SPP-I @ 1+ ARGC @ <
    SPP-I @ MAX-POS-PARAMS < AND
  WHILE
    SPP-I @ 1+ ARGV@ SPP-I @ POS-PARAM-SLOT POS-PARAM-MAX
    SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
    SPP-I @ 1+ SPP-I !
  REPEAT
  ARGC @ 1- DUP MAX-POS-PARAMS > IF DROP MAX-POS-PARAMS THEN
  POS-PARAM-COUNT ! ;

VARIABLE SPFSA-I

: SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-SYS-ARGS ( --- )
  \ Populates POS-PARAMS/POS-PARAM-COUNT from relf's own command-line
  \ arguments beyond the script path itself (SYS-ARG(0)) - so
  \ "relfsh script.sh arg1 arg2" makes $1/$2 available within the
  \ script from the very start, without needing an explicit "set"
  \ call. Called once, from the top-level dispatch, right before
  \ SH-FILE runs the script.
  SHADOW{ | SPFSA-I }
  BEGIN
    SPFSA-I @ 1+ SYS-ARGC <
    SPFSA-I @ MAX-POS-PARAMS < AND
  WHILE
    SPFSA-I @ 1+ SYS-ARG SPFSA-I @ POS-PARAM-SLOT POS-PARAM-MAX
    SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
    SPFSA-I @ 1+ SPFSA-I !
  REPEAT
  SYS-ARGC 1- DUP 0 < IF DROP 0 THEN
  DUP MAX-POS-PARAMS > IF DROP MAX-POS-PARAMS THEN
  POS-PARAM-COUNT ! ;

VARIABLE SPPD-DEPTH

: SAVE-POS-PARAMS ( depth --- )
  SHADOW{ SPPD-DEPTH }
  SPPD-DEPTH @ POS-PARAMS-SAVE-SLOT
  POS-PARAMS SWAP MAX-POS-PARAMS POS-PARAM-MAX * MOVE
  SPPD-DEPTH @ POS-PARAM-COUNT-SAVE-SLOT POS-PARAM-COUNT @ SWAP ! ;

: RESTORE-POS-PARAMS ( depth --- )
  SHADOW{ SPPD-DEPTH }
  SPPD-DEPTH @ POS-PARAMS-SAVE-SLOT POS-PARAMS MAX-POS-PARAMS POS-PARAM-MAX * MOVE
  SPPD-DEPTH @ POS-PARAM-COUNT-SAVE-SLOT @ POS-PARAM-COUNT ! ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Tokenizer: splits LINE-BUF (u chars from ACCEPT) on whitespace, in
\ place. Supports single quotes ('...', fully literal, no escapes
\ recognized inside), double quotes ("...", recognizing \" and \\ as
\ escapes for a literal " or \ - no $/`` handling, since there's no
\ expansion to apply them to), and backslash-escaping a single
\ character outside any quotes. Adjacent quoted/unquoted/escaped
\ spans concatenate into one token, matching real shell behavior
\ (e.g. 'ab'c"d e"f is one token, "abcd ef"). Quote/escape characters
\ themselves are stripped; what's left is compacted in place, which
\ is always safe here since compaction only ever shrinks a token
\ (removes bytes), never grows it. Each token's start address is
\ recorded into ARGV (NUL-terminated per EXECVE's own convention);
\ whether *any* part of a token came from inside quotes or an escape
\ is recorded in the parallel ARGV-QUOTED array, so a token like '<'
\ or "cd" that happens to match an operator or builtin name isn't
\ mistaken for one downstream (see PARSE-REDIRECTIONS, AT-PIPE?,
\ DISPATCH) - it stays a literal argument, matching real shells.
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE TOK-POS
VARIABLE TOK-END
VARIABLE TOK-OUT
VARIABLE TK-I
VARIABLE TK-J
VARIABLE TK-Q

4096 CONSTANT TOK-BUF-MAX
TOK-BUF-MAX BUFFER: TOK-BUF
TOK-BUF-MAX BUFFER: EXP-BUF
VARIABLE TOK-OUT-END
\ TOK-BUF holds the line's words as they were written; EXP-BUF holds
\ them after expansion. Splitting the two is what lets expansion
\ happen at a different time from tokenizing (Iteration 113).
\ Expanded words are written HERE, not back over the line they came
\ from. Until Iteration 108 the tokenizer compacted in place: TOK-OUT
\ trailed TOK-POS through LINE-BUF, which worked for quote-stripping
\ (output is always shorter) and not for expansion (a value can be
\ longer than the "$name" it replaces). ENSURE-ROOM existed to shift
\ the unread input rightward and keep the write cursor behind the read
\ cursor, and four separate bugs were one call to it being missing -
\ $VAR (26), the numeric expansions (46), "$*"'s separator (99) and
\ "$@"'s field break (103).
\
\ With a separate output buffer the hazard does not exist: there is no
\ shared buffer to overrun. That is item 2 of PARSE-EXPAND-PLAN.md,
\ and the first part of its Stage 1 - the input line now survives
\ tokenizing intact, which is what re-expanding it per command will
\ need.
VARIABLE TOK-WAS-QUOTED?
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: ARGV-QUOTED   ( one byte flag per ARGV entry )
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: ARGV-NAME-QUOTED  \ true if THIS token's very
                                          \ first character came from
                                          \ inside a quote - used only
                                          \ by TRY-ASSIGNMENT, to
                                          \ reject a fully-quoted
                                          \ 'NAME=value' (the name
                                          \ itself quoted, not a real
                                          \ assignment) while still
                                          \ accepting NAME="value"
                                          \ (only the value quoted, a
                                          \ perfectly valid one).
                                          \ Distinct from ARGV-QUOTED
                                          \ itself, which records "was
                                          \ ANY part of this token
                                          \ quoted" - using that one
                                          \ here would incorrectly
                                          \ reject NAME="value" too,
                                          \ since it can't distinguish
                                          \ which part was quoted.

: WS? ( c --- f )  DUP 32 = SWAP 9 = OR ;

: SKIP-WS ( --- )
  BEGIN
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS ! REPEAT ;

: EMIT-TOK-CHAR ( c --- )
  \ The bounds check is the only limit left on how much a line can
  \ expand to. Over-long output is truncated rather than overrunning,
  \ matching how every other fixed-size copy in this file behaves.
  \
  \ TOK-OUT-END rather than a fixed buffer: the same emit path fills
  \ TOK-BUF with raw words and EXP-BUF with expanded ones, and only
  \ whoever set TOK-OUT knows which (Iteration 113).
  TOK-OUT @ TOK-OUT-END @ < IF
    TOK-OUT @ C!  TOK-OUT @ 1+ TOK-OUT !
  ELSE
    DROP
  THEN ;

\ ==================================================================
\ $VAR expansion: $NAME, ${NAME}, $? (last exit status), $$ (this
\ shell's own PID). Recognized both outside quotes and inside double
\ quotes - not inside single quotes, which stay fully literal
\ (COPY-SINGLE-QUOTED never looks for $ at all, matching POSIX).
\ Deliberately narrow scope: no ${VAR:-default}-style modifiers, no
\ positional parameters ($1/$@/$#), no command substitution - just
\ environment-variable lookup (reusing GETENV, same as everywhere
\ else in this shell - there's no separate "shell variable" concept)
\ plus the two special forms. An expanded value is never re-split on
\ whitespace (unlike a real shell's unquoted expansion) - the whole
\ expansion becomes part of whichever token it's embedded in, and is
\ never re-interpreted as an operator/builtin name downstream, same
\ as quoted/escaped content (see ARGV-QUOTED).
\ ==================================================================

: NAME-CHAR? ( c --- f )  \ POSIX portable variable-name characters
  DUP 95 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN                  ( underscore )
  DUP 48 < 0= OVER 58 < AND IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN    ( 0-9 )
  DUP 65 < 0= OVER 91 < AND IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN    ( A-Z )
  DUP 97 < 0= OVER 123 < AND IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN   ( a-z )
  DROP 0 ;

64 BUFFER: VARNAME-BUF
VARIABLE VN-LEN
24 BUFFER: DIGIT-BUF
VARIABLE DIGIT-N

: EMIT-DECIMAL ( n --- )
  \ Appends n's decimal digits via EMIT-TOK-CHAR (used for $? and $$).
  \ An explicit divide/mod loop rather than the pictured-numeric-output
  \ words, to keep this self-contained and easy to verify directly.
  DUP 0< IF 45 EMIT-TOK-CHAR NEGATE THEN
  DUP 0= IF DROP 48 EMIT-TOK-CHAR EXIT THEN
  0 DIGIT-N !
  BEGIN DUP 0 > WHILE
    DUP 10 MOD 48 +  DIGIT-BUF DIGIT-N @ + C!
    DIGIT-N @ 1+ DIGIT-N !
    10 /
  REPEAT
  DROP
  DIGIT-N @ 0 DO
    DIGIT-BUF DIGIT-N @ 1- I - + C@ EMIT-TOK-CHAR
  LOOP ;

: TYPE0-TO-TOK ( c-addr --- )  \ copies a NUL-terminated C string's
                                 \ characters into the token being
                                 \ built - the expanded value, from GETENV
  BEGIN DUP C@ DUP WHILE EMIT-TOK-CHAR CHAR+ REPEAT 2DROP ;

\ ==================================================================
\ IFS field splitting: an *unquoted* $VAR/${...}/$(...)/$((...))
\ expansion result is split into separate ARGV entries wherever a run
\ of IFS whitespace appears within it - unlike a literal character
\ copied straight from the input line, or one coming from inside
\ double quotes (never split, matching POSIX). Scope limit: only
\ space/tab are treated as IFS here - not yet a customizable $IFS
\ shell variable, and not newline either (an embedded newline in an
\ expansion result is left as an ordinary character for now). Whether
\ TOK-WAS-QUOTED? is currently set is exactly the signal already
\ available for "am I inside double quotes right now" (COPY-DOUBLE-
\ QUOTED sets it before calling EXPAND-VAR for a $VAR within "..."),
\ so no new quote-tracking is needed here at all.
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE IFS-SPLIT-PENDING?  \ true once an IFS character has been
                               \ seen but not yet resolved - the
                               \ actual split is deferred until the
                               \ next non-IFS character actually needs
                               \ writing, so a run of trailing IFS
                               \ whitespace (at the very end of the
                               \ expansion, or the token) never
                               \ produces a spurious empty trailing
                               \ field, and multiple consecutive IFS
                               \ characters collapse into a single
                               \ split rather than several empty ones.
VARIABLE TAP-S
VARIABLE TAP-L
VARIABLE TAP-I

: TAP-NAME-CHAR? ( c --- f )
  DUP 95 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP 47 > OVER 58 < AND IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP 64 > OVER 91 < AND IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP 96 > SWAP 123 < AND ;

: TOKEN-IS-ASSIGN-PREFIX? ( --- f )
  \ Does the token built so far look like "NAME=" ? Read from the
  \ token being accumulated (ARGV's current entry through TOK-OUT)
  \ rather than kept as a flag, so it needs no reset discipline.
  \ Only a word in ASSIGNMENT POSITION counts. "echo a=$(...)" is an
  \ ordinary argument that happens to contain '=', and POSIX field
  \ splitting DOES apply to it - suppressing it there was wrong, and
  \ was found by the differential harness on its first run
  \ (Iteration 83). Approximated as "first word of the command": that
  \ covers "q=$p" and "x=$(...)" and correctly excludes an argument.
  \ A second assignment in "a=1 b=$p cmd" is not covered - POSIX
  \ allows a run of them - and is a known narrowing.
  ARGC @ 0 <> IF 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ TAP-S !
  TAP-S @ 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  TOK-OUT @ TAP-S @ - TAP-L !
  TAP-L @ 2 < IF 0 EXIT THEN
  0 TAP-I !
  BEGIN
    TAP-I @ TAP-L @ < IF TAP-S @ TAP-I @ + C@ 61 <> ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    TAP-S @ TAP-I @ + C@ TAP-NAME-CHAR? 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
    TAP-I @ 1+ TAP-I !
  REPEAT
  TAP-I @ 0 > TAP-I @ TAP-L @ < AND ;

VARIABLE IN-ASSIGN-CONTEXT?
\ True once the token being built looks like "NAME=" - i.e. an
\ assignment word. POSIX does NOT field-split the value of an
\ assignment, so "q=$p" and "x=$(echo a b)" each stay a single word
\ however many blanks the expansion contains. Set by SCAN-TOKEN as it
\ scans, cleared at the start of every token.

VARIABLE IN-DQ-CONTEXT?      \ true while currently inside a real,
                               \ syntactic double-quoted span - set (and
                               \ later cleared) by COPY-DOUBLE-QUOTED
                               \ itself around its own body. Distinct
                               \ from TOK-WAS-QUOTED?, which EXPAND-VAR
                               \ sets unconditionally for *every*
                               \ expansion it performs (marking the
                               \ result as "quoted" so it's never
                               \ re-interpreted as an operator or
                               \ keyword later, regardless of whether
                               \ the expansion is syntactically inside
                               \ real double quotes or bare/unquoted) -
                               \ reusing that flag here would make
                               \ EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR think *every*
                               \ expansion is quoted and never split
                               \ anything at all (found as a real bug
                               \ by testing directly: a bare, unquoted
                               \ $x came out completely empty).

VARIABLE IN-BRACED-WORD?     \ true while scanning the word of a
                               \ ${VAR:-word}. Literal text there is
                               \ part of the expansion's result and is
                               \ field-split when unquoted, so
                               \ "n ${nope:-a b c}" passes three
                               \ arguments; literal text in an ordinary
                               \ word never is, since SCAN-TOKEN stops
                               \ at the separator instead of emitting
                               \ it.

VARIABLE EXPAND-SUPPRESSED?  \ true while a word is being scanned only
                               \ to find where it ends, not for its
                               \ value - the untaken branch of
                               \ ${VAR:-word}. Everything is still
                               \ dispatched (that is what makes the two
                               \ branches agree about the word's
                               \ extent), but a $(...) inside must not
                               \ actually run. See
                               \ EXPAND-BRACED-WORD-ASIDE.

4 BUFFER: IFS-NM
VARIABLE IFSC-C
VARIABLE IFSC-P

: IFS-CHAR? ( c --- f )
  \ Is c a field separator? POSIX: the characters in $IFS, defaulting
  \ to space/tab/newline when IFS is unset. This used to be hardcoded
  \ to space and tab, so `IFS=':'` had no effect at all on splitting -
  \ the variable could be set and was simply never read.
  \
  \ An IFS that is set but *empty* correctly disables splitting
  \ entirely, since no character is a member of an empty set.
  IFSC-C !
  73 IFS-NM C!  70 IFS-NM 1+ C!  83 IFS-NM 2 + C!  0 IFS-NM 3 + C!
  IFS-NM LOOKUP-VAR IFSC-P !
  IFSC-P @ 0= IF
    IFSC-C @ 32 = IFSC-C @ 9 = OR IFSC-C @ 10 = OR EXIT
  THEN
  BEGIN IFSC-P @ C@ WHILE
    IFSC-P @ C@ IFSC-C @ = IF -1 EXIT THEN
    IFSC-P @ 1+ IFSC-P !
  REPEAT
  0 ;

: IFS-SPLIT-HERE ( --- )
  \ Finishes the current ARGV entry (NUL-terminating at TOK-OUT,
  \ recording it as unquoted, advancing ARGC) and starts a new one
  \ right after, if there's still room in ARGV.
  0 TOK-OUT @ C!
  0 ARGV-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
  ARGC @ MAX-ARGS 1- < IF
    ARGC @ 1+ ARGC !
    TOK-OUT @ 1+ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + !
  ELSE
    -1 ARGS-OVERFLOW? !
  THEN
  TOK-OUT @ 1+ TOK-OUT ! ;

: EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR ( c --- )
  \ Like EMIT-TOK-CHAR, but for a character coming from an unquoted
  \ expansion result, subject to IFS field splitting.
  IN-DQ-CONTEXT? @ TOKEN-IS-ASSIGN-PREFIX? OR IF
    EMIT-TOK-CHAR
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP IFS-CHAR? IF
    DROP
    -1 IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? !
  ELSE
    IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? @ IF
      0 IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? !
      \ Only actually split if the current entry has received some
      \ content already (TOK-OUT has moved past where it started) -
      \ otherwise this is leading IFS whitespace at the very start of
      \ the token, which should be absorbed rather than producing a
      \ spurious empty leading field.
      TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ <> IF
        IFS-SPLIT-HERE
      THEN
    THEN
    EMIT-TOK-CHAR
  THEN ;

: TYPE0-TO-TOK-SPLIT ( c-addr --- )  \ like TYPE0-TO-TOK, but the
                                       \ copied characters are subject
                                       \ to IFS field splitting - used
                                       \ for an *unquoted* $VAR/${...}/
                                       \ $(...)/$((...)) result;
                                       \ TYPE0-TO-TOK itself remains
                                       \ used for GETENV/other paths
                                       \ that don't go through
                                       \ expansion at all.
  BEGIN DUP C@ DUP WHILE EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR CHAR+ REPEAT 2DROP ;

\ ==================================================================
\ $(command) - command substitution
\ Runs the enclosed text as a single external command (no builtins,
\ same v0.7 scope limit as pipe segments not supporting builtins
\ either), captures its stdout via a pipe, strips trailing newlines
\ (matching POSIX), and splices the captured bytes into the token
\ being built - literally, with no further expansion or quote
\ processing applied to the captured output itself.
\
\ This runs *during* the outer line's own tokenization (SCAN-TOKEN or
\ COPY-DOUBLE-QUOTED call EXPAND-VAR, which calls this, while LINE-BUF/
\ ARGV/ARGC/TOK-POS/TOK-END/TOK-OUT are all still mid-scan for the
\ *outer* line) - reusing TOKENIZE/ARGV/LINE-BUF for the *inner*
\ command's own parsing would corrupt that in-progress outer state.
\ So this copies the inner text into its own buffer and runs it in the
\ forked child through the REAL tokenizer, as replayed input lines -
\ the same machinery a loop body uses. The child has its own copy of
\ LINE-BUF/ARGV/TOK-*, so there is nothing left to corrupt.
\
\ That replaced CMDSUB-TOKENIZE, a private whitespace-only tokenizer
\ that had been extended three times (Iterations 71, 73, 104) and
\ still could not do pipes, redirection, ';', or several commands.
\ Ramey's regret about bash's own parse_comsub - that it "knows an
\ uncomfortable amount of shell syntax and duplicates rather more of
\ the token-reading code than is optimal" - is recorded in GOALS.md
\ against this exact word, with the instruction to replace rather
\ than extend it. (Iteration 105.)
\
\ RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD needs the replay machinery and RUN-CHILD, defined much later
\ in this file (in the pipe/path-search sections) - broken via the
\ same deferred-word pattern already used for RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL: a
\ stub here, patched to the real word's execution token once it's
\ defined below.
\ ==================================================================

LINE-MAX BUFFER: CMDSUB-LINE-BUF
LINE-MAX BUFFER: CMDSUB-LINES     \ the same text, NUL-separated per
                                   \ line, ready to be replayed
VARIABLE CMDSUB-LINES-LEN
VARIABLE CS-POS
VARIABLE CS-COPY-I
VARIABLE CS-SQ?
VARIABLE CS-DQ?
VARIABLE CS-CLOSER
VARIABLE CS-DEPTH        \ nested "$(" still open within this one
VARIABLE CS-NONBLANK?

256 CONSTANT CMDSUB-OUT-MAX
CMDSUB-OUT-MAX BUFFER: CMDSUB-OUT-BUF
VARIABLE CMDSUB-OUT-LEN

: CMDSUB-SPLIT-LINES ( u --- )
  \ CMDSUB-LINE-BUF holds u raw bytes of command text, which may span
  \ several physical lines. Rewrites it into CMDSUB-LINES with each
  \ newline replaced by a NUL - the NUL-separated shape every replay
  \ source in this file uses - and records whether any non-blank
  \ character was seen at all, so "$( )" runs nothing.
  SHADOW{ CS-POS | CS-COPY-I }
  CS-POS @ 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER CMDSUB-LINES
  0 CS-NONBLANK? !
  BEGIN CS-COPY-I @ CS-POS @ < WHILE
    CMDSUB-LINE-BUF CS-COPY-I @ + C@
    DUP 10 = IF DROP 0 ELSE
      DUP WS? 0= IF -1 CS-NONBLANK? ! THEN
    THEN
    CMDSUB-LINES CS-COPY-I @ + C!
    CS-COPY-I @ 1+ CS-COPY-I !
  REPEAT
  0 CMDSUB-LINES CS-POS @ + C!
  CS-POS @ 1+ CMDSUB-LINES-LEN ! ;

DEFER RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD-CALL


VARIABLE CS-FD
VARIABLE CMDSUB-PIPE-RD
VARIABLE CMDSUB-PIPE-WR
VARIABLE CMDSUB-PID

: READ-ALL-CMDSUB-OUTPUT ( fd --- )
  \ Reads everything available from fd into CMDSUB-OUT-BUF, up to its
  \ fixed capacity - output beyond that is silently truncated rather
  \ than overflowing the buffer.
  SHADOW{ CS-FD }
  0 CMDSUB-OUT-LEN !
  BEGIN
    CMDSUB-OUT-MAX CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ - 0 >
  WHILE
    CMDSUB-OUT-BUF CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ +
    CMDSUB-OUT-MAX CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ -
    CS-FD @ READ-FILE DROP
    DUP 0 = IF DROP EXIT THEN
    CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ + CMDSUB-OUT-LEN !
  REPEAT ;

\ ==================================================================
\ The body arena
\ ==================================================================
\ Defined here rather than down with while/for because the replay
\ machinery below is its first user - the same file-ordering care the
\ deferred-word patches elsewhere in this file exist for.

4096 CONSTANT BODY-ARENA-INIT

VARIABLE BODY-ARENA-BASE    \ heap address, 0 until first use
VARIABLE BODY-ARENA-SIZE    \ current capacity in bytes
VARIABLE BODY-ARENA-TOP     \ bump pointer, an OFFSET not an address
VARIABLE BA-NEED
VARIABLE BA-NEWSIZE

: BODY@ ( offset --- addr )  BODY-ARENA-BASE @ + ;

: BODY-GROW ( needed --- )
  \ Grow (or first create) the arena to at least `needed` bytes,
  \ doubling so repeated growth stays amortized. RESIZE is allowed to
  \ MOVE the block - measured, not assumed: growing a blocked
  \ allocation past glibc's mmap threshold relocates it nearly every
  \ time. That is safe here precisely because nothing outside holds a
  \ raw pointer into the arena; everything holds an offset and goes
  \ through BODY@.
  SHADOW{ BA-NEED }
  BODY-ARENA-SIZE @ DUP 0= IF DROP BODY-ARENA-INIT THEN BA-NEWSIZE !
  BEGIN BA-NEWSIZE @ BA-NEED @ < WHILE
    BA-NEWSIZE @ 2 * BA-NEWSIZE !
  REPEAT
  BODY-ARENA-BASE @ 0= IF
    BA-NEWSIZE @ ALLOCATE ABORT" shell: body arena out of memory"
  ELSE
    BODY-ARENA-BASE @ BA-NEWSIZE @ RESIZE ABORT" shell: body arena out of memory"
  THEN
  BODY-ARENA-BASE !
  BA-NEWSIZE @ BODY-ARENA-SIZE ! ;

: BODY-ALLOC ( n --- offset )
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @ SWAP OVER +            ( old new )
  DUP BODY-ARENA-SIZE @ > IF DUP BODY-GROW THEN
  BODY-ARENA-TOP !                        ( old )
  ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Arithmetic expansion: $((expression)) - a real, precedence-climbing
\ recursive-descent expression grammar (each level below binds
\ tighter than the one above it, matching C/POSIX precedence):
\   AE-OR   := AE-AND ( "||" AE-AND )*
\   AE-AND  := AE-EQ ( "&&" AE-EQ )*
\   AE-EQ   := AE-REL ( ("=="|"!=") AE-REL )*
\   AE-REL  := AE-ADD ( ("<"|">"|"<="|">=") AE-ADD )*
\   AE-ADD  := AE-MUL ( ("+"|"-") AE-MUL )*
\   AE-MUL  := AE-PRIMARY ( ("*"|"/"|"%") AE-PRIMARY )*
\   AE-PRIMARY := NUMBER | NAME | "(" AE-OR ")" | ("-"|"+"|"!") AE-PRIMARY
\ Scope limits, all deliberate: no bitwise operators (&, |, ^, <<, >>,
\ ~), no ternary (?:), no assignment forms (=, +=, etc.) or ++/--
\ within the expression, no octal/hex literals (decimal only). A
\ variable that's unset, or whose value doesn't start with an optional
\ '-' followed by digits, is treated as 0 - matching how POSIX
\ arithmetic treats a non-numeric operand as an error in principle,
\ but this shell's own established style favors a harmless fallback
\ over a hard error throughout (see LOOKUP-VAR/EMIT-DECIMAL and
\ friends elsewhere in this file). Operates on ARITH-BUF, a private
\ copy of the expression text (extracted by EXPAND-ARITH below,
\ tracking paren depth so a nested "(...)" inside the expression
\ itself isn't mistaken for the arithmetic expansion's own closing
\ "))"), with its own AE-POS/AE-END position tracking, entirely
\ separate from the main tokenizer's own TOK-POS/TOK-END - this
\ evaluator has no awareness of quoting, $VAR expansion, or anything
\ else about the outer command line at all, only of the extracted
\ expression text itself.
\ ==================================================================

256 BUFFER: ARITH-BUF
VARIABLE AE-POS
VARIABLE AE-END

: AE-SKIP-WS ( --- )
  BEGIN
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
  REPEAT ;

: AE-DIGIT? ( c --- f )
  DUP 48 < 0= SWAP 58 < AND ;

: AE-PARSE-NUMBER ( --- n )
  0
  BEGIN
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ AE-DIGIT? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    10 * AE-POS @ C@ 48 - +
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
  REPEAT ;

64 BUFFER: AE-VARNAME-BUF
VARIABLE AE-VN-LEN

: AE-READ-VARNAME ( --- )
  0 AE-VN-LEN !
  BEGIN
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ NAME-CHAR? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@ AE-VARNAME-BUF AE-VN-LEN @ + C!
    AE-VN-LEN @ 1+ AE-VN-LEN !
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
  REPEAT
  0 AE-VARNAME-BUF AE-VN-LEN @ + C! ;

: AE-LOOKUP-NUMERIC ( --- n )
  \ An unset variable, or one whose value isn't itself a (possibly
  \ "-"-prefixed) run of digits, is treated as 0.
  AE-VARNAME-BUF LOOKUP-VAR DUP 0= IF
    DROP 0
  ELSE
    DUP C@ 45 = IF
      1+ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL NEGATE
    ELSE
      DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL
    THEN
  THEN ;

: AE-BOOL-TO-INT ( f --- n )  \ Forth's own -1/0 boolean convention to
                                \ POSIX arithmetic's 1/0
  NEGATE ;
: AE-TRUTHY? ( n --- f )  0= 0= ;

: AE-MUL-OP? ( c --- f )
  DUP 42 = OVER 47 = OR SWAP 37 = OR ;

: AE-ADD-OP? ( c --- f )
  DUP 43 = SWAP 45 = OR ;

DEFER AE-OR-CALL   \ AE-PRIMARY (a parenthesized subexpression is a
                      \ full expression in its own right) and AE-OR
                      \ (the top of the grammar) are mutually
                      \ recursive across several intervening levels -
                      \ rather than defining all seven levels as one
                      \ giant, deeply-nested word, AE-PRIMARY reaches
                      \ AE-OR through this deferred call, patched
                      \ right after AE-OR itself is defined below.


: AE-PRIMARY ( --- n )
  AE-SKIP-WS
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ 40 = IF                    ( '(' )
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
    AE-OR-CALL
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ 41 = IF AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! THEN THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ 45 = IF                    ( unary '-' )
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
    RECURSE NEGATE
    EXIT
  THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ 43 = IF                    ( unary '+' )
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
    RECURSE
    EXIT
  THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ 33 = IF                    ( unary '!' logical not )
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
    RECURSE AE-TRUTHY? 0= AE-BOOL-TO-INT
    EXIT
  THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ AE-DIGIT? IF
    AE-PARSE-NUMBER
    EXIT
  THEN
  \ "$((  $a + 2 ))" - a '$' before a name inside an arithmetic
  \ expression. The text reaches here unexpanded, because
  \ NORMALIZE-OPERATORS copies a $((...)) region verbatim, so the
  \ sigil is simply skipped: POSIX allows a variable to be written
  \ with or without it here and both mean the same thing.
  AE-POS @ C@ 36 = IF AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! THEN
  AE-READ-VARNAME
  AE-LOOKUP-NUMERIC ;

: AE-MUL ( --- n )
  AE-PRIMARY
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ AE-MUL-OP? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@
    DUP 42 = IF
      DROP AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! AE-PRIMARY *
    ELSE DUP 47 = IF
      DROP AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! AE-PRIMARY /
    ELSE
      DROP AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! AE-PRIMARY MOD
    THEN THEN
  REPEAT ;

: AE-ADD ( --- n )
  AE-MUL
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ AE-ADD-OP? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@ 43 = IF
      AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! AE-MUL +
    ELSE
      AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! AE-MUL -
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

VARIABLE AE-SH-C

: AE-SHIFT-OP? ( --- f )   \ "<<" or ">>" at AE-POS?
  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ AE-SH-C !
  AE-SH-C @ 60 = AE-SH-C @ 62 = OR
  AE-POS @ 1+ C@ AE-SH-C @ = AND ;

: AE-SHIFT ( --- n )
  \ C/POSIX precedence: shifts bind tighter than the relational
  \ operators and looser than "+"/"-". AE-REL below now calls this
  \ instead of AE-ADD, and must not mistake "<<" for "<".
  AE-ADD
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-SHIFT-OP?
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@ 60 = IF
      AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !  AE-ADD LSHIFT
    ELSE
      AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !  AE-ADD RSHIFT
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

: AE-REL ( --- n )
  \ "<="/">=" checked by matching the leading '<'/'>' first, then
  \ peeking one further character for a following '=' - matching how
  \ AE-EQ itself distinguishes "==<"/"!=" from a bare '='/'!' (which
  \ don't appear in this grammar at all, so a bare one is simply not
  \ matched by anything here and left for the caller to report as an
  \ unmatched trailing character, the same sparse-error-handling style
  \ used throughout this shell).
  AE-SHIFT
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF
      AE-POS @ C@ DUP 60 = SWAP 62 = OR
      AE-SHIFT-OP? 0= AND
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@ 60 = IF                      ( starts with '<' )
      AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
      AE-POS @ AE-END @ < AE-POS @ C@ 61 = AND IF
        AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
        AE-SHIFT SWAP < 0= AE-BOOL-TO-INT     ( "<=" )
      ELSE
        AE-SHIFT < AE-BOOL-TO-INT             ( "<" )
      THEN
    ELSE                                       ( starts with '>' )
      AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
      AE-POS @ AE-END @ < AE-POS @ C@ 61 = AND IF
        AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
        AE-SHIFT < 0= AE-BOOL-TO-INT           ( ">=" )
      ELSE
        AE-SHIFT SWAP < AE-BOOL-TO-INT         ( ">" )
      THEN
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

: AE-EQ ( --- n )
  AE-REL
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF
      AE-POS @ C@ DUP 61 = SWAP 33 = OR
      AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 61 = ELSE 0 THEN AND
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@ 61 = IF
      AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
      AE-REL = AE-BOOL-TO-INT
    ELSE
      AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
      AE-REL = 0= AE-BOOL-TO-INT
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

VARIABLE AE-BIT-C

: AE-SINGLE-OP? ( c --- f )
  \ Is character c present at AE-POS and NOT doubled? Distinguishes
  \ the bitwise "&"/"|" from the logical "&&"/"||", which bind looser
  \ and are handled further out.
  AE-BIT-C !
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ AE-BIT-C @ = 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ 1+ C@ AE-BIT-C @ <> ELSE -1 THEN ;

: AE-BITAND ( --- n )
  AE-EQ
  BEGIN AE-SKIP-WS 38 AE-SINGLE-OP? WHILE
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !  AE-EQ AND
  REPEAT ;

: AE-BITXOR ( --- n )
  AE-BITAND
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ 94 = ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !  AE-BITAND XOR
  REPEAT ;

: AE-BITOR ( --- n )
  AE-BITXOR
  BEGIN AE-SKIP-WS 124 AE-SINGLE-OP? WHILE
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !  AE-BITXOR OR
  REPEAT ;

: AE-AND ( --- n )
  AE-BITOR
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < AND IF
      AE-POS @ C@ 38 = AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 38 = AND
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
    AE-BITOR
    SWAP AE-TRUTHY? SWAP AE-TRUTHY? AND AE-BOOL-TO-INT
  REPEAT ;

: AE-OR ( --- n )
  AE-AND
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < AND IF
      AE-POS @ C@ 124 = AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 124 = AND
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
    AE-AND
    SWAP AE-TRUTHY? SWAP AE-TRUTHY? OR AE-BOOL-TO-INT
  REPEAT ;

VARIABLE AE-EVAL-LEN

24 BUFFER: AE-NUMBUF
VARIABLE ANS-I
VARIABLE ANS-N
64 BUFFER: AE-ASGN-NAME
VARIABLE AE-ASGN-OP
VARIABLE AE-SAVE-POS
VARIABLE AE-ASGN-C
VARIABLE AE-SLOT

VARIABLE ANS-NEG

: N>STR ( n --- c-addr )
  \ Decimal text for n, NUL-terminated, built from the END of
  \ AE-NUMBUF backwards so no reversal pass is needed. Returns a
  \ pointer to the first digit, which is somewhere inside the buffer.
  \ Needed because a variable's value is a string: an arithmetic
  \ assignment has to write one back.
  SHADOW{ ANS-N | ANS-I ANS-NEG }
  0 AE-NUMBUF 23 + C!
  22 ANS-I !
  ANS-N @ 0< IF -1 ANS-NEG ! ANS-N @ NEGATE ANS-N ! THEN
  ANS-N @ 0= IF
    48 AE-NUMBUF ANS-I @ + C!
    ANS-I @ 1- ANS-I !
  ELSE
    BEGIN ANS-N @ 0 > WHILE
      ANS-N @ 10 MOD 48 + AE-NUMBUF ANS-I @ + C!
      ANS-N @ 10 / ANS-N !
      ANS-I @ 1- ANS-I !
    REPEAT
  THEN
  ANS-NEG @ IF
    45 AE-NUMBUF ANS-I @ + C!
    ANS-I @ 1- ANS-I !
  THEN
  AE-NUMBUF ANS-I @ 1+ + ;

: AE-ASGN-OP? ( --- f )
  \ At AE-POS: "=" (not "=="), or one of "+= -= *= /= %=".
  \ Consumes the operator and records it in AE-ASGN-OP (0 for plain
  \ "="), or leaves AE-POS untouched and answers false.
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ AE-ASGN-C !
  AE-ASGN-C @ 61 = IF
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 61 = IF 0 EXIT THEN THEN
    0 AE-ASGN-OP !  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !  -1 EXIT
  THEN
  AE-ASGN-C @ AE-MUL-OP? AE-ASGN-C @ AE-ADD-OP? OR 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 61 = 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-ASGN-C @ AE-ASGN-OP !  AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !  -1 ;

: AE-TRY-ASSIGN? ( --- f )
  \ Lookahead for "NAME op= ...". Restores AE-POS on any mismatch, so
  \ an ordinary expression starting with a name is unaffected.
  AE-POS @ AE-SAVE-POS !
  AE-SKIP-WS
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < 0= IF AE-SAVE-POS @ AE-POS ! 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ NAME-CHAR? 0= IF AE-SAVE-POS @ AE-POS ! 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ AE-DIGIT?   IF AE-SAVE-POS @ AE-POS ! 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-READ-VARNAME
  AE-VARNAME-BUF AE-ASGN-NAME 64 SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  AE-SKIP-WS
  AE-ASGN-OP? 0= IF AE-SAVE-POS @ AE-POS ! 0 EXIT THEN
  -1 ;

: AE-ASGN-OLD ( --- n )   \ current numeric value of the target
  AE-ASGN-NAME AE-VARNAME-BUF 64 SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  AE-LOOKUP-NUMERIC ;

: AE-EXPR ( --- n )
  \ Lowest precedence level: assignment, right-associative. Anything
  \ that is not an assignment falls straight through to AE-OR, so this
  \ costs one lookahead per expression.
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @
  SHADOW{ BODY-ARENA-TOP | AE-SLOT }
  AE-TRY-ASSIGN? 0= IF AE-OR EXIT THEN
  \ The target name and the operator both have to survive the
  \ recursive evaluation of the right-hand side, which may itself be
  \ an assignment ("a=b=1") and overwrite AE-ASGN-NAME/AE-ASGN-OP.
  \ The operator rides on the data stack; the name gets a slot in the
  \ body arena, released by the local holding the bump pointer.
  64 BODY-ALLOC AE-SLOT !
  AE-ASGN-NAME AE-SLOT @ BODY@ 64 SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  AE-ASGN-OP @
  RECURSE                                   ( op rhs )
  AE-SLOT @ BODY@ AE-ASGN-NAME 64 SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  SWAP ?DUP IF
    DUP 43 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD + ELSE
    DUP 45 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD SWAP - ELSE
    DUP 42 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD * ELSE
    DUP 47 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD SWAP / ELSE
                DROP AE-ASGN-OLD SWAP MOD
    THEN THEN THEN THEN
  THEN
  DUP N>STR
  AE-ASGN-NAME SWAP SET-SHVAR ;

\ Patched here, not next to AE-OR: the deferred call must resolve to
\ AE-EXPR, which is defined above only now.
' AE-EXPR IS AE-OR-CALL   \ a parenthesized subexpression may assign

: AE-EVAL ( c-addr u --- n )
  \ Copies (c-addr u) into ARITH-BUF (a private copy, so the
  \ evaluator's own AE-POS/AE-END never alias the main tokenizer's
  \ LINE-BUF/TOK-POS/TOK-END) and evaluates it as a full expression.
  DUP AE-EVAL-LEN !
  ARITH-BUF SWAP MOVE
  ARITH-BUF AE-POS !
  ARITH-BUF AE-EVAL-LEN @ + AE-END !
  AE-EXPR ;

256 BUFFER: ARITH-EXPR-BUF
VARIABLE ARITH-EXPR-LEN
VARIABLE ARITH-DEPTH

: EXPAND-ARITH ( --- )
  \ TOK-POS is right after "$((" (both opening parens already
  \ consumed by the caller). Scans for the matching "))" - tracking
  \ how many *extra*, unmatched "(" are open within the expression
  \ itself (ARITH-DEPTH), so a nested "(...)" inside the expression
  \ (e.g. "$(( (1+2)*3 ))") isn't mistaken for the arithmetic
  \ expansion's own closing "))" - before evaluating the extracted
  \ text (via AE-EVAL) and emitting the result as a decimal number.
  \ Bounds-checked against ARITH-EXPR-BUF's own fixed size the same
  \ defensive way this file already handles other fixed-size copies -
  \ an expression longer than that is silently truncated rather than
  \ overflowing, not reported as an error.
  0 ARITH-DEPTH !
  0 ARITH-EXPR-LEN !
  BEGIN
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF
      TOK-POS @ C@ 41 = ARITH-DEPTH @ 0 = AND
      TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-END @ < AND
      TOK-POS @ 1+ C@ 41 = AND
      0=
    ELSE
      0
    THEN
  WHILE
    TOK-POS @ C@ 40 = IF ARITH-DEPTH @ 1+ ARITH-DEPTH ! THEN
    TOK-POS @ C@ 41 = IF
      ARITH-DEPTH @ 0 > IF ARITH-DEPTH @ 1- ARITH-DEPTH ! THEN
    THEN
    ARITH-EXPR-LEN @ 255 < IF
      TOK-POS @ C@ ARITH-EXPR-BUF ARITH-EXPR-LEN @ + C!
      ARITH-EXPR-LEN @ 1+ ARITH-EXPR-LEN !
    THEN
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  REPEAT
  TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ 2 + TOK-POS ! THEN   \ skip the closing double-paren
  ARITH-EXPR-BUF ARITH-EXPR-LEN @ AE-EVAL EMIT-DECIMAL ;

: EXPAND-CMDSUB ( closer --- )
  \ TOK-POS is just inside the substitution; `closer` is the character
  \ that ends it - ')' for $(...), '`' for the backquote form, which
  \ is otherwise identical. Parameterising the terminator was all the
  \ backquote form needed; a second copy of this word would have been
  \ exactly the duplication FORTH-STYLE.md warns about.
  CS-CLOSER !
  0 CS-COPY-I !
  0 CS-SQ? !  0 CS-DQ? !  0 CS-DEPTH !
  BEGIN
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF
      TOK-POS @ C@ CS-CLOSER @ <> CS-SQ? @ OR CS-DQ? @ OR CS-DEPTH @ 0 <> OR
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    \ A ')' inside quotes belongs to the command text, not to the
    \ substitution: $(echo "a)b") must not stop at the first ')'.
    TOK-POS @ C@ 39 = CS-DQ? @ 0= AND IF CS-SQ? @ 0= CS-SQ? ! THEN
    TOK-POS @ C@ 34 = CS-SQ? @ 0= AND IF CS-DQ? @ 0= CS-DQ? ! THEN
    \ A nested "$(" opens another level, so its own ')' does not close
    \ this one - "$(echo $(echo x))". Only for the paren form; the
    \ backquote form has no nesting to track.
    CS-CLOSER @ 41 = CS-SQ? @ 0= AND CS-DQ? @ 0= AND IF
      TOK-POS @ C@ 36 = TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-END @ < AND IF
        TOK-POS @ 1+ C@ 40 = IF CS-DEPTH @ 1+ CS-DEPTH ! THEN
      THEN
      TOK-POS @ C@ 41 = CS-DEPTH @ 0 > AND IF
        CS-DEPTH @ 1- CS-DEPTH !
      THEN
    THEN
    CS-COPY-I @ 2 + ENSURE-BUFFER CMDSUB-LINE-BUF
    CS-COPY-I @ LINE-HARD-MAX < IF
      TOK-POS @ C@ CMDSUB-LINE-BUF CS-COPY-I @ + C!
      CS-COPY-I @ 1+ CS-COPY-I !
    THEN
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  REPEAT
  TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS ! THEN   ( skip closing paren )
  EXPAND-SUPPRESSED? @ IF EXIT THEN
  \ The text has been consumed; the command must not run. This is the
  \ branch of ${VAR:-$(cmd)} that is not taken - the word is still
  \ scanned, to find where it ends, but a shell that ran the command
  \ anyway would be visibly wrong (Iteration 104).
  CS-COPY-I @ CMDSUB-SPLIT-LINES
  CS-NONBLANK? @ IF
    PIPE DROP CMDSUB-PIPE-WR ! CMDSUB-PIPE-RD !
    FORK DUP CMDSUB-PID !
    0 = IF
      CMDSUB-PIPE-WR @ 1 DUP2 DROP
      CMDSUB-PIPE-WR @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
      CMDSUB-PIPE-RD @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
      RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD-CALL
      127 SYS-EXIT
    THEN
    CMDSUB-PIPE-WR @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
    CMDSUB-PIPE-RD @ READ-ALL-CMDSUB-OUTPUT
    CMDSUB-PIPE-RD @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
    CMDSUB-PID @ WAITPID DROP DROP
    BEGIN                                    ( strip trailing newlines )
      CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 0 > IF
        CMDSUB-OUT-BUF CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 1- + C@ 10 =
      ELSE 0 THEN
    WHILE
      CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 1- CMDSUB-OUT-LEN !
    REPEAT
    \ CMDSUB-OUT-LEN can genuinely be 0 here (the substituted command
    \ produced no output at all, e.g. $(true) or $(printf)) - guarded
    \ explicitly rather than relying on "0 0 DO ... LOOP" to mean
    \ zero iterations. It doesn't in this kernel: DO/LOOP here treats
    \ start=limit as wrapping through the *entire* unsigned range
    \ rather than skipping the loop, so an unguarded zero-length splice
    \ here read (and wrote, via EMIT-TOK-CHAR) billions of bytes past
    \ CMDSUB-OUT-BUF until it hit unmapped memory and segfaulted -
    \ found via mrsh's async.sh (see PROGRESS.md's crash-hardening
    \ entry) and reproduced down to a single command: `echo $(true)`.
    CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 0 > IF
      \ EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR, not EMIT-TOK-CHAR: an *unquoted* command
      \ substitution is subject to IFS field splitting exactly as an
      \ unquoted $VAR is, so "for c in $(echo a s d f)" runs four
      \ times. EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR checks IN-DQ-CONTEXT? itself, so
      \ "$(...)" inside double quotes still yields one word.
      CMDSUB-OUT-BUF CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 0 DO
        DUP I + C@ EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR
      LOOP DROP
    THEN
  THEN ;

VARIABLE EAP-I
VARIABLE EAP-AT
VARIABLE EMPTY-AT-WORD?   \ this word was an unsatisfied "$@"

: EMIT-ALL-POS-PARAMS ( at? --- )
  \ Emits every positional parameter. at? distinguishes $@ from $*,
  \ which differ only INSIDE double quotes: "$@" gives one field per
  \ parameter, "$*" one field with them joined. Unquoted they behave
  \ alike, because the joining space is then an ordinary IFS character
  \ and normal splitting does the rest.
  \
  \ "$@" is the one expansion POSIX allows to produce several fields
  \ from a quoted word, so it cannot go through EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR -
  \ which suppresses splitting inside quotes, correctly, for
  \ everything else - and calls IFS-SPLIT-HERE directly.
  SHADOW{ EAP-AT | EAP-I }
  \ POSIX: "$@" with no positional parameters produces NO field, where
  \ any other quoted empty word produces one. Recorded here and acted
  \ on when the token ends, since whether the word is empty overall is
  \ not known yet - "x$@y" is still a single field "xy".
  EAP-AT @ POS-PARAM-COUNT @ 0= AND
  TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ = AND IF
    -1 EMPTY-AT-WORD? !
  THEN
  BEGIN EAP-I @ POS-PARAM-COUNT @ < WHILE
    \ The separator needs room reserved just as the parameters do.
    \ Without this each one overwrote a byte of unread input, so
    \ "echo \"[$*]\"" with two parameters lost its closing bracket -
    \ the same in-place-growth hazard as the numeric expansions in
    \ Iteration 46, in the one place that emits a character of its own
    \ rather than copying a value.
    EAP-I @ 0 > IF
      EAP-AT @ IN-DQ-CONTEXT? @ AND IF
        \ The field break needs room reserved too. IFS-SPLIT-HERE
        \ writes a NUL and advances TOK-OUT, so it grows the output by
        \ one byte like the separator below does. It is balanced when
        \ EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR calls it - the IFS character that
        \ triggered the split was consumed from the input and not
        \ emitted - but here nothing was consumed: "$@" synthesises the
        \ break out of nothing. Without this, "1  $@  2" with three
        \ parameters lost its "  2" (Iteration 103).
        IFS-SPLIT-HERE
      ELSE
        32 EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR
      THEN
    THEN
    EAP-I @ POS-PARAM-SLOT TYPE0-TO-TOK-SPLIT
    EAP-I @ 1+ EAP-I !
  REPEAT ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Parameter-expansion modifiers: ${#VAR} (length), ${VAR:-word}/
\ ${VAR-word} (default value), ${VAR:=word}/${VAR=word} (assign
\ default), ${VAR:+word}/${VAR+word} (alternate value). The ':'
\ variants trigger on VAR being either unset OR empty; the plain
\ variants trigger on unset only. Scope limits, both documented here
\ rather than silently: the "word" portion is treated as a literal
\ string - no further $-expansion is applied to it, unlike real POSIX
\ - and no nested braces are tracked while reading it, so an inner
\ "}" ends it early. ${VAR%word}/${VAR%%word}/${VAR#word}/
\ ${VAR##word} (prefix/suffix removal) are a separate, not yet
\ implemented piece - genuinely new pattern-matching logic, not just
\ string comparison, unlike the modifiers here.
\ ==================================================================

256 BUFFER: PEWORD-BUF
VARIABLE PEWORD-LEN
VARIABLE PEW-COLON?
VARIABLE PEW-SUFFIX?     \ set by EXPAND-BRACED-VAR before calling
                          \ TRIM-PARAM-CALL: true for %/%% (suffix
                          \ removal), false for #/## (prefix removal).
VARIABLE PEW-LONGEST?    \ true for %%/## (longest match), false for
                          \ %/# (shortest match).
DEFER TRIM-PARAM-CALL   \ patched to TRIM-PARAM's own xt right after
                          \ it's defined, much further down (the same
                          \ deferred-word pattern already used for
                          \ RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL/RUN-FUNC-BODY-CALL/
                          \ TRY-ASSIGNMENT-CALL) - needed since
                          \ TRIM-PARAM depends on GLOB-MATCH, defined
                          \ far later in the file than EXPAND-BRACED-
                          \ VAR is.


DEFER SCAN-CHAR-CALL
\ SCAN-TOKEN's one-character dispatch - quotes, backslash, '$', a
\ backquote, an assignment tilde. Patched to the real word far below,
\ where COPY-DOUBLE-QUOTED and friends exist; needed here because the
\ "word" in ${VAR:-word} is ordinary word text and gets expanded by
\ exactly the same machinery as any other, rather than by a second
\ copy of it.

VARIABLE CBW-START
VARIABLE EBW-NAME       \ arena offset of the saved name
VARIABLE EBW-NAMELEN

: EXPAND-BRACED-WORD ( --- )
  \ TOK-POS is at the first character of the word in ${VAR:-word}.
  \ Expands it in place into the token being built, and consumes the
  \ closing '}'.
  \
  \ A '}' at this level ends the word; one inside quotes, or inside a
  \ nested ${...} or $(...), does not, because the dispatch consumes
  \ those regions whole. Nesting therefore works without being
  \ arranged for.
  \
  \ VARNAME-BUF is saved across the scan and put back afterwards. This
  \ word introduced reentrancy where there was none - the old
  \ READ-PEWORD only copied bytes - and an inner $NAME writes its own
  \ name into that same buffer, which both TRIM-PARAM and
  \ ${VAR:=word}'s SET-SHVAR read after the scan returns.
  \ "${x#$HOME}" trimmed HOME from HOME and quietly gave empty.
  \ FORTH-STYLE.md section 9, found by testing rather than by reading.
  \
  \ Per-invocation arena storage rather than a second fixed buffer,
  \ since the nesting has no bound: BODY-ARENA-TOP is a local, so the
  \ allocation is released on every exit path.
  IN-BRACED-WORD? @ BODY-ARENA-TOP @
  SHADOW{ IN-BRACED-WORD? BODY-ARENA-TOP | EBW-NAME EBW-NAMELEN }
  -1 IN-BRACED-WORD? !
  VN-LEN @ 1+ EBW-NAMELEN !
  EBW-NAMELEN @ BODY-ALLOC EBW-NAME !
  VARNAME-BUF EBW-NAME @ BODY@ EBW-NAMELEN @ MOVE
  BEGIN
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ C@ 125 <> ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    SCAN-CHAR-CALL
  REPEAT
  TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS ! THEN
  EBW-NAME @ BODY@ VARNAME-BUF EBW-NAMELEN @ MOVE
  EBW-NAMELEN @ 1- VN-LEN ! ;

: EXPAND-BRACED-WORD-ASIDE ( --- )
  \ Consumes the word without letting any of it be seen: the branch of
  \ ${VAR:-word} that is not taken.
  \
  \ Deliberately the same scan as the live path rather than a separate
  \ skipper. Two scanners would be two answers to "where does this word
  \ end", and FORTH-STYLE.md section 11 is about exactly that: copies
  \ drift, and here they would drift on quoted braces.
  \
  \ Three things make the scan invisible. TOK-OUT is declared as a
  \ local, so it is restored on exit and whatever was written is
  \ rewound. IN-DQ-CONTEXT? suppresses field splitting, which would
  \ otherwise advance ARGC for a "$@" in the discarded word.
  \ EXPAND-SUPPRESSED? stops a $(...) from running.
  IN-DQ-CONTEXT? @ EXPAND-SUPPRESSED? @ TOK-OUT @
  SHADOW{ IN-DQ-CONTEXT? EXPAND-SUPPRESSED? TOK-OUT }
  -1 IN-DQ-CONTEXT? !
  -1 EXPAND-SUPPRESSED? !
  EXPAND-BRACED-WORD ;

: CAPTURE-BRACED-WORD ( --- )
  \ Expands the word and leaves the result in PEWORD-BUF instead of in
  \ the token: what ${VAR%pattern} needs, since the pattern is matched
  \ against rather than emitted, and what ${VAR:=word} needs for the
  \ value it assigns.
  \
  \ The token buffer is the scratch space - expand into it and rewind.
  \
  \ Field splitting is suppressed: a pattern is one pattern, and an
  \ assigned value is one value.
  IN-DQ-CONTEXT? @ TOK-OUT @ SHADOW{ IN-DQ-CONTEXT? TOK-OUT }
  TOK-OUT @ CBW-START !
  -1 IN-DQ-CONTEXT? !
  EXPAND-BRACED-WORD
  TOK-OUT @ CBW-START @ - DUP 255 > IF DROP 255 THEN
  PEWORD-LEN !
  CBW-START @ PEWORD-BUF PEWORD-LEN @ MOVE
  0 PEWORD-BUF PEWORD-LEN @ + C! ;

: USE-BRACED-WORD? ( value-or-0 --- f )
  \ Does this modifier's word apply? True when the variable is unset,
  \ or empty and the modifier was written with a ':'. The three
  \ branches below all asked this in the same two nested IFs; asking it
  \ once is what lets each branch be a plain choice between two words.
  DUP 0= IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  CSTRLEN 0= PEW-COLON? @ AND ;

: READ-VARNAME ( --- )
  \ Reads a POSIX portable variable name from TOK-POS into
  \ VARNAME-BUF, *without* consuming whatever follows (unlike the
  \ plain, unmodified ${NAME} path, which consumes through the closing
  \ '}' itself) - stops right at the first non-name character, so the
  \ caller can inspect what comes next to detect a modifier.
  0 VN-LEN !
  BEGIN
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ C@ NAME-CHAR? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    TOK-POS @ C@ VARNAME-BUF VN-LEN @ + C!
    VN-LEN @ 1+ VN-LEN !
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  REPEAT
  0 VARNAME-BUF VN-LEN @ + C! ;

: EMIT-LOOKED-UP-VAR ( value-addr-or-0 --- )
  \ Shared with the plain ${NAME}/$NAME paths below: emits a looked-up
  \ value if found, otherwise emits nothing (an unset variable
  \ expands to empty).
  DUP 0= IF DROP ELSE
    TYPE0-TO-TOK-SPLIT
  THEN ;

: EXPAND-BRACED-VAR ( --- )
  \ TOK-POS is right after the opening '{'.
  TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < TOK-POS @ C@ 35 = AND IF   ( ${#VAR}: length )
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    READ-VARNAME
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS ! THEN
    VARNAME-BUF LOOKUP-VAR DUP 0= IF DROP 0 ELSE CSTRLEN THEN
    EMIT-DECIMAL
    EXIT
  THEN
  READ-VARNAME
  TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < 0= IF        ( ran off the end - malformed;
                                        fall back to a plain lookup )
    VARNAME-BUF LOOKUP-VAR EMIT-LOOKED-UP-VAR
    EXIT
  THEN
  TOK-POS @ C@ 125 = IF               ( ${NAME}: plain, unmodified )
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    VARNAME-BUF LOOKUP-VAR EMIT-LOOKED-UP-VAR
    EXIT
  THEN
  -1 PEW-COLON? !
  TOK-POS @ C@ 58 = IF                ( ':' introducer, optional )
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  ELSE
    0 PEW-COLON? !
  THEN
  TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < 0= IF EXIT THEN   ( malformed - bail quietly )
  TOK-POS @ C@
  DUP 45 = IF                         ( '-': default value )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    VARNAME-BUF LOOKUP-VAR
    DUP USE-BRACED-WORD? IF
      DROP EXPAND-BRACED-WORD
    ELSE
      EXPAND-BRACED-WORD-ASIDE
      EMIT-LOOKED-UP-VAR
    THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 61 = IF                         ( '=': assign default )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    VARNAME-BUF LOOKUP-VAR
    DUP USE-BRACED-WORD? IF
      DROP
      CAPTURE-BRACED-WORD
      VARNAME-BUF PEWORD-BUF SET-SHVAR
      PEWORD-BUF TYPE0-TO-TOK-SPLIT
    ELSE
      EXPAND-BRACED-WORD-ASIDE
      EMIT-LOOKED-UP-VAR
    THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 43 = IF                         ( '+': alternate value )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    VARNAME-BUF LOOKUP-VAR
    USE-BRACED-WORD? IF               ( unset/empty: no alternate )
      EXPAND-BRACED-WORD-ASIDE
    ELSE
      EXPAND-BRACED-WORD
    THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 37 = IF                         ( '%' or '%%': suffix removal )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    -1 PEW-SUFFIX? !
    0 PEW-LONGEST? !
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < TOK-POS @ C@ 37 = AND IF
      TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
      -1 PEW-LONGEST? !
    THEN
    CAPTURE-BRACED-WORD
    TRIM-PARAM-CALL
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 35 = IF                         ( '#' or '##': prefix removal )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    0 PEW-SUFFIX? !
    0 PEW-LONGEST? !
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < TOK-POS @ C@ 35 = AND IF
      TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
      -1 PEW-LONGEST? !
    THEN
    CAPTURE-BRACED-WORD
    TRIM-PARAM-CALL
    EXIT
  THEN
  DROP ;                              ( unrecognized modifier: bail )

: EXPAND-VAR ( --- )
  \ TOK-POS is at the '$' character.
  -1 TOK-WAS-QUOTED? !
  TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < 0= IF 36 EMIT-TOK-CHAR EXIT THEN
  TOK-POS @ C@
  DUP 63 = IF                     ( $? )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    LAST-STATUS @ EMIT-DECIMAL
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 33 = IF                     ( $! - most recent background pid )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    BG-PID @ EMIT-DECIMAL
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 36 = IF                     ( $$ )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    GETPID EMIT-DECIMAL
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 48 - DUP 0 > SWAP 10 < AND IF  ( $1-$9: positional parameters )
    48 -
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    1-
    DUP POS-PARAM-COUNT @ < IF
      POS-PARAM-SLOT TYPE0-TO-TOK-SPLIT
    ELSE
      DROP
    THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 35 = IF                     ( $# )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    POS-PARAM-COUNT @ EMIT-DECIMAL
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 64 = IF                     ( $@ )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    -1 EMIT-ALL-POS-PARAMS
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 42 = IF                     ( $* )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    0 EMIT-ALL-POS-PARAMS
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 40 = IF                     \ command substitution, or
                                   \ arithmetic expansion if doubled
    DROP
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-END @ < TOK-POS @ 1+ C@ 40 = AND IF
      TOK-POS @ 2 + TOK-POS !
      EXPAND-ARITH
    ELSE
      TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !          ( skip '(' )
      41 EXPAND-CMDSUB
    THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 123 = IF                    ( ${...} )
    DROP TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    EXPAND-BRACED-VAR
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP NAME-CHAR? 0= IF
    DROP 36 EMIT-TOK-CHAR          ( $ not followed by a name: literal $ )
    EXIT
  THEN
  DROP
  0 VN-LEN !
  BEGIN
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ C@ NAME-CHAR? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    TOK-POS @ C@ VARNAME-BUF VN-LEN @ + C!
    VN-LEN @ 1+ VN-LEN !
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  REPEAT
  0 VARNAME-BUF VN-LEN @ + C!
  VARNAME-BUF LOOKUP-VAR DUP 0= IF DROP ELSE
    TYPE0-TO-TOK-SPLIT
  THEN ;

: COPY-SINGLE-QUOTED ( --- )
  \ TOK-POS is at the opening '. Copies everything up to (not
  \ including) the matching closing ' verbatim - no escapes at all
  \ recognized inside single quotes, matching POSIX. An unterminated
  \ quote just consumes to the end of the input rather than erroring
  \ (a real shell would prompt for a continuation line - not done
  \ here yet).
  -1 TOK-WAS-QUOTED? !
  TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  BEGIN
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ C@ 39 <> ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    TOK-POS @ C@ EMIT-TOK-CHAR
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  REPEAT
  TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS ! THEN ;

: COPY-DOUBLE-QUOTED ( --- )
  \ Like COPY-SINGLE-QUOTED, but recognizes \" and \\ as escapes for a
  \ literal " or \. Any other backslash (e.g. \n) is passed through
  \ as-is (backslash and the following character both kept), matching
  \ POSIX's own rule that backslash is only special before specific
  \ characters inside double quotes.
  -1 TOK-WAS-QUOTED? !
  -1 IN-DQ-CONTEXT? !
  TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  BEGIN
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ C@ 34 <> ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    TOK-POS @ C@ 92 = IF
      TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-END @ < IF
        TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
        TOK-POS @ C@
        \ POSIX: inside double quotes a backslash is special only
        \ before $ ` " \ (and newline). '$' and '`' were missing, so
        \ "\$a" kept its backslash instead of producing a literal $.
        DUP 34 = OVER 92 = OR OVER 36 = OR OVER 96 = OR IF
          EMIT-TOK-CHAR
        ELSE
          92 EMIT-TOK-CHAR EMIT-TOK-CHAR
        THEN
        TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
      ELSE
        92 EMIT-TOK-CHAR
        TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
      THEN
    ELSE TOK-POS @ C@ 36 = IF
      EXPAND-VAR
    ELSE TOK-POS @ C@ 96 = IF
      TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
      96 EXPAND-CMDSUB
    ELSE
      TOK-POS @ C@ EMIT-TOK-CHAR
      TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    THEN THEN THEN
  REPEAT
  0 IN-DQ-CONTEXT? !
  TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS ! THEN ;

: COPY-ESCAPED-CHAR ( --- )
  \ TOK-POS is at a backslash outside any quotes: makes the next
  \ character literal (e.g. \| for a literal pipe character).
  -1 TOK-WAS-QUOTED? !
  TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF
    TOK-POS @ C@ EMIT-TOK-CHAR
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  THEN ;

256 BUFFER: PW-LINE
64  BUFFER: PW-HOME
64  BUFFER: PW-WANT
VARIABLE PW-FID
VARIABLE PW-N
VARIABLE PW-I
VARIABLE PW-F
VARIABLE PW-S
VARIABLE PW-LEN
VARIABLE PW-ULEN   \ username length; PW-I is clobbered by PW-FIELD

: PW-FIELD ( n --- addr len )   \ field n of PW-LINE, ':'-separated
  PW-F !
  0 PW-I !  0 PW-S !
  BEGIN
    PW-S @ PW-F @ < PW-I @ PW-N @ < AND
  WHILE
    PW-LINE PW-I @ + C@ 58 = IF PW-S @ 1+ PW-S ! THEN
    PW-I @ 1+ PW-I !
  REPEAT
  PW-LINE PW-I @ +
  PW-I @
  BEGIN
    PW-I @ PW-N @ < IF PW-LINE PW-I @ + C@ 58 <> ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE PW-I @ 1+ PW-I ! REPEAT
  PW-I @ SWAP - ;

: PASSWD-HOME ( name-addr name-len --- addr | 0 )
  \ A named user's home directory. Returns 0 for an unknown user,
  \ which leaves "~nosuchuser" literal - what POSIX requires.
  \
  \ Through GETPWHOME, which is getpwnam(3), and therefore through
  \ NSS. This used to read /etc/passwd directly, which is one NSS
  \ source among several: on a host using LDAP, SSSD, NIS or
  \ systemd-homed a real user need not appear in that file at all,
  \ and "~alice" stayed literal. The failure was quiet and
  \ environment-dependent - it could never show up on a developer
  \ machine and would show up on exactly the hosts where centrally
  \ managed accounts are the point, which is why it was recorded in
  \ GOALS.md rather than trusted to be remembered (Iteration 120).
  \
  \ The name is copied into a NUL-terminated buffer first: it arrives
  \ as an address and a length pointing into the line being scanned,
  \ and getpwnam wants a C string. The result points into getpwnam's
  \ own static storage, valid until the next call, so it is copied
  \ out here rather than returned raw.
  PW-LEN !
  PW-WANT PW-LEN @ MOVE
  0 PW-WANT PW-LEN @ + C!
  PW-WANT GETPWHOME
  DUP 0= IF EXIT THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN PW-HOME 64 SAFE-COPY-NUL DROP
  DROP PW-HOME ;

: TRY-TILDE-USER ( --- )
  \ "~user" / "~user/path". Leaves the word untouched if the name is
  \ not in the password file.
  0 PW-I !
  BEGIN
    TOK-POS @ 1+ PW-I @ + TOK-END @ < IF
      TOK-POS @ 1+ PW-I @ + C@ DUP 47 <> SWAP WS? 0= AND
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE PW-I @ 1+ PW-I ! REPEAT
  PW-I @ 0= IF EXIT THEN
  \ Keep the length somewhere PASSWD-HOME cannot reach: it calls
  \ PW-FIELD, which uses PW-I as its own cursor. Reusing one scratch
  \ variable across a call that reaches it is the hazard
  \ FORTH-STYLE.md section 9 is about, and it cost "~root/x" its
  \ "/x".
  PW-I @ PW-ULEN !
  TOK-POS @ 1+ PW-ULEN @ PASSWD-HOME
  DUP 0= IF DROP EXIT THEN
  TOK-POS @ 1+ PW-ULEN @ + TOK-POS !
  TYPE0-TO-TOK-SPLIT ;

: TRY-TILDE-EXPAND ( --- )
  \ Called once, right at the start of SCAN-TOKEN, before any other
  \ character of the token is processed - POSIX tilde expansion only
  \ applies to a bare "~" right at the very start of a word, never one
  \ appearing anywhere else within it. A no-op unless TOK-POS is
  \ sitting on an unquoted "~" that's either the whole token (followed
  \ by whitespace or end of input) or immediately followed by "/".
  \ Scope limit: only this bare "~" form (expanding to $HOME) is
  \ supported - "~user" (another user's home directory, needing a
  \ password-database lookup this shell has no access to) and "~+"/
  \ "~-" ($PWD/$OLDPWD, and this shell doesn't track $OLDPWD at all)
  \ are not implemented; a "~" in either of those shapes is left
  \ untouched, same as any other literal character.
  TOK-POS @ C@ 126 <> IF EXIT THEN
  TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-END @ < IF
    TOK-POS @ 1+ C@ DUP 47 = SWAP WS? OR 0= IF TRY-TILDE-USER EXIT THEN
  THEN
  TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  S" HOME" ENVNAMBUF SWAP MOVE 0 ENVNAMBUF 4 + C!
  ENVNAMBUF LOOKUP-VAR DUP 0= IF DROP ELSE
    TYPE0-TO-TOK-SPLIT
  THEN ;

: ASSIGN-TILDE-POINT? ( --- f )
  \ Are we at a point in an ASSIGNMENT word where a tilde expands -
  \ i.e. directly after the '=' or after a ':'?
  TOKEN-IS-ASSIGN-PREFIX? 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ > 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  TOK-OUT @ 1- C@ DUP 61 = SWAP 58 = OR ;

: SCAN-TOKEN-CHAR ( --- )
  \ Dispatches the one character at TOK-POS, consuming it (or the whole
  \ quoted/expanded region it opens) and emitting its content at
  \ TOK-OUT.
  \
  \ Factored out of SCAN-TOKEN so EXPAND-BRACED-WORD can use it: the
  \ word in ${VAR:-word} is ordinary word text, and the alternative was
  \ a second copy of this dispatch that would have had to be kept in
  \ step with this one.
  TOK-POS @ C@
  DUP 96 = IF
    DROP
    -1 TOK-WAS-QUOTED? !
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    96 EXPAND-CMDSUB
  ELSE DUP 39 = IF DROP COPY-SINGLE-QUOTED
  ELSE DUP 34 = IF DROP COPY-DOUBLE-QUOTED
  ELSE DUP 92 = IF DROP COPY-ESCAPED-CHAR
  ELSE DUP 36 = IF DROP EXPAND-VAR
  ELSE DUP 126 = ASSIGN-TILDE-POINT? AND IF
    \ POSIX expands a tilde in an ASSIGNMENT after the '=' and after
    \ each unquoted ':', so "a=~/stuff" and "PATH=~/bin:~/sbin" both
    \ work. Elsewhere in a word '~' is literal, which is why this is
    \ not simply "is the character a tilde" - and why it is here and
    \ not in COPY-DOUBLE-QUOTED, where a tilde stays literal too.
    DROP TRY-TILDE-EXPAND
  ELSE
    IN-BRACED-WORD? @ IF EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR ELSE EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN
    TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
  THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN ;

' SCAN-TOKEN-CHAR IS SCAN-CHAR-CALL

: SCAN-TOKEN ( --- )
  \ Reads from TOK-POS in LINE-BUF, writing the *unquoted, expanded*
  \ content via EMIT-TOK-CHAR into TOK-BUF, until unquoted whitespace
  \ or the end of input. The two buffers are separate, so the output
  \ can be longer than the input it came from.
  TRY-TILDE-EXPAND
  BEGIN
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ C@ WS? 0= ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    SCAN-TOKEN-CHAR
  REPEAT ;

MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: EW-ARGV
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: EW-ARGQ
VARIABLE EW-ARGC
VARIABLE EW-I

: TOKENIZE-RAW ( --- )
  \ Copies each word recorded by NORMALIZE-OPERATORS into TOK-BUF,
  \ verbatim and NUL-terminated: quotes, '$', backquotes and all.
  \
  \ Finding the words is no longer this word's job, so not expanding
  \ them costs nothing - there is no scanner here to put into a
  \ "raw mode". That is what Iteration 112 bought.
  \ The raw words are at most the normalized text plus a NUL each; the
  \ expanded ones get the same room. Both grow here, before any word
  \ is written, so nothing grows while ARGV points into them.
  BUFFER-SIZE NORM-BUF 2* DUP ENSURE-BUFFER TOK-BUF ENSURE-BUFFER EXP-BUF
  TOK-BUF TOK-OUT !
  TOK-BUF BUFFER-SIZE TOK-BUF + TOK-OUT-END !
  0 ARGC !
  SHADOW{ | TK-I TK-J TK-Q }
  BEGIN TK-I @ NORM-WCOUNT @ < ARGC @ MAX-ARGS 1- < AND WHILE
    TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + !
    \ A leading quote character is recorded now, while the quotes are
    \ still there to see. EXPAND-WORDS needs it after they are gone,
    \ to tell "" (a field) from ${nope:-} (no field).
    NORM-BUF NORM-WORDS TK-I @ CELLS + @ + C@
    DUP 39 = SWAP 34 = OR
    ARGV-NAME-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
    0 TK-J !
    0 TK-Q !
    BEGIN TK-J @ NORM-WLENS TK-I @ CELLS + @ < WHILE
      NORM-BUF NORM-WORDS TK-I @ CELLS + @ + TK-J @ + C@
      \ ARGV-QUOTED means "do not re-read this as an operator or a
      \ keyword". The splitters and LINE-IS? run on the RAW words now,
      \ so the flag has to be set here rather than by EXPAND-WORDS,
      \ and the question it answers has changed shape: it used to mean
      \ "was quoted, or came out of an expansion", and now means
      \ "contains a quote or a backslash somewhere". Those agree on
      \ everything that matters - an expansion result was never
      \ literal text to begin with, and only literal text can be an
      \ operator (Iteration 114).
      DUP 39 = OVER 34 = OR OVER 92 = OR IF -1 TK-Q ! THEN
      EMIT-TOK-CHAR
      TK-J @ 1+ TK-J !
    REPEAT
    0 EMIT-TOK-CHAR
    TK-Q @ ARGV-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
    ARGC @ 1+ ARGC !
    TK-I @ 1+ TK-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + ! ;

: EXPAND-WORDS ( --- )
  \ ARGV holds the line's raw words. Expands each one into EXP-BUF and
  \ rebuilds ARGV from the results.
  \
  \ One raw word can produce several fields - IFS splitting of an
  \ unquoted expansion - or none at all, which is why the raw list is
  \ snapshotted first rather than rewritten in place.
  \
  \ SCAN-TOKEN stops at unquoted whitespace. That cannot occur inside
  \ a raw word - the spans were cut at exactly those points - but it
  \ certainly can inside a word that has already been expanded once,
  \ and some paths do reach this twice: a group used as a pipeline
  \ stage has its body carved out of an ARGV the enclosing line
  \ expanded, then runs it through RUN-TOKENIZED, whose leaf expands
  \ it again. So each word is scanned until its span is used up,
  \ splitting at any whitespace left in the middle. A second pass then
  \ re-splits rather than truncating - `"a b"` stays two words instead
  \ of becoming `a`.
  ARGC @ EW-ARGC !
  0 EW-I !
  BEGIN EW-I @ EW-ARGC @ < WHILE
    ARGV EW-I @ CELLS + @    EW-ARGV EW-I @ CELLS + !
    ARGV-NAME-QUOTED EW-I @ + C@   EW-ARGQ EW-I @ + C!
    EW-I @ 1+ EW-I !
  REPEAT
  \ Write into whichever of the two buffers the current words are NOT
  \ in. Normally that is EXP-BUF, since the words come from
  \ TOKENIZE-RAW. But a group used as a pipeline stage has its body
  \ carved out of an ARGV the enclosing line already expanded, and
  \ that body is expanded again when it runs; reading and writing one
  \ buffer at once corrupts it, where alternating merely re-splits -
  \ which for an already-expanded word is what would have happened
  \ anyway.
  ARGC @ 0 > IF ARGV @ ELSE 0 THEN
  DUP EXP-BUF 1- > SWAP EXP-BUF BUFFER-SIZE EXP-BUF + < AND IF TOK-BUF ELSE EXP-BUF THEN
  DUP TOK-OUT !
  BUFFER-SIZE TOK-BUF BUFFER-SIZE EXP-BUF MIN + TOK-OUT-END !
  0 ARGC !
  0 EW-I !
  BEGIN EW-I @ EW-ARGC @ < WHILE
    EW-ARGV EW-I @ CELLS + @ TOK-POS !
    TOK-POS @ DUP CSTRLEN + TOK-END !
    BEGIN
    0 TOK-WAS-QUOTED? !
    0 EMPTY-AT-WORD? !
    0 IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? !
    0 IN-DQ-CONTEXT? !
    ARGC @ MAX-ARGS 1- < IF
      TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + !
      EW-ARGQ EW-I @ + C@ ARGV-NAME-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
      SCAN-TOKEN
      0 EMIT-TOK-CHAR
      TOK-WAS-QUOTED? @ ARGV-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
      \ POSIX: an unquoted expansion that produces nothing yields NO
      \ field at all - "echo A ${nope:-} B" passes two arguments, not
      \ three. A word that was quoted still counts, even when empty:
      \ "" is a field.
      \
      \ ARGV-NAME-QUOTED, not TOK-WAS-QUOTED?: the latter is also set
      \ by an expansion (it is EXPAND-VAR's signal that the result is
      \ not to be re-read as an operator), so it cannot distinguish
      \ "" from ${nope:-}. ARGV-NAME-QUOTED records an actual leading
      \ quote character, which is the question being asked here.
      ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ C@ 0=
      ARGV-NAME-QUOTED ARGC @ + C@ 0= EMPTY-AT-WORD? @ OR AND IF
        TOK-OUT @ 1- TOK-OUT !      ( un-emit the NUL )
      ELSE
        ARGC @ 1+ ARGC !
      THEN
    ELSE
      SCAN-TOKEN
      0 EMIT-TOK-CHAR
    THEN
    BEGIN
      TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
    WHILE
      TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    REPEAT
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < 0=
    UNTIL
    EW-I @ 1+ EW-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + ! ;

: TOKENIZE ( u --- )
  \ NORM-BUF holds the normalized line and NORM-WORDS/NORM-WLENS its
  \ word boundaries, both left there by NORM-TOKENIZE's call to
  \ NORMALIZE-OPERATORS. u is that pass's byte count and is no longer
  \ needed: where the words are is recorded rather than re-derived.
  \
  \ Expansion still runs here, immediately. Moving this second call to
  \ the execution paths - so a word is expanded after everything
  \ earlier on its line has run - is the last step of Stage 1b in
  \ PARSE-EXPAND-PLAN.md, and is a change of behaviour rather than of
  \ structure, so it is kept separate from this one.
  DROP
  TOKENIZE-RAW ;

: ARGV-QUOTED@ ( i --- f )  ARGV-QUOTED + C@ ;
: ARGV-NAME-QUOTED@ ( i --- f )  ARGV-NAME-QUOTED + C@ ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Redirection: <, >, >>
\ Scans the tokenized ARGV for these operators; each one found records
\ the filename token that follows it into a dedicated variable, and
\ both tokens are excluded from the argv the command itself will see.
\ Built as a fresh copy into a second array (ARGV2/ARGC2) rather than
\ shifting ARGV in place - an earlier attempt at this (see PROGRESS.md,
\ Iteration 5) got tangled trying to do in-place index shifting with
\ unverified stack tricks; a plain copy-excluding-some-entries pass is
\ far harder to get wrong, at the cost of one extra small buffer.
\ At most one of each operator is honored per command line (a second
\ ">" just overwrites the first's recorded filename); combining
\ multiple output redirections isn't meaningful anyway without pipes.
\ ==================================================================

MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: ARGV2
VARIABLE ARGC2
\ A general list of redirections rather than one slot per operator.
\ The old shape had a fixed REDIR-IN/OUT/APPEND-FILE, which cannot
\ express "2>file" (a redirection on a file descriptor other than the
\ default) or "2>&1" (duplicating one descriptor onto another) at all.
\ Each entry is a target fd, an operation, and either a filename or a
\ source fd. They are applied in order, which matters: "> f 2>&1"
\ sends both streams to the file, while "2>&1 > f" does not.

DEFER READ-INPUT-CALL
\ READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE is defined much further down (it needs the
\ script-file and replay machinery), but here-documents have to read
\ input at redirection-parsing time. Same deferred-word pattern used
\ for RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL and friends.


VARIABLE JC-LEN
VARIABLE JC-N
VARIABLE JC-I

: JC-CONTINUES? ( --- f )
  \ True when LINE-BUF's first JC-LEN bytes end in an ODD number of
  \ backslashes. Each pair is one escaped backslash; an odd one left
  \ over is the continuation.
  \
  \ Parity, not "is the previous character a backslash". That older
  \ test called "a\\\" escaped, because it saw a backslash before the
  \ last one - but three backslashes are an escaped backslash followed
  \ by a real continuation, which is exactly the shape
  \ 2.2-quoted-characters.sh uses.
  \
  \ JC-I is a scratch local rather than a plain global: the run is
  \ rescanned after every join, and READ-INPUT-CALL can reach a great
  \ deal of the shell in between.
  SHADOW{ | JC-I }
  JC-LEN @ JC-I !
  BEGIN
    JC-I @ 0 > IF LINE-BUF JC-I @ 1- + C@ 92 = ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    JC-I @ 1- JC-I !
  REPEAT
  JC-LEN @ JC-I @ - 1 AND 1 = ;

: JOIN-CONTINUATIONS ( u --- u2 )
  \ LINE-BUF holds u bytes. A line ending in an unescaped backslash is
  \ continued by the next one: the backslash and the newline are both
  \ removed and the lines become a single logical line, per POSIX.
  \
  \ Done at the reading end, before quote tracking or tokenizing, so
  \ every consumer sees one line and nothing downstream needs to know
  \ line continuation exists. It reads through READ-INPUT-CALL, so a
  \ continuation inside a loop body or function reads from the stored
  \ body like everything else.
  JC-LEN !
  BEGIN
    JC-CONTINUES?
  WHILE
    JC-LEN @ 1- JC-LEN !
    JC-LEN @ BUFFER-BODY LINE-BUF READ-INPUT-CALL JC-N !
    LONG-LINE? @ IF 0 EXIT THEN        \ nothing of it may run
    JC-N @ 0= IF JC-LEN @ EXIT THEN
    JC-LEN @ JC-N @ + JC-LEN !
  REPEAT
  JC-LEN @ ;

8192 CONSTANT HEREDOC-MAX
HEREDOC-MAX BUFFER: HEREDOC-BUF
LINE-MAX BUFFER: HD-LINE        \ grows: see LINE-BUF
VARIABLE HEREDOC-LEN
VARIABLE HD-N
VARIABLE HD-DELIM
VARIABLE HD-STRIP?
VARIABLE HD-GUARD
VARIABLE HD-S
VARIABLE HD-RD
VARIABLE HD-WR

: HD-LINE-START ( --- addr )
  \ Start of the just-read line, past leading tabs when the operator
  \ was "<<-". POSIX strips tabs only - not spaces - which is what
  \ makes "<<-" usable for indenting a here-document inside a loop
  \ without indenting its contents.
  HD-STRIP? @ 0= IF HD-LINE EXIT THEN
  0 HD-S !
  BEGIN
    HD-S @ HD-N @ < IF HD-LINE HD-S @ + C@ 9 = ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE HD-S @ 1+ HD-S ! REPEAT
  HD-LINE HD-S @ + ;

: HD-READ ( delim-addr --- )
  \ Consume input lines into HEREDOC-BUF until one equals the
  \ delimiter. Reads through READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE, so a here-document
  \ inside a loop body or a function reads from the stored body just
  \ like everything else does.
  \
  \ Scope limits, documented rather than silent: no expansion is
  \ performed on the body (POSIX expands $VAR unless the delimiter is
  \ quoted); one here-document per command; and HD-GUARD stops a
  \ runaway at EOF, since READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE reports an empty line
  \ and end-of-input identically.
  HD-DELIM !
  0 HEREDOC-LEN !
  0 HD-GUARD !
  BEGIN
    0 BUFFER-BODY HD-LINE READ-INPUT-CALL HD-N !
    HD-GUARD @ 1+ HD-GUARD !
    HD-GUARD @ 4096 > IF EXIT THEN
    HD-LINE-START HD-N @ HD-LINE-START HD-LINE - -
    HD-DELIM @ STR0= 0=
  WHILE
    HD-LINE-START
    HD-N @ HD-LINE-START HD-LINE - -            ( addr len )
    DUP HEREDOC-LEN @ + 1+ HEREDOC-MAX < IF
      DUP >R
      HEREDOC-BUF HEREDOC-LEN @ + SWAP MOVE
      HEREDOC-LEN @ R> + HEREDOC-LEN !
      10 HEREDOC-BUF HEREDOC-LEN @ + C!
      HEREDOC-LEN @ 1+ HEREDOC-LEN !
    ELSE
      2DROP
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: REDIR-FD
MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: REDIR-OP
MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: REDIR-TGT
VARIABLE REDIR-N
VARIABLE PR-I     \ shared scan cursor, declared as a local by each scanner
VARIABLE RA-FD
VARIABLE RA-OP
VARIABLE AR-I

: DIGIT-TOKEN? ( i --- f )   \ a one-character token that is a digit
  DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN 1 = SWAP C@ AE-DIGIT? AND ;

: REDIR-OP-AT ( i --- op | 0 )   \ 1 "<"  2 ">"  3 ">>"  4 ">&"  5 "<&"
  DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN
  2DUP S" <"  STR= IF 2DROP 1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" >"  STR= IF 2DROP 2 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" >>" STR= IF 2DROP 3 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" >&" STR= IF 2DROP 4 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" <&" STR= IF 2DROP 5 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" <<" STR= IF 2DROP 6 EXIT THEN
       S" <<-" STR= IF 7 EXIT THEN
  0 ;

: REDIR-ADD ( fd op --- )
  \ Records one redirection, consuming its target token. Reads PR-I,
  \ which is a local of PARSE-REDIRECTIONS - safe because a local IS
  \ the variable, so it holds that invocation's value throughout.
  SHADOW{ RA-FD RA-OP }
  PR-I @ ARGC @ < 0= IF EXIT THEN
  RA-FD @      REDIR-FD  REDIR-N @ CELLS + !
  RA-OP @      REDIR-OP  REDIR-N @ CELLS + !
  PR-I @ ARGV@ REDIR-TGT REDIR-N @ CELLS + !
  REDIR-N @ 1+ REDIR-N !
  RA-OP @ 6 = RA-OP @ 7 = OR IF
    RA-OP @ 7 = HD-STRIP? !
    PR-I @ ARGV@ HD-READ
  THEN
  PR-I @ 1+ PR-I ! ;

: PARSE-REDIRECTIONS ( --- )
  \ Pulls redirections out of ARGV into the list above; what remains
  \ is what the command itself sees. A leading digit attached to an
  \ operator ("2>file") arrives as its own token, so the digit and the
  \ operator are recognised as a pair here rather than in the
  \ tokenizer.
  SHADOW{ | PR-I }
  0 REDIR-N !
  0 ARGC2 !
  BEGIN PR-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    PR-I @ DIGIT-TOKEN? PR-I @ 1+ ARGC @ < AND
    IF PR-I @ 1+ REDIR-OP-AT ELSE 0 THEN
    ?DUP IF
      PR-I @ ARGV@ C@ 48 - SWAP        ( fd op )
      PR-I @ 2 + PR-I !
      REDIR-ADD
    ELSE
      PR-I @ REDIR-OP-AT ?DUP IF
        DUP 2 = OVER 3 = OR OVER 4 = OR IF 1 ELSE 0 THEN SWAP   ( fd op )
        PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
        REDIR-ADD
      ELSE
        PR-I @ ARGV@ ARGV2 ARGC2 @ CELLS + !
        ARGC2 @ 1+ ARGC2 !
        PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
      THEN
    THEN
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV2 ARGC2 @ CELLS + !
  0 PR-I !
  BEGIN PR-I @ ARGC2 @ < WHILE
    ARGV2 PR-I @ CELLS + @  ARGV PR-I @ CELLS + !
    PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV ARGC2 @ CELLS + !
  ARGC2 @ ARGC ! ;

: OPEN-FOR ( c-addr fam --- fid ok? )
  SWAP DUP CSTRLEN ROT OPEN-FILE 0= ;

: APPLY-REDIRECTIONS ( --- )
  \ Runs in the forked child, before RUN-CHILD/EXECVE. A redirection
  \ that fails to open is skipped rather than aborting the command -
  \ no error reporting yet, matching this file's sparse error handling
  \ elsewhere.
  0 AR-I !
  BEGIN AR-I @ REDIR-N @ < WHILE
    REDIR-OP AR-I @ CELLS + @
    DUP 6 = OVER 7 = OR IF
      \ Feed the body through a pipe rather than a temporary file.
      \ The write happens before anything reads, so a body larger than
      \ the pipe buffer would block - HEREDOC-MAX is well under it.
      DROP
      PIPE DROP HD-WR ! HD-RD !
      HEREDOC-BUF HEREDOC-LEN @ HD-WR @ WRITE-FILE DROP
      HD-WR @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
      HD-RD @ REDIR-FD AR-I @ CELLS + @ DUP2 DROP
      HD-RD @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
    ELSE
    DUP 4 = OVER 5 = OR IF
      \ "2>&1": duplicate the named source descriptor onto the target.
      DROP
      REDIR-TGT AR-I @ CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL
      REDIR-FD  AR-I @ CELLS + @ DUP2 DROP
    ELSE
      DUP 1 = IF DROP R/O ELSE 3 = IF A/O ELSE W/O THEN THEN
      REDIR-TGT AR-I @ CELLS + @ SWAP OPEN-FOR IF
        DUP REDIR-FD AR-I @ CELLS + @ DUP2 DROP
        CLOSE-FILE DROP
      ELSE
        DROP
      THEN
    THEN
    THEN
    AR-I @ 1+ AR-I !
  REPEAT ;

\ ==================================================================
\ PATH search + exec
\ ==================================================================

: HAS-SLASH? ( c-addr u --- f )
  \ u can be 0 in principle (an empty ARGV[0], however unlikely in
  \ practice) - guarded explicitly, same reasoning as EXPAND-CMDSUB's
  \ splice loop: this kernel's DO/LOOP doesn't treat start=limit as
  \ zero iterations.
  DUP 0 > IF
    0 DO DUP I + C@ 47 = IF UNLOOP DROP -1 EXIT THEN LOOP
    DROP 0
  ELSE
    2DROP 0
  THEN ;

VARIABLE PATH-CUR
VARIABLE PATH-END2

\ Try one PATH directory (c-addr u, no trailing slash) against ARGV[0].
\ Builds "dir/cmd" into PATHBUF and attempts EXECVE. Only returns if
\ that attempt failed.
: TRY-DIR ( c-addr u --- )
  PATHBUF B-RESET
  B-STR
  47 B-CHAR
  ARGV @ B-CSTR
  B-FINISH
  ARGV PATHBUF EXECVE DROP ;

: SEARCH-PATH ( --- )  \ only returns if every PATH candidate failed
  S" PATH" ENVNAMBUF SWAP MOVE 0 ENVNAMBUF 4 + C!  ( NUL-terminate "PATH" )
  ENVNAMBUF GETENV
  DUP 0= IF DROP EXIT THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN OVER + PATH-END2 !
  PATH-CUR !
  BEGIN PATH-CUR @ PATH-END2 @ < WHILE
    PATH-CUR @             ( start of this dir entry )
    BEGIN
      PATH-CUR @ PATH-END2 @ < IF PATH-CUR @ C@ 58 <> ELSE 0 THEN
    WHILE PATH-CUR @ 1+ PATH-CUR ! REPEAT
    ( start ) PATH-CUR @ OVER -   ( start len )
    TRY-DIR
    PATH-CUR @ PATH-END2 @ < IF PATH-CUR @ 1+ PATH-CUR ! THEN
  REPEAT ;

: RUN-CHILD ( --- )  \ resolve ARGV[0] and exec it; only returns on total failure
  ARGV @ DUP CSTRLEN HAS-SLASH? IF
    ARGV ARGV @ EXECVE DROP
  ELSE
    SEARCH-PATH
  THEN
  \ Reached only when the exec failed, which every caller follows with
  \ `127 SYS-EXIT`. Until Iteration 154 nothing was printed at all:
  \ `relfsh -c nosuchcommand` exited 127 with BOTH streams empty,
  \ where every reference shell reports on stderr. That silence is
  \ why Iteration 152's status-127 bug looked like correct output -
  \ the stray `{` segment was being run as a command name and failing
  \ without a word.
  \
  \ On fd 2 via ERR-*, not `."`: this runs in the forked child, whose
  \ stdout may be a pipe belonging to the next stage of a pipeline,
  \ and a diagnostic must not become that stage's input.
  S" shell: " ERR-TYPE
  ARGV @ ERR-CSTR
  S" : command not found" ERR-TYPE
  ERR-NL ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Builtins - these must run in the shell's own process (not a forked
\ child), since their whole point is to change *this* process's own
\ state (cwd, environment) in a way a child's changes couldn't
\ propagate back from.
\ ==================================================================

: DO-CD ( --- )
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    ARGV 1 CELLS + @ CHDIR
    IF ." cd: no such directory" CR 1 LAST-STATUS ! ELSE 0 LAST-STATUS ! THEN
  ELSE
    S" HOME" ENVNAMBUF SWAP MOVE 0 ENVNAMBUF 4 + C!
    ENVNAMBUF GETENV DUP 0= IF
      DROP 0 LAST-STATUS !
    ELSE
      CHDIR IF 1 ELSE 0 THEN LAST-STATUS !
    THEN
  THEN ;

: DO-PWD ( --- )
  CWDBUF PATH-MAX GETCWD DROP CWDBUF SWAP TYPE CR
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: DO-EXPORT ( --- )
  \ export NAME=VALUE - finds '=' in ARGV[1], splits it, records it
  \ as both a shell-local variable (SET-SHVAR) and an environment
  \ variable (SETENV) - so subsequently-expanded $NAME sees the same
  \ value whether or not it's also inherited by a child process.
  \ export NAME (no '=') exports an already-set shell-local variable
  \ into the environment too, if there is one - a no-op otherwise (no
  \ "mark for future export" tracking yet, a real but small gap
  \ against full POSIX export semantics).
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN
    \ u (ARGV[1]'s length) can be 0 (e.g. `export ""`), guarded for
    \ the same reason as HAS-SLASH?/B-STR above.
    ( c-addr u ) DUP 0 > IF
      0 DO
        DUP I + C@ 61 = IF          ( found '=' at offset I within c-addr )
          DUP I + 0 SWAP C!         ( NUL the '=' in place: c-addr )
          DUP I CHARS + CHAR+       ( c-addr value-addr )
          2DUP SET-SHVAR
          SWAP SETENV DROP
          0 LAST-STATUS !
          UNLOOP EXIT
        THEN
      LOOP
      DROP
      ( no '=' found: bare NAME - export an existing shell-local value )
      ARGV 1 CELLS + @ GET-SHVAR
      DUP 0 <> IF
        ARGV 1 CELLS + @ SETENV DROP
      ELSE
        DROP
      THEN
    ELSE
      2DROP
    THEN
  THEN
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: DO-UNSET ( --- )
  \ unset NAME - removes an exported environment variable and, if
  \ present, a shell-local one (REMOVE-SHVAR) too. Silently does
  \ nothing if given no argument or a name that was never set
  \ (matching real shells: unsetting an already-unset name isn't an
  \ error).
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP REMOVE-SHVAR UNSETENV
    IF 1 ELSE 0 THEN LAST-STATUS !
  ELSE
    0 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

16 CONSTANT MAX-FUNCS
64 CONSTANT FUNC-NAME-MAX

MAX-FUNCS FUNC-NAME-MAX * BUFFER: FUNC-NAMES
VARIABLE FUNC-COUNT

: FUNC-NAME-SLOT ( i --- addr )  FUNC-NAME-MAX * FUNC-NAMES + ;

VARIABLE FF-NAME
VARIABLE FF-I

: FIND-FUNC ( name-addr --- i | -1 )  \ linear scan, matching
                                        \ FIND-SHVAR's own style.
                                        \ Declared here, well before
                                        \ SET-FUNC/RUN-FUNC-BODY define
                                        \ the rest of a function's own
                                        \ storage (a function's body
                                        \ can be arbitrarily large, so
                                        \ that part of the table lives
                                        \ down near RUN-STORED-LINE
                                        \ instead), since DISPATCH
                                        \ needs FIND-FUNC to check
                                        \ whether a command name is
                                        \ actually a defined function
                                        \ before falling through to a
                                        \ builtin or external command.
  SHADOW{ FF-NAME | FF-I }
  BEGIN FF-I @ FUNC-COUNT @ < WHILE
    FF-I @ FUNC-NAME-SLOT FF-NAME @ CSTR= IF FF-I @ EXIT THEN
    FF-I @ 1+ FF-I !
  REPEAT
  -1 ;

DEFER RUN-FUNC-BODY-CALL   \ patched to RUN-FUNC-BODY's own xt right
                              \ after it's defined, much further down -
                              \ the same deferred-word pattern already
                              \ used for RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL, needed
                              \ here for the same reason: DISPATCH is
                              \ defined long before a function's own
                              \ body-replay logic is.


\ ==================================================================
\ test / [ - string, numeric, and (limited) file-existence tests.
\ Scope limits, all deliberate: no -f/-d/-r/-w/-x/-s (would need a
\ real stat/access primitive this kernel doesn't expose, only
\ OPEN-FILE - -e is approximated by attempting to open the path
\ read-only, which can't distinguish a file from a directory); no
\ -a/-o (deprecated in POSIX anyway); no "(" ")" grouping; a 3-arg
\ form is always "STRING1 OP STRING2" and a 4-arg form is always
\ "! STRING1 OP STRING2" - "! -z STRING"-style 3-arg negated unary
\ tests, and 5+ argument forms generally, aren't handled.
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE TEST-START
VARIABLE TEST-END

: TEST-NARGS ( --- n )  TEST-END @ TEST-START @ - ;
: TEST-ARGN ( i --- c-addr )  TEST-START @ + ARGV@ ;

: TEST-PARSE-INT ( c-addr --- n )  \ handles an optional leading "-",
                                      \ unlike PARSE-DECIMAL alone
  DUP C@ 45 = IF
    1+ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL NEGATE
  ELSE
    DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL
  THEN ;

: TEST-FILE-EXISTS? ( c-addr --- f )
  R/O OPEN-FOR IF
    CLOSE-FILE DROP -1
  ELSE
    DROP 0
  THEN ;

: TEST-IS-BANG? ( c-addr --- f )
  S" !" ROT STR0= ;

VARIABLE TEST-OP
VARIABLE TEST-A
VARIABLE TEST-B

: TEST-BINARY? ( a-addr op-addr b-addr --- f )
  SHADOW{ TEST-A TEST-OP TEST-B }
  S" ="   TEST-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-A @ TEST-B @ CSTR= EXIT THEN
  S" !="  TEST-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-A @ TEST-B @ CSTR= 0= EXIT THEN
  S" -eq" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT = EXIT
  THEN
  S" -ne" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT = 0= EXIT
  THEN
  S" -lt" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT < EXIT
  THEN
  S" -le" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT < 0= EXIT
  THEN
  S" -gt" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT < EXIT
  THEN
  S" -ge" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT < 0= EXIT
  THEN
  0 ;

VARIABLE TEST-U-OP
VARIABLE TEST-U-OPERAND

: TEST-UNARY? ( op-addr operand-addr --- f )
  \ -f/-d are approximated as "does this path exist at all" (same as
  \ -e) - this kernel has no real stat/access primitive to actually
  \ distinguish a regular file from a directory, only OPEN-FILE.
  SHADOW{ TEST-U-OP TEST-U-OPERAND }
  S" -z" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ CSTRLEN 0= EXIT THEN
  S" -n" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ CSTRLEN 0= 0= EXIT THEN
  S" -e" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ TEST-FILE-EXISTS? EXIT THEN
  S" -f" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ TEST-FILE-EXISTS? EXIT THEN
  S" -d" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ TEST-FILE-EXISTS? EXIT THEN
  0 ;

: EVAL-TEST-ARGS ( --- f )  \ TEST-START/TEST-END already set by the
                              \ caller - evaluates ARGV[TEST-START,
                              \ TEST-END) as a test expression.
  TEST-NARGS 0 = IF 0 EXIT THEN
  TEST-NARGS 1 = IF
    0 TEST-ARGN CSTRLEN 0= 0= EXIT
  THEN
  TEST-NARGS 2 = IF
    0 TEST-ARGN TEST-IS-BANG? IF
      1 TEST-ARGN CSTRLEN 0= EXIT
    ELSE
      0 TEST-ARGN 1 TEST-ARGN TEST-UNARY? EXIT
    THEN
  THEN
  TEST-NARGS 3 = IF
    0 TEST-ARGN 1 TEST-ARGN 2 TEST-ARGN TEST-BINARY? EXIT
  THEN
  TEST-NARGS 4 = IF
    0 TEST-ARGN TEST-IS-BANG? IF
      1 TEST-ARGN 2 TEST-ARGN 3 TEST-ARGN TEST-BINARY? 0= EXIT
    THEN
  THEN
  0 ;

: DO-TEST ( --- )  \ ARGV[0] is "test" - evaluates ARGV[1..ARGC).
  1 TEST-START !
  ARGC @ TEST-END !
  EVAL-TEST-ARGS IF 0 ELSE 1 THEN LAST-STATUS ! ;

: DO-BRACKET-TEST ( --- )  \ ARGV[0] is "[" - like DO-TEST, but the
                             \ last argument must be a matching "]",
                             \ excluded from the range evaluated.
  ARGC @ 2 < IF
    ." [: missing matching ']'" CR
    2 LAST-STATUS !
    EXIT
  THEN
  ARGC @ 1- ARGV@ S" ]" ROT STR0= 0= IF
    ." [: missing matching ']'" CR
    2 LAST-STATUS !
    EXIT
  THEN
  1 TEST-START !
  ARGC @ 1- TEST-END !
  EVAL-TEST-ARGS IF 0 ELSE 1 THEN LAST-STATUS ! ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Assignment machinery, moved ahead of DISPATCH so `readonly NAME=value`
\ can reuse it instead of reimplementing the split (Iteration 51).
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE AW-I

: FIND-EQ-OR-END ( c-addr --- offset )  \ offset of the first '=' in
                                          \ c-addr, or its CSTRLEN if
                                          \ none is found before the
                                          \ terminating NUL.
  0 AW-I !
  BEGIN
    DUP AW-I @ + C@ DUP 0 <> SWAP 61 <> AND
  WHILE
    AW-I @ 1+ AW-I !
  REPEAT
  DROP AW-I @ ;

: VALID-NAME? ( c-addr u --- f )  \ is (c-addr u) a non-empty,
                                    \ POSIX-valid variable name? (first
                                    \ character not a digit, every
                                    \ character NAME-CHAR? - which
                                    \ itself does allow digits, correct
                                    \ for every character *after* the
                                    \ first).
  DUP 0= IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  OVER C@ DUP 48 < 0= SWAP 58 < AND IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  0 DO
    DUP I + C@ NAME-CHAR? 0= IF UNLOOP DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  LOOP
  DROP -1 ;

: ASSIGNMENT-EQPOS ( c-addr --- eqpos | -1 )  \ c-addr: a NUL-
                                                \ terminated string
                                                \ (ARGV[0]). The offset
                                                \ of its first '=' if
                                                \ everything before it
                                                \ forms a valid,
                                                \ non-empty POSIX name
                                                \ - else -1 (not an
                                                \ assignment word at
                                                \ all).
  DUP FIND-EQ-OR-END
  2DUP + C@ 61 <> IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP VALID-NAME? 0= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  SWAP DROP ;

SHVAR-NAME-MAX BUFFER: ASSIGN-NAME-BUF
SHVAR-VALUE-MAX BUFFER: ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF

VARIABLE DA-ADDR
VARIABLE DA-EQ

: SPLIT-ASSIGN-AT ( c-addr eqpos --- )
  \ Split a validated "NAME=value" word into ASSIGN-NAME-BUF and
  \ ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF, without deciding what to do with them. Shared by
  \ DO-ASSIGN-AT (shell variables) and DO-ALIAS.
  SHADOW{ DA-ADDR DA-EQ }
  DA-ADDR @ DA-EQ @ ASSIGN-NAME-BUF SHVAR-NAME-MAX SAFE-COPY-NUL DROP
  DA-ADDR @ DA-EQ @ + 1+
  DA-ADDR @ CSTRLEN DA-EQ @ - 1-
  ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF SHVAR-VALUE-MAX VALUE-COPY ;

: DO-ASSIGN-AT ( c-addr eqpos --- )
  \ Split a validated "NAME=value" word at its '=' and record it.
  \ Factored out of DO-ASSIGN so `readonly NAME=value` uses the same
  \ code rather than a second copy of the splitting.
  SPLIT-ASSIGN-AT
  ASSIGN-NAME-BUF ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF SET-SHVAR ;

: DO-ASSIGN ( eqpos --- )  \ ARGV[0] is a validated "NAME=value"
                            \ assignment word (see ASSIGNMENT-EQPOS)
                            \ and eqpos is its '='s offset. Splits it
                            \ into ASSIGN-NAME-BUF/ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF and
                            \ records it via SET-SHVAR - does NOT
                            \ export it (see DO-EXPORT for how export
                            \ interacts with this). A no-op (not even
                            \ touching LAST-STATUS) if SUPPRESS-EXEC?
                            \ is set - this line is being read only to
                            \ correctly consume a skipped if/while
                            \ branch's own body, not actually run.
  SUPPRESS-EXEC? @ IF DROP EXIT THEN
  0 VALUE-FAILED? !
  ARGV @ SWAP DO-ASSIGN-AT
  VALUE-FAILED? @ IF 2 ELSE 0 THEN LAST-STATUS ! ;

\ ==================================================================
\ The builtin table
\ ==================================================================
\ DISPATCH used to be sixty lines of
\ `S" name" ARGV @ STR0= IF DO-name -1 EXIT THEN`, one per builtin,
\ and `command -v` needed a second hand-maintained copy of the same
\ name list. Both are replaced by one table: registering a builtin is
\ adding a row, and every consumer reads the same rows.
\
\ A linked list built in the dictionary rather than a fixed array, so
\ there is no maximum number of builtins to pick and get wrong. Each
\ entry is [ link | xt | len | name bytes ], where link and xt are
\ OFFSETS from START - they are written into an image, and an
\ absolute address there is stale the moment it reloads (see
\ FORTH-STYLE.md).
\
\ Because registration is a runtime call rather than compiled-in
\ syntax, Forth code loaded later can add builtins the same way -
\ which is the mechanism the `forth` builtin discussed in Iteration
\ 38 needs.

VARIABLE BUILTIN-LIST   0 BUILTIN-LIST !
VARIABLE BI-XT
VARIABLE BI-ADDR
VARIABLE BI-LEN
VARIABLE BI-HERE

: BUILTIN ( xt c-addr u --- )
  SHADOW{ BI-XT BI-ADDR BI-LEN | BI-HERE }
  \ ALIGN first: HERE after a colon definition is not aligned, and the
  \ entry is cells read with @ (PROGRESS.md 212).
  ALIGN HERE BI-HERE !
  BUILTIN-LIST @ ,
  BI-XT @ START @ - ,
  BI-LEN @ ,
  BI-ADDR @ HERE BI-LEN @ MOVE
  BI-LEN @ ALLOT
  ALIGN
  BI-HERE @ START @ - BUILTIN-LIST ! ;

VARIABLE FB-NAME
VARIABLE FB-E

: FIND-BUILTIN ( c-addr --- xt | 0 )
  SHADOW{ FB-NAME | FB-E }
  BUILTIN-LIST @ FB-E !
  BEGIN FB-E @ WHILE
    FB-E @ START @ +
    DUP 3 CELLS +  OVER 2 CELLS + @   ( entry name-addr name-len )
    FB-NAME @ STR0= IF
      CELL+ @ START @ + EXIT
    THEN
    @ FB-E !
  REPEAT
  0 ;

: BUILTIN-NAME? ( c-addr --- f )  FIND-BUILTIN 0= 0= ;

\ The alias TABLE lives here, ahead of DO-COMMAND, which has to
\ report an alias. The expansion machinery (TRY-ALIAS/DO-ALIAS)
\ stays further down, where NORMALIZE-OPERATORS and TOKENIZE are
\ available.

32  CONSTANT MAX-ALIASES
64  CONSTANT ALIAS-NAME-MAX
256 CONSTANT ALIAS-VALUE-MAX
MAX-ALIASES ALIAS-NAME-MAX  * BUFFER: ALIAS-NAMES
MAX-ALIASES ALIAS-VALUE-MAX * BUFFER: ALIAS-VALUES
LINE-MAX BUFFER: ALIAS-LINE
VARIABLE ALIAS-COUNT
VARIABLE ALIAS-LINE-LEN
VARIABLE ALIAS-DEPTH
VARIABLE AL-I
VARIABLE AL-LEN
VARIABLE AL-NAME
VARIABLE AL-VALUE

: ALIAS-NAME-SLOT  ( i --- addr )  ALIAS-NAME-MAX  * ALIAS-NAMES  + ;
: ALIAS-VALUE-SLOT ( i --- addr )  ALIAS-VALUE-MAX * ALIAS-VALUES + ;

: FIND-ALIAS ( name-addr --- i | -1 )
  SHADOW{ AL-NAME | AL-I }
  BEGIN AL-I @ ALIAS-COUNT @ < WHILE
    AL-I @ ALIAS-NAME-SLOT AL-NAME @ CSTR= IF AL-I @ EXIT THEN
    AL-I @ 1+ AL-I !
  REPEAT
  -1 ;

: SET-ALIAS ( name-addr value-addr --- )
  SHADOW{ AL-NAME AL-VALUE | AL-I }
  AL-NAME @ FIND-ALIAS AL-I !
  AL-I @ -1 = IF
    ALIAS-COUNT @ MAX-ALIASES < 0= IF
      ." alias: too many aliases" CR EXIT
    THEN
    ALIAS-COUNT @ AL-I !
    ALIAS-COUNT @ 1+ ALIAS-COUNT !
  THEN
  AL-NAME  @ AL-I @ ALIAS-NAME-SLOT  ALIAS-NAME-MAX  SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  AL-VALUE @ AL-I @ ALIAS-VALUE-SLOT ALIAS-VALUE-MAX SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP ;

VARIABLE DO-I


: MARK-READONLY ( name-addr --- )
  FIND-SHVAR DUP -1 = IF DROP EXIT THEN
  SHVAR-RO + -1 SWAP C! ;

: PRINT-READONLY ( --- )
  \ "readonly -p", and "readonly" with no operands: list the read-only
  \ variables in a form that could be re-read as input, per POSIX.
  0 DO-I !
  BEGIN DO-I @ SHVAR-COUNT @ < WHILE
    DO-I @ SHVAR-RO + C@ IF
      ." readonly "
      DO-I @ SHVAR-NAME-SLOT DUP CSTRLEN TYPE
      [CHAR] = EMIT
      DO-I @ SHVAR-VALUE-SLOT DUP CSTRLEN TYPE
      CR
    THEN
    DO-I @ 1+ DO-I !
  REPEAT ;

: DO-READONLY ( --- )
  \ readonly NAME | readonly NAME=value | readonly -p. With a value it
  \ assigns first, then marks; the mark must come second or the
  \ assignment would be refused by its own flag.
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ARGC @ 1 = IF PRINT-READONLY EXIT THEN
  ARGC @ 2 = IF
    S" -p" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF PRINT-READONLY EXIT THEN
  THEN
  ARGC @ 1 < IF EXIT THEN
  1 DO-I !
  BEGIN DO-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    DO-I @ ARGV@ ASSIGNMENT-EQPOS
    DUP -1 = IF
      DROP
      DO-I @ ARGV@ MARK-READONLY
    ELSE
      DO-I @ ARGV@ SWAP DO-ASSIGN-AT
      ASSIGN-NAME-BUF MARK-READONLY
    THEN
    DO-I @ 1+ DO-I !
  REPEAT ;

: DO-SHIFT ( --- )
  \ shift [n]: drop the first n positional parameters. Out-of-range
  \ n is an error and leaves the parameters untouched, per POSIX.
  1
  ARGC @ 1 > IF DROP ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL THEN
  DUP POS-PARAM-COUNT @ > IF
    DROP ." shift: count out of range" CR 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
  THEN
  \ "shift 0" is valid and a no-op. Without this guard the count of 0
  \ reaches DO as "0 0 DO", which in this kernel runs the entire
  \ unsigned range - the same hazard as TYPE-N-TO-TOK in Iteration 88,
  \ found by auditing every DO here rather than by anything hitting it.
  DUP 0 > IF
  DUP 0 DO
    0 DO-I !
    BEGIN DO-I @ 1+ POS-PARAM-COUNT @ < WHILE
      DO-I @ 1+ POS-PARAM-SLOT DO-I @ POS-PARAM-SLOT POS-PARAM-MAX MOVE
      DO-I @ 1+ DO-I !
    REPEAT
    POS-PARAM-COUNT @ 1- POS-PARAM-COUNT !
  LOOP
  THEN
  DROP
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: CMD-V-FOUND ( c-addr u --- )   \ report a hit and exit 0
  TYPE CR 0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: PATH-LOOKUP? ( --- f )
  \ Is ARGV[1] findable in PATH? Prints the full path if so. Same
  \ PATH walk as SEARCH-PATH, but testing for existence instead of
  \ exec'ing - deliberately a separate word rather than a flag on
  \ SEARCH-PATH, whose whole contract is "only returns on failure".
  S" PATH" ENVNAMBUF SWAP MOVE 0 ENVNAMBUF 4 + C!
  ENVNAMBUF GETENV
  DUP 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN OVER + PATH-END2 !
  PATH-CUR !
  BEGIN PATH-CUR @ PATH-END2 @ < WHILE
    PATH-CUR @
    BEGIN
      PATH-CUR @ PATH-END2 @ < IF PATH-CUR @ C@ 58 <> ELSE 0 THEN
    WHILE PATH-CUR @ 1+ PATH-CUR ! REPEAT
    PATH-CUR @ OVER -
    PATHBUF B-RESET B-STR 47 B-CHAR ARGV 1 CELLS + @ B-CSTR B-FINISH
    PATHBUF TEST-FILE-EXISTS? IF
      PATHBUF DUP CSTRLEN CMD-V-FOUND
      -1 EXIT
    THEN
    PATH-CUR @ PATH-END2 @ < IF PATH-CUR @ 1+ PATH-CUR ! THEN
  REPEAT
  0 ;

VARIABLE RW-NAME

: RESERVED-WORD? ( c-addr --- f )
  \ The POSIX shell reserved words. Kept as one list here rather than
  \ scattered through RUN-TOKENIZED's own LINE-IS? checks, which test
  \ them individually for a different purpose (dispatch, not
  \ classification).
  RW-NAME !
  S" if"    RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" then"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" else"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" elif"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" fi"    RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" do"    RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" done"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" case"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" esac"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" while" RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" until" RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" for"   RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" in"    RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" {"     RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" }"     RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" !"     RW-NAME @ STR0= ;

: DO-COMMAND ( --- )
  \ command -v NAME. The other forms (command NAME args, -p, -V) are
  \ not implemented; -v is what scripts actually use to test for a
  \ command's existence.
  ARGC @ 3 < IF 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  S" -v" 1 ARGV@ STR0= 0= IF 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  \ POSIX: command -v reports reserved words and aliases too, not just
  \ functions, builtins and PATH entries. Order matters - an alias
  \ shadows a command of the same name, and a reserved word is not a
  \ command at all.
  2 ARGV@ RESERVED-WORD? IF
    2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN CMD-V-FOUND EXIT
  THEN
  2 ARGV@ FIND-ALIAS DUP -1 <> IF
    ." alias " 2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN TYPE
    [CHAR] = EMIT [CHAR] ' EMIT
    ALIAS-VALUE-SLOT DUP CSTRLEN TYPE
    [CHAR] ' EMIT CR
    0 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
  THEN
  DROP
  2 ARGV@ FIND-FUNC -1 <> IF
    2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN CMD-V-FOUND EXIT
  THEN
  2 ARGV@ BUILTIN-NAME? IF
    2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN CMD-V-FOUND EXIT
  THEN
  ARGV 2 CELLS + @ ARGV 1 CELLS + !   \ PATH-LOOKUP? reads ARGV[1]
  PATH-LOOKUP? 0= IF 1 LAST-STATUS ! THEN ;


\ The builtins that used to be written inline in DISPATCH's chain.

: DO-COLON ( --- )  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: DO-EXIT ( --- )
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL SYS-EXIT
  ELSE
    LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT
  THEN ;

: DO-RETURN ( --- )
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL LAST-STATUS !
  THEN
  FUNC-DEPTH @ 0 > IF
    -1 RETURN-PENDING? !
  ELSE
    ." return: not in a function" CR
  THEN ;

: DO-BREAK ( --- )
  LOOP-DEPTH @ 0 > IF
    -1 LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? !  -1 LOOP-BREAK? !  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ELSE
    ." break: not in a loop" CR  1 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

: DO-CONTINUE ( --- )
  LOOP-DEPTH @ 0 > IF
    -1 LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? !  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ELSE
    ." continue: not in a loop" CR  1 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

: DO-SET ( --- )
  \ "set a b c" replaces whichever positional parameters are currently
  \ active. Scope limit: option flags (-e, -x) are not handled, so
  \ "set -e" would be treated as setting $1 to the literal "-e".
  \ "set -- a b c" is the standard way to set the parameters safely:
  \ the "--" marks the end of options so a value beginning with '-'
  \ is not mistaken for one. It must not itself become $1.
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    S" --" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF
      \ shifted in place; SHIFT-ARGV-DOWN itself is defined further
      \ down the file. ARGC is at least 2 here, so the loop count is
      \ at least 1 - this kernel's DO with start = limit would run the
      \ whole unsigned range.
      ARGC @ 1- 0 DO
        I 1+ CELLS ARGV + @     I CELLS ARGV + !
        I 1+ ARGV-QUOTED + C@   I ARGV-QUOTED + C!
      LOOP
      ARGC @ 1- ARGC !
      0 ARGC @ CELLS ARGV + !
    THEN
  THEN
  SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-ARGV
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

LINE-MAX BUFFER: FORTH-BUF
VARIABLE FTH-LEN
VARIABLE FA-ADDR
VARIABLE FA-LEN
VARIABLE FTH-I

: FORTH-APPEND ( c-addr u --- )
  SHADOW{ FA-ADDR FA-LEN }
  FTH-LEN @ FA-LEN @ + 2 + ENSURE-BUFFER FORTH-BUF
  FTH-LEN @ FA-LEN @ + LINE-HARD-MAX < IF
    FA-ADDR @ FORTH-BUF FTH-LEN @ + FA-LEN @ MOVE
    FTH-LEN @ FA-LEN @ + FTH-LEN !
    32 FORTH-BUF FTH-LEN @ + C!
    FTH-LEN @ 1+ FTH-LEN !
  THEN ;

: DO-FORTH ( --- )
  \ forth WORDS... - hand the rest of the line to the Forth
  \ interpreter. This is the extension mechanism discussed back in
  \ Iteration 38: the shell is written in Forth and the interpreter is
  \ already present, so an escape hatch costs almost nothing - and
  \ because BUILTIN is an ordinary runtime call, Forth loaded this way
  \ can register further builtins itself.
  \
  \ The arguments arrive already tokenized and expanded, so $VAR works
  \ and quoting is the shell's, not Forth's; they are rejoined with
  \ single spaces, which is fine for Forth's own whitespace-delimited
  \ syntax. Caveats, deliberately not papered over: a Forth error
  \ ABORTs the whole shell, and a word that unbalances the stack
  \ corrupts this shell rather than a child process, because there is
  \ no isolation. That is the nature of the facility.
  SHADOW{ | FTH-I }
  0 FTH-LEN !
  1 FTH-I !
  BEGIN FTH-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    FTH-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN FORTH-APPEND
    FTH-I @ 1+ FTH-I !
  REPEAT
  FORTH-BUF FTH-LEN @ EVALUATE
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

LINE-MAX        BUFFER: READ-BUF     \ grows: see LINE-BUF
SHVAR-VALUE-MAX BUFFER: READ-FIELD
VARIABLE RD-LEN
VARIABLE RD-POS
VARIABLE RD-VI
VARIABLE RD-NAME
VARIABLE RD-S
VARIABLE RD-E

: RD-SKIP-WS ( --- )
  BEGIN
    RD-POS @ RD-LEN @ < IF READ-BUF RD-POS @ + C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE RD-POS @ 1+ RD-POS ! REPEAT ;

: RD-SET ( name-addr start end --- )   \ assign READ-BUF[start,end)
  SHADOW{ RD-NAME RD-S RD-E }
  READ-BUF RD-S @ + RD-E @ RD-S @ -
  READ-FIELD SHVAR-VALUE-MAX VALUE-COPY
  RD-NAME @ READ-FIELD SET-SHVAR ;

: DO-READ ( --- )
  \ read [-r] NAME... - one line from the real stdin, split on
  \ whitespace, one field per name; the LAST name receives everything
  \ that remains, trailing blanks trimmed, per POSIX.
  \
  \ Reads fd 0 directly rather than through READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE:
  \ that word reads the *script* when one is running, which is what
  \ if/while need, but `read` must take the process's own stdin or
  \ "while read x" over a pipe would consume the script instead.
  \ READ-LINE's flag distinguishes a genuine EOF (status 1) from an
  \ empty line, which ACCEPT alone could not.
  \
  \ -r is accepted and ignored: this shell does no backslash
  \ processing on the line either way, so -r is already the behaviour.
  SHADOW{ | RD-VI }
  0 VALUE-FAILED? !
  0 BUFFER-BODY READ-BUF 0 READ-LINE-INTO
  LONG-LINE? @ IF 2DROP EXIT THEN      \ reported; the variables are left alone
  0= IF DROP 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  RD-LEN !
  0 RD-POS !
  1 RD-VI !
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    S" -r" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF 2 RD-VI ! THEN
  THEN
  ARGC @ RD-VI @ > 0= IF 0 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  BEGIN
    RD-LEN @ 0 > IF READ-BUF RD-LEN @ 1- + C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    RD-LEN @ 1- RD-LEN !
  REPEAT
  BEGIN RD-VI @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    RD-SKIP-WS
    RD-POS @
    RD-VI @ 1+ ARGC @ < IF
      BEGIN
        RD-POS @ RD-LEN @ < IF READ-BUF RD-POS @ + C@ WS? 0= ELSE 0 THEN
      WHILE RD-POS @ 1+ RD-POS ! REPEAT
    ELSE
      RD-LEN @ RD-POS !
    THEN
    RD-VI @ ARGV@ SWAP RD-POS @ RD-SET
    RD-VI @ 1+ RD-VI !
  REPEAT
  VALUE-FAILED? @ IF 2 ELSE 0 THEN LAST-STATUS ! ;

64 BUFFER: GO-NMBUF
8  BUFFER: GO-VALBUF
VARIABLE GO-CPOS      \ character position within the current argument
VARIABLE GO-LASTIND   \ OPTIND as this word last left it
VARIABLE GO-IND
VARIABLE GO-OPTS
VARIABLE GO-NAME
VARIABLE GO-ARG
VARIABLE GO-C
VARIABLE GO-J
VARIABLE GO-POS
VARIABLE GO-NMLEN

: >NM ( c-addr u --- c-addr' )
  \ NUL-terminated copy of an S" literal, for the shell-variable words
  \ which all take C strings.
  DUP GO-NMLEN !
  GO-NMBUF SWAP MOVE
  0 GO-NMBUF GO-NMLEN @ + C!
  GO-NMBUF ;

: GO-OPTIND@ ( --- n )
  S" OPTIND" >NM LOOKUP-VAR
  DUP 0= IF DROP 1 EXIT THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL ;

: GO-OPTIND! ( n --- )   N>STR S" OPTIND" >NM SWAP SET-SHVAR ;
: GO-OPTARG! ( c-addr --- )  S" OPTARG" >NM SWAP SET-SHVAR ;

: GO-SETNAME ( c --- )   \ set the user's variable to one character
  GO-VALBUF C!  0 GO-VALBUF 1+ C!
  GO-NAME @ GO-VALBUF SET-SHVAR ;

: GO-FIND-OPT ( c --- pos | -1 )
  GO-C !
  0 GO-J !
  BEGIN GO-J @ GO-OPTS @ CSTRLEN < WHILE
    GO-OPTS @ GO-J @ + C@ GO-C @ = IF GO-J @ EXIT THEN
    GO-J @ 1+ GO-J !
  REPEAT
  -1 ;

: GO-DONE ( c --- )   \ no more options: set the variable and return 1
  GO-SETNAME
  GO-IND @ GO-OPTIND!
  GO-IND @ GO-LASTIND !
  1 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: GO-CLEAR-OPTARG ( --- )
  \ An option that takes no argument leaves OPTARG unset, so a value
  \ from a previous iteration must not linger - "opt=a arg=val" where
  \ the val belonged to the -c before it.
  0 GO-VALBUF C!
  S" OPTARG" >NM GO-VALBUF SET-SHVAR ;

: GO-STEP ( --- )   \ finished with this character; advance if at end
  GO-CPOS @ 1+ GO-CPOS !
  GO-ARG @ GO-CPOS @ + C@ 0= IF
    GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND !
    1 GO-CPOS !
  THEN ;

: GO-FINISH ( --- )
  GO-IND @ GO-OPTIND!
  GO-IND @ GO-LASTIND !
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: DO-GETOPTS ( --- )
  \ getopts OPTSTRING NAME - parse the positional parameters one
  \ option at a time, per POSIX. OPTIND is a shell variable so a
  \ script can reset it; GO-CPOS tracks the position *within* a
  \ clustered argument like "-abc" and is reset whenever OPTIND
  \ changes underneath us, which is how "OPTIND=1" restarts parsing.
  ARGC @ 3 < IF 2 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  1 ARGV@ GO-OPTS !
  2 ARGV@ GO-NAME !
  GO-OPTIND@ GO-IND !
  GO-IND @ GO-LASTIND @ <> IF 1 GO-CPOS ! THEN
  GO-CPOS @ 0= IF 1 GO-CPOS ! THEN
  BEGIN
    GO-IND @ POS-PARAM-COUNT @ > IF 63 GO-DONE EXIT THEN
    GO-IND @ 1- POS-PARAM-SLOT GO-ARG !
    GO-ARG @ C@ 45 <> IF 63 GO-DONE EXIT THEN
    GO-ARG @ CSTRLEN 1 = IF 63 GO-DONE EXIT THEN
    GO-ARG @ CSTRLEN 2 = GO-ARG @ 1+ C@ 45 = AND IF
      GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND ! 63 GO-DONE EXIT
    THEN
    GO-ARG @ GO-CPOS @ + C@ GO-C !
    GO-C @ 0= IF
      GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND !
      1 GO-CPOS !
    ELSE
      GO-C @ GO-FIND-OPT GO-POS !
      GO-POS @ -1 = IF
        GO-CLEAR-OPTARG 63 GO-SETNAME GO-STEP GO-FINISH EXIT
      THEN
      GO-OPTS @ GO-POS @ 1+ + C@ 58 = IF
        \ this option takes an argument: the rest of this word if
        \ there is any, otherwise the whole of the next one
        GO-CPOS @ 1+ GO-CPOS !
        GO-ARG @ GO-CPOS @ + C@ 0= IF
          GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND !
          GO-IND @ POS-PARAM-COUNT @ > IF
            58 GO-SETNAME
          ELSE
            GO-IND @ 1- POS-PARAM-SLOT GO-OPTARG!
            GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND !
            GO-C @ GO-SETNAME
          THEN
        ELSE
          GO-ARG @ GO-CPOS @ + GO-OPTARG!
          GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND !
          GO-C @ GO-SETNAME
        THEN
        1 GO-CPOS !
        GO-FINISH EXIT
      THEN
      GO-CLEAR-OPTARG GO-C @ GO-SETNAME GO-STEP GO-FINISH EXIT
    THEN
  0 UNTIL ;

VARIABLE WT-PID

: DO-WAIT ( --- )
  \ wait [pid]: with an operand, wait for that child; with none, wait
  \ for all of them. A shell with no children at all is not an error.
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL WAITPID
    DUP 0= IF DROP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND LAST-STATUS ! ELSE 2DROP THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  BEGIN
    -1 WAITPID          ( raw ior )
    DUP 0= WHILE
    DROP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND LAST-STATUS !
  REPEAT
  2DROP ;

\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ ulimit - POSIX specifies exactly one resource, the file size limit,
\ in 512-byte blocks. GETFSIZE/SETFSIZE (engine primitives, Iteration
\ 125) already work in blocks for that reason, so there is no
\ conversion here. bash reports 1024-byte blocks instead, which is why
\ mrsh's ulimit.sh passes under sh and fails under bash - see
\ PROGRESS.md's Iteration 124 entry.
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------

: TYPE-UNSIGNED ( u --- )   \ print without Forth's trailing space
  0 <# #S #> TYPE ;

: TYPE-FSIZE ( --- )
  GETFSIZE DUP 0< IF DROP ." unlimited" ELSE TYPE-UNSIGNED THEN CR ;

: ULIMIT-ARG ( c-addr --- n )   \ "unlimited" or a block count
  DUP CSTRLEN 2DUP S" unlimited" 2SWAP STR= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  PARSE-DECIMAL ;

: DO-ULIMIT ( --- )
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ARGC @ 1 = IF TYPE-FSIZE EXIT THEN
  S" -f" 1 ARGV@ STR0= 0= IF
    ." ulimit: only -f is supported" CR 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
  THEN
  ARGC @ 2 = IF TYPE-FSIZE EXIT THEN
  2 ARGV@ ULIMIT-ARG SETFSIZE IF
    ." ulimit: cannot set limit" CR 1 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

' DO-WAIT         S" wait"     BUILTIN
' DO-ULIMIT       S" ulimit"   BUILTIN
' DO-GETOPTS      S" getopts"  BUILTIN
' DO-READ         S" read"     BUILTIN
' DO-FORTH        S" forth"    BUILTIN
' DO-COLON        S" :"        BUILTIN
' DO-TEST         S" test"     BUILTIN
' DO-BRACKET-TEST S" ["        BUILTIN
' DO-CD           S" cd"       BUILTIN
' DO-PWD          S" pwd"      BUILTIN
' DO-EXPORT       S" export"   BUILTIN
' DO-UNSET        S" unset"    BUILTIN
' DO-EXIT         S" exit"     BUILTIN
' DO-RETURN       S" return"   BUILTIN
' DO-BREAK        S" break"    BUILTIN
' DO-CONTINUE     S" continue" BUILTIN
' DO-SET          S" set"      BUILTIN
' DO-SHIFT        S" shift"    BUILTIN
' DO-READONLY     S" readonly" BUILTIN
' DO-COMMAND      S" command"  BUILTIN

: DISPATCH ( --- f )  \ f = -1 if handled as a builtin, 0 if not
  \ A quoted 'cd' is a literal argument, not the builtin - same
  \ reasoning as ARGV-QUOTED everywhere else. Functions are checked
  \ after builtins, so a builtin name cannot be overridden by a
  \ function, matching real shells.
  ARGC @ 0 > 0 ARGV-QUOTED@ 0= AND IF
    ARGV @ FIND-BUILTIN ?DUP IF EXECUTE -1 EXIT THEN
    ARGV @ FIND-FUNC DUP -1 <> IF
      RUN-FUNC-BODY-CALL -1 EXIT
    THEN
    DROP
  THEN
  0 ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Main loop
\ ==================================================================

: RUN-EXTERNAL ( --- )
  FORK
  DUP 0 = IF
    DROP APPLY-REDIRECTIONS RUN-CHILD 127 SYS-EXIT
  ELSE
    WAITPID DROP           ( raw wait status )
    8 RSHIFT 255 AND       ( decode WEXITSTATUS - the normal-exit case only;
                              a signal-terminated child isn't distinguished
                              from one that exited with that same low byte
                              in v0.1 - see PROGRESS.md )
    LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

: SH-PROMPT ( --- )
  \ To fd 2, not stdout. Every reference shell - bash, dash, mksh,
  \ ksh, yash, posh and busybox - writes its prompt to stderr, and for
  \ a good reason: `... | relfsh` had the prompt interleaved into the
  \ shell's own output, so a piped script's stdout was not what the
  \ script printed. `printf 'echo hi\n' | relfsh` produced "$ \nhi\n$ "
  \ where every other shell produces "hi\n".
  \
  \ This shell still PRINTS a prompt when stdin is not a terminal,
  \ which the others do not - that needs an isatty the engine has no
  \ primitive for, and adding one means touching cross.4's
  \ hand-embedded dispatch numbers, deliberately not done here. On
  \ stderr the remaining divergence no longer corrupts stdout.
  S" $ " 2 WRITE-FILE DROP ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Pipes: a single "cmd1 | cmd2" per line
\ Split off into its own pass, run *before* redirection parsing, and
\ deliberately mutually exclusive with it for v0.2: if a line has a
\ "|", redirection syntax on either side is left untouched (passed
\ through as a literal argument) rather than risked - combining the
\ two correctly (figuring out which side a "<"/">" belongs to) is
\ real complexity better tackled once each piece is independently
\ solid, not solved speculatively alongside the first pipe support.
\ Builtins aren't supported on either side of a pipe in v0.2 either -
\ both sides always run via RUN-CHILD (PATH search + exec), reusing
\ that already-proven logic unchanged by copying each side's own argv
\ into the *global* ARGV/ARGC from within that side's own forked
\ child - safe, since fork() gives each child an independent copy of
\ all memory, so overwriting ARGV there can't affect the parent or
\ the other child.
\ ==================================================================

MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: PIPE-ARGV   \ a private copy of the whole
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: PIPE-ARGQ         \ tokenized line, so each
VARIABLE PIPE-ARGC                      \ segment can be extracted
VARIABLE PIPE-NSEG                      \ without disturbing ARGV
VARIABLE PIPE-PRESENT?

: AT-END? ( --- f )  PR-I @ ARGC @ < 0= ;

VARIABLE GRP-DEPTH
\ How many '('/'{' are open at the scan position. Every operator scan
\ below (';', '&&', '||', '|') only recognises its operator at depth
\ 0, so a group used as an ordinary command keeps its own separators
\ to itself: "{ a; b; } && c" splits at the '&&', not at either ';'.
\ Bash gets this from a real grammar; this is the flat-token-array
\ equivalent - see GOALS.md's notes on Ramey's bash chapter.

: GRP-TRACK ( i --- )   \ update GRP-DEPTH for the token just consumed
  DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN
  2DUP S" (" STR= IF 2DROP GRP-DEPTH @ 1+ GRP-DEPTH ! EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" {" STR= IF 2DROP GRP-DEPTH @ 1+ GRP-DEPTH ! EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" )" STR= IF 2DROP GRP-DEPTH @ 1- GRP-DEPTH ! EXIT THEN
  S" }" STR= IF GRP-DEPTH @ 1- GRP-DEPTH ! THEN ;

: AT-GROUP-END? ( --- f )   \ is the whole line one group and nothing else?
  ARGC @ 0 = IF 0 EXIT THEN
  0 GRP-DEPTH !
  0 PR-I !
  BEGIN PR-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    PR-I @ GRP-TRACK
    GRP-DEPTH @ 0 = PR-I @ 1+ ARGC @ < AND IF 0 EXIT THEN
    PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
  REPEAT
  -1 ;

\ ==================================================================
\ ';' - multiple commands per line, run in sequence regardless of
\ each one's own exit status (unlike '&&'/'||', not yet implemented).
\ Same whitespace-delimited-token convention as '|' below (';' only
\ acts as a separator when it's its own token - "a;b" is one literal
\ argument, same as "a|b" already is). Splits off *only* the first
\ ';' each call, leaving everything after it (which may itself
\ contain further ';'s) in ARGV-SEMI-REST for RUN-TOKENIZED to feed
\ back through this same logic on its next call - see RUN-TOKENIZED
\ for how the two pieces get run in turn.
\ ==================================================================

MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: ARGV-SEMI-LEFT
MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: ARGV-SEMI-REST
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: ARGQ-SEMI-LEFT
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: ARGQ-SEMI-REST
VARIABLE ARGC-SEMI-LEFT
VARIABLE ARGC-SEMI-REST

: AT-TOKEN? ( c-addr u --- f )
  \ Is the token at PR-I this exact literal, unquoted? Six predicates
  \ used to spell this out separately, differing only in the literal.
  AT-END? IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  PR-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@ IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  PR-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN 2SWAP STR= ;

: AT-OP? ( c-addr u --- f )
  \ AT-TOKEN?, but only outside any group - so a ';' or '|' belonging
  \ to a "( ... )" body is not mistaken for the enclosing line's own.
  GRP-DEPTH @ 0 <> IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  AT-TOKEN? ;

: AT-SEMI? ( --- f )  S" ;"  AT-OP? ;

: SPLIT-SEMI ( --- f )  \ f = -1 if a ';' token was found (the part
                          \ before it is in ARGV-SEMI-LEFT/ARGC-SEMI-
                          \ LEFT (each entry's own ARGV-QUOTED flag at
                          \ split time mirrored into ARGQ-SEMI-LEFT,
                          \ for COPY-ARGV-Q to restore later - see its
                          \ own comment for why this matters), the
                          \ part after in ARGV-SEMI-REST/ARGC-SEMI-
                          \ REST/ARGQ-SEMI-REST - either can be empty,
                          \ e.g. a leading or trailing ';'), 0
                          \ otherwise (nothing touched).
  SHADOW{ | PR-I }
  0 GRP-DEPTH !
  0 ARGC-SEMI-LEFT !
  BEGIN AT-END? AT-SEMI? OR 0= WHILE
    PR-I @ GRP-TRACK
    PR-I @ ARGV@ ARGV-SEMI-LEFT ARGC-SEMI-LEFT @ CELLS + !
    PR-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@ ARGQ-SEMI-LEFT ARGC-SEMI-LEFT @ + C!
    ARGC-SEMI-LEFT @ 1+ ARGC-SEMI-LEFT !
    PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV-SEMI-LEFT ARGC-SEMI-LEFT @ CELLS + !
  AT-SEMI? 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !                    ( skip the ";" token itself )
  0 ARGC-SEMI-REST !
  BEGIN AT-END? 0= WHILE
    PR-I @ ARGV@ ARGV-SEMI-REST ARGC-SEMI-REST @ CELLS + !
    PR-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@ ARGQ-SEMI-REST ARGC-SEMI-REST @ + C!
    ARGC-SEMI-REST @ 1+ ARGC-SEMI-REST !
    PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV-SEMI-REST ARGC-SEMI-REST @ CELLS + !
  -1 ;

: AT-PIPE? ( --- f )  S" |" AT-OP? ;

: PIPE-AT-BAR? ( i --- f )   \ is PIPE-ARGV[i] an unquoted "|" ?
  DUP PIPE-ARGQ + C@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  CELLS PIPE-ARGV + @ DUP CSTRLEN S" |" STR= ;

VARIABLE SP-I

: SPLIT-PIPE ( --- )
  \ Copies the whole tokenized line into PIPE-ARGV/PIPE-ARGQ and counts
  \ the unquoted "|" tokens. Segments are extracted lazily by
  \ RUN-PIPELINE rather than being split into fixed left/right arrays,
  \ which is what lifts the old one-pipe-per-line limit: a line has
  \ PIPE-NSEG segments for any number of pipes.
  SHADOW{ | SP-I }
  0 GRP-DEPTH !
  0 PIPE-PRESENT? !
  ARGC @ PIPE-ARGC !
  1 PIPE-NSEG !
  BEGIN SP-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    SP-I @ ARGV@         PIPE-ARGV SP-I @ CELLS + !
    SP-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@  PIPE-ARGQ SP-I @ + C!
    SP-I @ 1+ SP-I !
  REPEAT
  0 SP-I !
  BEGIN SP-I @ PIPE-ARGC @ < WHILE
    SP-I @ PIPE-AT-BAR? IF
      PIPE-NSEG @ 1+ PIPE-NSEG !
      -1 PIPE-PRESENT? !
    THEN
    SP-I @ 1+ SP-I !
  REPEAT ;

VARIABLE PSO-I
VARIABLE PSO-LEN

: PIPE-SEGMENTS-OK? ( --- f )
  \ Every segment must be non-empty, matching the old "both sides
  \ non-empty" guard - "a | | b" or a trailing "|" runs nothing rather
  \ than misbehaving.
  SHADOW{ | PSO-I PSO-LEN }
  BEGIN PSO-I @ PIPE-ARGC @ < WHILE
    PSO-I @ PIPE-AT-BAR? IF
      PSO-LEN @ 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
      0 PSO-LEN !
    ELSE
      PSO-LEN @ 1+ PSO-LEN !
    THEN
    PSO-I @ 1+ PSO-I !
  REPEAT
  PSO-LEN @ 0= 0= ;

VARIABLE CAQ-ARGV
VARIABLE CAQ-ARGQ
VARIABLE CAQ-N

: COPY-ARGV-Q ( src-argv src-argq src-argc --- )
  \ Overwrite the global ARGV/ARGC with a segment, carrying each
  \ entry's own ARGV-QUOTED flag with it from a parallel byte array
  \ captured at split time.
  \
  \ There used to be a bare COPY-ARGV beside this, moving the words
  \ and not the flags, and leaving ARGV-QUOTED holding whatever a
  \ PREVIOUS tokenization left at each index. Every stale-flag bug
  \ this project has had was a call to it: Iteration 28's vanishing
  \ "&&" (an earlier segment's expansion result, always marked
  \ quoted, sat at the same index and hid a real operator),
  \ Iteration 47's literal ";", Iteration 49's group body. Two of
  \ its call sites were fixed in 28 and GOALS.md has carried "the
  \ extent of the same hazard at the other call sites is unverified"
  \ ever since.
  \
  \ It is verified now, by deleting the word: the last caller went
  \ with CMDSUB-TOKENIZE in Iteration 105, and the two loops are one
  \ loop here. A word that moves words without their flags no longer
  \ exists to be called by accident. FORTH-STYLE.md section 8, which
  \ says every copy carries the flags - now by construction rather
  \ than by remembering.
  \
  \ The zero guard is not optional: CAQ-N can be 0 (an empty pipeline
  \ segment), and this kernel's DO with start = limit runs the entire
  \ unsigned range.
  SHADOW{ CAQ-ARGV CAQ-ARGQ CAQ-N }
  CAQ-N @ ARGC !
  CAQ-N @ 0 > IF
    CAQ-N @ 0 DO
      CAQ-ARGQ @ I +       C@ ARGV-QUOTED I +       C!
      CAQ-ARGV @ I CELLS + @  ARGV        I CELLS + !
    LOOP
  THEN
  0 ARGV CAQ-N @ CELLS + ! ;



\ ==================================================================
\ Moved ahead of RUN-PIPELINE and RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE, both of
\ which now recognise a group as an ordinary command (Iteration 49).
\ ==================================================================

: LINE-IS? ( c-addr u --- f )  \ does the current tokenized ARGV[0]
                                 \ equal this literal, exactly, and
                                 \ unquoted? (a quoted 'if'/'then'/etc
                                 \ is just a literal argument, same
                                 \ reasoning as ARGV-QUOTED elsewhere)
  ARGC @ 0 = IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  0 ARGV-QUOTED@ IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV @ STR0= ;

DEFER RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL


MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: ARGV-GROUP-BODY
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: ARGQ-GROUP-BODY   \ the body's own quoted flags
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: ARGQ-SHIFT        \ likewise for ARGV-SHIFT
\ Carrying each word's quoted flag with the word, rather than leaving
\ ARGV-QUOTED holding whatever a previous tokenization left at that
\ index. Bash attaches flags to the word itself (WORD_DESC); this file
\ keeps parallel arrays, and EVERY stale-flag bug it has had -
\ Iteration 28's vanishing '&&', Iteration 47's literal ';', and the
\ group body's own ';' surviving into a pipeline stage - is a copy
\ that moved words without them. See GOALS.md's notes on Ramey's bash
\ chapter.
VARIABLE ARGC-GROUP-BODY
VARIABLE GROUP-PID

VARIABLE SG-ADDR
VARIABLE SG-LEN

: SPLIT-GROUP ( c-addr u --- f )
  \ ARGV[0] is the opening "(" or "{" (checked by the caller); c-addr u
  \ is the matching closer. Everything between them goes to
  \ ARGV-GROUP-BODY/ARGQ-GROUP-BODY (may be empty, e.g. "( )").
  \ f = 0 and nothing touched if the line ends first - an unterminated
  \ group, not reported as an error, matching this file's sparse error
  \ handling elsewhere.
  \
  \ This replaces SPLIT-GROUP-PAREN and SPLIT-GROUP-BRACE, which were
  \ the same twenty lines differing only in the closing token - and
  \ neither tracked depth, so "( a ( b ) c )" stopped at the INNER
  \ ')'. Counting depth here fixes that as a side effect of merging
  \ them.
  SG-LEN ! SG-ADDR !
  1 PR-I !
  1 GRP-DEPTH !
  0 ARGC-GROUP-BODY !
  BEGIN
    AT-END? IF 0 ELSE
      SG-ADDR @ SG-LEN @ AT-TOKEN? GRP-DEPTH @ 1 = AND 0=
    THEN
  WHILE
    PR-I @ ARGV@ ARGV-GROUP-BODY ARGC-GROUP-BODY @ CELLS + !
    PR-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@ ARGQ-GROUP-BODY ARGC-GROUP-BODY @ + C!
    ARGC-GROUP-BODY @ 1+ ARGC-GROUP-BODY !
    PR-I @ GRP-TRACK
    PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV-GROUP-BODY ARGC-GROUP-BODY @ CELLS + !
  AT-END? IF 0 EXIT THEN
  -1 ;

: SPLIT-GROUP-PAREN ( --- f )  S" )" SPLIT-GROUP ;
: SPLIT-GROUP-BRACE ( --- f )  S" }" SPLIT-GROUP ;

: DO-SUBSHELL ( --- )
  \ SPLIT-GROUP-PAREN has already run (see RUN-TOKENIZED), leaving the
  \ body in ARGV-GROUP-BODY/ARGC-GROUP-BODY. Forks; the child copies
  \ the body into the global ARGV/ARGC (safe - COPY-ARGV-Q inside a
  \ freshly forked child is the same pattern RUN-PIPELINE's own sides
  \ already use) and runs it via RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL, so the body's own
  \ ';'/'&&'/'||'/'|' all work normally, then exits with whatever
  \ LAST-STATUS that left behind. The parent waits and takes the
  \ child's exit status as this command's own.
  FORK DUP GROUP-PID !
  0 = IF
    ARGV-GROUP-BODY ARGQ-GROUP-BODY ARGC-GROUP-BODY @ COPY-ARGV-Q
    RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
    LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT
  THEN
  GROUP-PID @ WAITPID DROP
  8 RSHIFT 255 AND LAST-STATUS ! ;

: DO-BRACE-GROUP ( --- )
  \ Same as DO-SUBSHELL, but no fork - the body runs directly in this
  \ shell, so any cd/variable/export changes inside it persist
  \ afterward. That's the whole reason a brace group exists as a
  \ separate construct from a subshell.
  ARGV-GROUP-BODY ARGQ-GROUP-BODY ARGC-GROUP-BODY @ COPY-ARGV-Q
  RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL ;

DEFER DO-MULTILINE-GROUP-CALL   \ patched to (DO-MULTILINE-GROUP) once
                                \ that and MG-SUB? exist, far below.

: DISPATCH-GROUP ( --- f )
  \ The one place a leading '(' or '{' is turned into a group. Returns
  \ true if it handled the line, in which case the caller must EXIT.
  \
  \ This used to be two verbatim copies, in RUN-TOKENIZED and in
  \ RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE, and they had drifted apart: only the
  \ RUN-TOKENIZED copy carried the ARGC=1 multi-line arm below. So a
  \ group that opened on one line was recognized when it WAS the whole
  \ line, and not when it followed '&&' or '||' - "true && {" ran the
  \ body correctly but left status 127, because the lone '{' segment
  \ fell through to be executed as a command name. Iteration 151 found
  \ the divergence by inspecting the file's largest duplicated block;
  \ 152 merged them, which is the fix.
  \
  \ ARGC=1 means the group opener is the entire line/segment, so the
  \ body arrives on following lines and DO-MULTILINE-GROUP collects
  \ them. Reached through a DEFER because DO-MULTILINE-GROUP needs the
  \ tokenizer and cannot be defined this early.
  ARGC @ 1 = IF
    S" (" LINE-IS? IF -1 DO-MULTILINE-GROUP-CALL -1 EXIT THEN
    S" {" LINE-IS? IF  0 DO-MULTILINE-GROUP-CALL -1 EXIT THEN
  THEN
  \ Otherwise the group closes on this same line. Guarded by
  \ AT-GROUP-END? because if a pipe follows the group this must fall
  \ through to SPLIT-PIPE, or the group runs alone and the rest of the
  \ pipeline is dropped - which is exactly what "(echo hi) | tr a-z
  \ A-Z" did without it.
  AT-GROUP-END? IF
    S" (" LINE-IS? IF SPLIT-GROUP-PAREN IF DO-SUBSHELL THEN -1 EXIT THEN
    S" {" LINE-IS? IF SPLIT-GROUP-BRACE IF DO-BRACE-GROUP THEN -1 EXIT THEN
  THEN
  0 ;

MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: PIPE-PIDS
VARIABLE PL-I
VARIABLE PL-PREV-RD
VARIABLE PL-RD
VARIABLE PL-WR
VARIABLE PL-START
VARIABLE PL-END
VARIABLE PL-J

: PIPE-SEG-TO-ARGV ( --- )
  \ Copy PIPE-ARGV[PL-START .. PL-END) into the global ARGV/ARGC. Only
  \ ever called inside a freshly forked child, so clobbering the global
  \ ARGV is safe - the same reasoning RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD and DO-SUBSHELL
  \ already rely on.
  0 PL-J !
  BEGIN PL-START @ PL-J @ + PL-END @ < WHILE
    PIPE-ARGV PL-START @ PL-J @ + CELLS + @  ARGV PL-J @ CELLS + !
    PIPE-ARGQ PL-START @ PL-J @ + + C@       ARGV-QUOTED PL-J @ + C!
    PL-J @ 1+ PL-J !
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV PL-J @ CELLS + !
  PL-J @ ARGC ! ;

: PIPE-STAGE-INTERNAL? ( --- f )
  \ Does this pipeline stage have to run inside the shell rather than
  \ being exec'd? Groups, compound commands, builtins and functions
  \ all do; anything else is an external command and goes straight to
  \ exec, so an ordinary pipeline still costs no extra process.
  \
  \ It runs in the forked child either way, so a builtin in a pipeline
  \ cannot affect this shell - "printf x | read a" leaves $a unset
  \ here exactly as it does in bash. That is the POSIX behaviour, not
  \ a limitation of running it in the child.
  \ Groups qualify because SPLIT-GROUP already captured their whole
  \ body from the current line. Multi-line compound commands
  \ (if/while/for/case) deliberately do NOT: their body lines are read
  \ from the input *after* the stage starts, and a forked child cannot
  \ do that reliably - it shares the script's file offset with the
  \ parent, so the two race for the same lines. Tried directly and it
  \ misbehaves exactly that way. Making them work means capturing the
  \ body before forking, which is its own piece of work.
  S" ("     LINE-IS? IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" {"     LINE-IS? IF -1 EXIT THEN
  ARGC @ 0 = IF 0 EXIT THEN
  0 ARGV-QUOTED@ IF 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV @ FIND-BUILTIN 0= 0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  ARGV @ FIND-FUNC -1 <> ;


\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ A compound command as the last pipeline stage
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ "printf ... | while read line; do ... done".
\
\ The whole difficulty is buffer lifetime: capturing the body READS
\ LINES, and reading a line overwrites both RAW-LINE-BUF and LINE-BUF
\ - and SPLIT-PIPE's PIPE-ARGV entries are pointers INTO LINE-BUF. So
\ the capture cannot happen after the split (Iteration 79), and the
\ raw text has to be preserved across it too (Iteration 78).
\
\ The order that works: save the raw line, capture the body (freely
\ clobbering everything), restore the line, derive the stage's own raw
\ text, then re-tokenize and let SPLIT-PIPE run on a line that is
\ intact again. All of it in the parent, before any fork - a forked
\ child shares the script's file offset and would race for the body
\ lines (Iteration 57).

LINE-MAX BUFFER: RAW-LINE-BUF
VARIABLE RAW-LINE-LEN

8192     BUFFER: PIPE-BODY
LINE-MAX BUFFER: PIPE-RAW
LINE-MAX BUFFER: PIPE-RAW-FULL
VARIABLE PIPE-BODY-LEN
VARIABLE PIPE-RAW-LEN
VARIABLE PIPE-RAW-FULL-LEN
VARIABLE PIPE-HAVE-BODY
VARIABLE PLS-I

DEFER PREPARE-PIPE-CALL

VARIABLE REPLAY-ACTIVE?
VARIABLE REPLAY-SRC
VARIABLE REPLAY-ARENA?
VARIABLE REPLAY-LEN
VARIABLE REPLAY-POS

: RUN-PIPELINE ( --- )
  \ Runs PIPE-NSEG segments connected by PIPE-NSEG-1 pipes. The old
  \ version handled exactly one pipe; this is the same shape
  \ generalized to a loop carrying one file descriptor forward.
  \
  \ Each iteration: create this segment's outgoing pipe (unless it is
  \ the last), fork, and in the child wire the previous segment's read
  \ end onto stdin and this pipe's write end onto stdout. The parent
  \ closes both ends it no longer needs - which is what makes each
  \ stage actually see EOF - and carries the read end forward.
  \
  \ Builtins still aren't supported in a pipeline: every segment runs
  \ via RUN-CHILD, the same scope limit as before.
  SHADOW{ | PL-I PL-PREV-RD PL-RD PL-WR PL-START PL-END PL-J }
  -1 PL-PREV-RD !
  BEGIN PL-I @ PIPE-NSEG @ < WHILE
    PL-START @ PL-END !
    BEGIN
      PL-END @ PIPE-ARGC @ < IF PL-END @ PIPE-AT-BAR? 0= ELSE 0 THEN
    WHILE
      PL-END @ 1+ PL-END !
    REPEAT
    PL-I @ 1+ PIPE-NSEG @ < IF
      PIPE DROP PL-WR ! PL-RD !
    ELSE
      -1 PL-RD !  -1 PL-WR !
    THEN
    FORK
    DUP 0 = IF
      DROP
      PL-PREV-RD @ 0 < 0= IF
        PL-PREV-RD @ 0 DUP2 DROP
        PL-PREV-RD @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
      THEN
      PL-WR @ 0 < 0= IF
        PL-WR @ 1 DUP2 DROP
        PL-WR @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
        PL-RD @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
      THEN
      PIPE-SEG-TO-ARGV
      \ Redirections on this stage are parsed and applied here, AFTER
      \ the pipe's own dup2s, so an explicit "> file" on a stage
      \ overrides the pipe as POSIX requires. This retires the
      \ "pipes and redirection are mutually exclusive" limit recorded
      \ in Iteration 7: stages used to go straight to RUN-CHILD with
      \ their redirection tokens still in ARGV, where the command saw
      \ them as ordinary arguments.
      PARSE-REDIRECTIONS
      APPLY-REDIRECTIONS
      PIPE-HAVE-BODY @ PL-I @ 1+ PIPE-NSEG @ = AND IF
        PIPE-RAW RAW-LINE-BUF PIPE-RAW-LEN @ MOVE
        PIPE-RAW-LEN @ RAW-LINE-LEN !
        PIPE-BODY REPLAY-SRC !  0 REPLAY-ARENA? !  -1 REPLAY-ACTIVE? !
        PIPE-BODY-LEN @ REPLAY-LEN !  0 REPLAY-POS !
        RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
        LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT
      THEN
      PIPE-STAGE-INTERNAL? IF
        RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
        LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT
      THEN
      RUN-CHILD
      127 SYS-EXIT
    THEN
    PIPE-PIDS PL-I @ CELLS + !
    PL-PREV-RD @ 0 < 0= IF PL-PREV-RD @ CLOSE-FILE DROP THEN
    PL-WR @ 0 < 0= IF PL-WR @ CLOSE-FILE DROP THEN
    PL-RD @ PL-PREV-RD !
    PL-END @ 1+ PL-START !
    PL-I @ 1+ PL-I !
  REPEAT
  \ Reap every child, not just the last: leaving them unreaped would
  \ accumulate zombies for the life of the shell. Only the last
  \ segment's status becomes the pipeline's, matching POSIX (a real
  \ shell also exposes the rest via PIPESTATUS; not done here).
  0 PL-I !
  BEGIN PL-I @ PIPE-NSEG @ < WHILE
    PIPE-PIDS PL-I @ CELLS + @ WAITPID DROP
    PL-I @ 1+ PIPE-NSEG @ < IF DROP ELSE 8 RSHIFT 255 AND LAST-STATUS ! THEN
    PL-I @ 1+ PL-I !
  REPEAT ;

\ ==================================================================
\ '&&'/'||' - a pipeline runs only if the operator connecting it to
\ the previous one permits it, based on the previous pipeline's own
\ exit status: '&&' requires success (0), '||' requires failure
\ (nonzero). Left-associative, evaluated left to right, same
\ precedence for both (POSIX gives them equal, left-to-right
\ precedence, tighter than ';' but looser than '|' - RUN-TOKENIZED
\ calls SPLIT-ANDOR only after ';' and the assignment-word check have
\ already ruled themselves out, and RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE below,
\ which SPLIT-PIPE's own '|' handling lives inside of, is what each
\ &&/||-separated piece is run through). Same whitespace-delimited-
\ token convention '|'/';' already have - a quoted '&&' or '||' is a
\ literal argument, not an operator (AT-AND?/AT-OR? both check
\ ARGV-QUOTED@, same as AT-PIPE?/AT-SEMI?).
\ ==================================================================

MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: ARGV-AO-LEFT
MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: ARGV-AO-REST
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: ARGQ-AO-LEFT
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: ARGQ-AO-REST
VARIABLE ARGC-AO-LEFT
VARIABLE ARGC-AO-REST
VARIABLE ANDOR-OP        ( 1 = && was found, 2 = || was found )
VARIABLE AO-PENDING-OP   \ which of the above applies to the NEXT
                          \ piece about to be considered
VARIABLE AO-CONTINUE

: AT-AND? ( --- f )  S" &&" AT-OP? ;

: AT-OR? ( --- f )  S" ||" AT-OP? ;

: SPLIT-ANDOR ( --- f )  \ f = -1 if a && or || token was found in the
                          \ *global* ARGV/ARGC (not touched by this
                          \ word itself, only scanned) - the piece
                          \ before it is in ARGV-AO-LEFT/ARGC-AO-LEFT
                          \ (each entry's own ARGV-QUOTED flag at split
                          \ time mirrored into ARGQ-AO-LEFT, for
                          \ COPY-ARGV-Q to restore later - see its own
                          \ comment for why this matters), which
                          \ operator it was in ANDOR-OP, and everything
                          \ after it (which may itself contain further
                          \ &&/||, not yet split) in ARGV-AO-REST/
                          \ ARGC-AO-REST/ARGQ-AO-REST. f = 0 if
                          \ neither was found at all (nothing touched
                          \ except ARGC-AO-LEFT, harmlessly reset).
  SHADOW{ | PR-I }
  0 GRP-DEPTH !
  0 ARGC-AO-LEFT !
  BEGIN AT-END? AT-AND? OR AT-OR? OR 0= WHILE
    PR-I @ GRP-TRACK
    PR-I @ ARGV@ ARGV-AO-LEFT ARGC-AO-LEFT @ CELLS + !
    PR-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@ ARGQ-AO-LEFT ARGC-AO-LEFT @ + C!
    ARGC-AO-LEFT @ 1+ ARGC-AO-LEFT !
    PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV-AO-LEFT ARGC-AO-LEFT @ CELLS + !
  AT-END? IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AT-AND? IF 1 ANDOR-OP ! ELSE 2 ANDOR-OP ! THEN
  PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !                    ( skip the "&&"/"||" token itself )
  0 ARGC-AO-REST !
  BEGIN AT-END? 0= WHILE
    PR-I @ ARGV@ ARGV-AO-REST ARGC-AO-REST @ CELLS + !
    PR-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@ ARGQ-AO-REST ARGC-AO-REST @ + C!
    ARGC-AO-REST @ 1+ ARGC-AO-REST !
    PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV-AO-REST ARGC-AO-REST @ CELLS + !
  -1 ;

: AO-SHOULD-RUN? ( --- f )  \ does AO-PENDING-OP (the operator right
                              \ before the piece about to be
                              \ considered) permit running it, given
                              \ the previous piece's own LAST-STATUS?
  AO-PENDING-OP @ 0 = IF -1 EXIT THEN
  AO-PENDING-OP @ 1 = IF LAST-STATUS @ 0= EXIT THEN
  LAST-STATUS @ 0 <> ;

DEFER TRY-ASSIGNMENT-CALL   \ patched to TRY-ASSIGNMENT's own xt right
                               \ after it's defined, further down (the
                               \ same deferred-word pattern already
                               \ used for RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL) - needed
                               \ since RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE is
                               \ defined long before ASSIGNMENT-EQPOS/
                               \ DO-ASSIGN are. A standalone assignment
                               \ has to be checked *here*, not only in
                               \ RUN-TOKENIZED's own fall-through,
                               \ since RUN-AND-OR-CHAIN calls this word
                               \ directly for each &&/||-separated
                               \ piece, bypassing RUN-TOKENIZED's own
                               \ checks entirely - found as a real bug
                               \ by testing directly: "x=hello &&
                               \ echo $x" silently failed, since
                               \ "x=hello" was never recognized as an
                               \ assignment at all inside the chain,
                               \ just path-searched as a literal,
                               \ failing command name.


\ Moved ahead of RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE, which now uses both for
\ pipeline negation ( ! cmd ).

MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: ARGV-SHIFT
VARIABLE ARGC-SHIFT

: SHIFT-ARGV-DOWN ( --- )  \ copies ARGV[1..ARGC-1] into
                            \ ARGV-SHIFT[0..] - "the rest of this
                            \ line" as its own command, used to run
                            \ if's condition (the part after "if").
  0 ARGC-SHIFT !
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    ARGC @ 1 DO
      I ARGV@ ARGV-SHIFT ARGC-SHIFT @ CELLS + !
      I ARGV-QUOTED@ ARGQ-SHIFT ARGC-SHIFT @ + C!
      ARGC-SHIFT @ 1+ ARGC-SHIFT !
    LOOP
  THEN
  0 ARGV-SHIFT ARGC-SHIFT @ CELLS + ! ;

: RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE ( --- )  \ runs whatever's currently in the
                                    \ global ARGV/ARGC as one pipeline
                                    \ (or simple command) - the logic
                                    \ that used to be inline at the end
                                    \ of RUN-TOKENIZED, factored out
                                    \ here so RUN-AND-OR-CHAIN below
                                    \ can call it once per &&/||-
                                    \ separated piece. A no-op if
                                    \ SUPPRESS-EXEC? is set - this line
                                    \ is being read only to correctly
                                    \ consume a skipped if/while
                                    \ branch's own body, not actually
                                    \ run. Also a no-op once
                                    \ RETURN-PENDING? is set - once a
                                    \ "return" builtin has run within
                                    \ the current function, nothing
                                    \ further in that same logical line
                                    \ (or any later line of that
                                    \ function's own body) should run
                                    \ either, regardless of ';'/'&&'/
                                    \ '||' structure. Likewise once
                                    \ LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? is set (a
                                    \ "break"/"continue" has run) -
                                    \ nothing further in the current
                                    \ loop iteration's own body (or the
                                    \ body of any function called from
                                    \ within it) should run either.
  \ PREPARE-PIPE-CALL first, because it may re-tokenize the whole line
  \ from raw text - and then expand, once. It used to sit further
  \ down, which was fine while tokenizing and expanding were the same
  \ act; now it would throw away an expanded ARGV and leave a raw one
  \ in its place.
  PREPARE-PIPE-CALL
  \ Expansion happens HERE, not during tokenizing: this is the point
  \ at which one command is about to run, and everything earlier on
  \ its line already has. That is what makes
  \ `FOO=bar; echo $FOO` print bar (Iteration 114).
  \
  \ Exactly once per ARGV. Expanding an already-expanded list would
  \ re-split its results and, since the source and destination buffers
  \ would then be the same one, corrupt them outright. That is why the
  \ call sits below the group handling rather than above it: a group's
  \ body is carved out of ARGV and then run through RUN-TOKENIZED,
  \ which expands it at its own leaf.
  SUPPRESS-EXEC? @ RETURN-PENDING? @ OR LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? @ OR
  IF EXIT THEN
  \ A trailing unquoted '&' runs the command in the background: fork,
  \ do not wait, report success immediately, and remember the child's
  \ pid for $!. The '&' itself is dropped first, so everything below
  \ sees an ordinary command - the child then runs it by recursing
  \ into this same word.
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    ARGC @ 1- DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ 0= SWAP ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" &" STR= AND IF
      ARGC @ 1- ARGC !
      0 ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + !
      FORK DUP 0 = IF
        DROP
        RECURSE
        LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT
      THEN
      BG-PID !
      0 LAST-STATUS !
      EXIT
    THEN
  THEN
  \ A '&&'/'||'/';' segment, or a pipeline stage, can itself be a
  \ group - "[ x ] || { echo fail; exit 1; }". Checked here as well as
  \ at the line level, since segments never pass through RUN-TOKENIZED.
  DISPATCH-GROUP IF EXIT THEN
  EXPAND-WORDS
  TRY-ASSIGNMENT-CALL IF EXIT THEN
  S" !" LINE-IS? IF
    \ Pipeline negation: run the rest and invert its status. POSIX
    \ negates the whole pipeline, which is why this sits here - ahead
    \ of SPLIT-PIPE - rather than being treated as a command name.
    SHIFT-ARGV-DOWN
    ARGV-SHIFT ARGQ-SHIFT ARGC-SHIFT @ COPY-ARGV-Q
    RECURSE
    LAST-STATUS @ 0= IF 1 ELSE 0 THEN LAST-STATUS !
    EXIT
  THEN
  SPLIT-PIPE
  PIPE-PRESENT? @ IF
    PIPE-SEGMENTS-OK? IF RUN-PIPELINE THEN
  ELSE
    PARSE-REDIRECTIONS
    ARGC @ 0 > IF
      DISPATCH 0= IF RUN-EXTERNAL THEN
    THEN
  THEN ;

VARIABLE AO-REST
VARIABLE AO-RESTQ
VARIABLE AO-RESTC
VARIABLE AO-SAVED-OP
VARIABLE AO-J

: AO-SAVE-REST ( --- )
  \ Copy the &&/||-remainder out of the shared ARGV-AO-REST arrays
  \ into per-invocation arena storage BEFORE running the piece before
  \ it. Running that piece can recurse all the way back into
  \ SPLIT-ANDOR - a group whose body contains "&&" does exactly that -
  \ which overwrites the remainder. Iteration 50 fixed the identical
  \ shape in RUN-TOKENIZED for ';' and recorded that this word had it
  \ too and had simply never been hit; it is hit by
  \ "{ true && echo inner; } && echo outer", which printed
  \ "inner inner".
  ARGC-AO-REST @ AO-RESTC !
  MAX-ARGS CELLS BODY-ALLOC AO-REST !
  MAX-ARGS       BODY-ALLOC AO-RESTQ !
  0 AO-J !
  BEGIN AO-J @ AO-RESTC @ < WHILE
    ARGV-AO-REST AO-J @ CELLS + @  AO-REST  @ BODY@ AO-J @ CELLS + !
    ARGQ-AO-REST AO-J @ +      C@  AO-RESTQ @ BODY@ AO-J @ +       C!
    AO-J @ 1+ AO-J !
  REPEAT ;

: AO-LOAD-REST ( --- )
  AO-REST @ BODY@ AO-RESTQ @ BODY@ AO-RESTC @ COPY-ARGV-Q ;

: RUN-AND-OR-CHAIN ( --- )
  \ AO-CONTINUE, AO-PENDING-OP and the arena pointers are locals, not
  \ globals: every one of them is held across RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE,
  \ which can recurse into this same word.
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @
  SHADOW{ BODY-ARENA-TOP | AO-REST AO-RESTQ AO-RESTC AO-J AO-CONTINUE AO-PENDING-OP AO-SAVED-OP }
  AO-SAVE-REST
  ANDOR-OP @ AO-SAVED-OP !
  ARGV-AO-LEFT ARGQ-AO-LEFT ARGC-AO-LEFT @ COPY-ARGV-Q
  RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE
  AO-SAVED-OP @ AO-PENDING-OP !
  AO-LOAD-REST
  -1 AO-CONTINUE !
  BEGIN AO-CONTINUE @ WHILE
    SPLIT-ANDOR IF
      AO-SAVE-REST
      ANDOR-OP @ AO-SAVED-OP !
      AO-SHOULD-RUN? IF
        ARGV-AO-LEFT ARGQ-AO-LEFT ARGC-AO-LEFT @ COPY-ARGV-Q
        RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE
      THEN
      AO-SAVED-OP @ AO-PENDING-OP !
      AO-LOAD-REST
    ELSE
      AO-SHOULD-RUN? IF RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE THEN
      0 AO-CONTINUE !
    THEN
  REPEAT ;


\ ==================================================================
\ Control structures: if/then/else/fi
\ Deliberately minimal for v0.5: no nesting support (a body containing
\ another if will misbehave - see PROGRESS.md), and then/else/fi must
\ each be alone on their own line (no ";"-separated forms, since there
\ is no ";" support at all yet). The condition is whatever follows
\ "if" on the same line - run via the normal command path, including
\ its own pipe/redirection handling. then/else bodies are read one
\ line at a time directly from stdin and executed immediately as
\ they're read, not buffered and re-scanned - this works for if
\ precisely because each body line only ever needs running once,
\ unlike a loop body (which is why while/for aren't attempted here -
\ they'd need a genuinely different, buffer-and-replay approach).
\
\ RUN-TOKENIZED (below) needs to detect "if" and hand off to DO-IF,
\ but DO-IF needs to run its own body lines through the same "act on
\ tokenized ARGV" logic RUN-TOKENIZED provides - a genuine mutual
\ dependency. Broken via a small deferred-word indirection
\ (RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL, patched to RUN-TOKENIZED's own execution token
\ once that word is defined below) rather than trying to reorder
\ around it.
\ ==================================================================


\ ==================================================================
\ '( list )' subshells and '{ list ; }' brace groups. Both require
\ whitespace around '(', ')', '{', '}' (this shell's established
\ convention for every operator so far - '|', ';', '&&', '||' all
\ need it too - though real POSIX shells don't require it around '('
\ specifically; a real gap against mrsh's own tests, which write
\ "(cmd)" with no spaces, but a deliberate, consistent scope choice
\ here rather than a special-cased tokenizer change). A subshell
\ forks: the body runs in a child process, so cd/variable/export
\ changes inside it don't affect this shell, and the parent takes the
\ child's exit status as its own (same FORK/WAITPID shape
\ RUN-PIPELINE already uses). A brace group runs its body directly in
\ this shell instead - no fork - so changes inside it persist
\ afterward, which is the entire reason it exists as a distinct
\ construct from a subshell. Neither supports nesting (a body
\ containing another '(' or '{' will misbehave - the same "no nesting"
\ scope limit if/while already have, and for the same reason: this
\ shell doesn't yet have real per-invocation state for control
\ structures, only shared globals). Detected by ARGV[0] alone (reusing
\ LINE-IS?, exactly as if/while already are) - this check runs *before*
\ ';'/'&&'/'||' splitting, since a group's own ';'/'&&'/'||' (used
\ inside its body) must stay scoped to that body rather than being
\ split as if it belonged to the outer line.
\ ==================================================================



\ ==================================================================
\ Shell-local variable assignment: a standalone "NAME=value" line
\ (the *entire* line - not a command with an assignment-looking
\ argument, and not "NAME=value command args..." either, which POSIX
\ treats as a *temporary* assignment scoped to just that one command's
\ execution - not implemented yet, a real, deliberate scope limit for
\ now) sets a shell-local variable via SET-SHVAR rather than being
\ looked up as a command name.
\ ==================================================================


: TRY-ASSIGNMENT ( --- f )  \ f = -1 if ARGV was a standalone
                              \ "NAME=value" assignment word (already
                              \ handled via DO-ASSIGN by the time this
                              \ returns), 0 otherwise (ARGV untouched).
  \ Checks ARGV-NAME-QUOTED, not ARGV-QUOTED - the latter records
  \ whether *any part* of the token was quoted, not whether the name
  \ portion specifically was, so x="value" (a very common, valid
  \ assignment - only the value is quoted) would otherwise be wrongly
  \ rejected just because SOME part of the token happened to go
  \ through a quote. A real, pre-existing bug found while testing IFS
  \ field splitting with a quoted, multi-word assignment value, but
  \ present already in the last committed version too, unrelated to
  \ that feature itself.
  ARGC @ 1 = 0 ARGV-NAME-QUOTED@ 0= AND IF
    ARGV @ ASSIGNMENT-EQPOS
    DUP -1 <> IF DO-ASSIGN -1 EXIT THEN
    DROP
  THEN
  0 ;
' TRY-ASSIGNMENT IS TRY-ASSIGNMENT-CALL


: RUN-SHIFTED ( --- )  \ runs ARGV-SHIFT/ARGC-SHIFT the same way a
                        \ normal top-level line would be
  ARGV-SHIFT ARGQ-SHIFT ARGC-SHIFT @ COPY-ARGV-Q
  RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL ;



VARIABLE SHFILE-FID
VARIABLE SHFILE-ACTIVE?   \ true while running via SH-FILE, so
                            \ READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE below knows to read
                            \ from SHFILE-FID rather than the real
                            \ process stdin - if/while's own internal
                            \ body-line reading (READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV)
                            \ goes through that same word, so this is
                            \ what makes if/while work at all when a
                            \ script is run via `relfsh script.sh`
                            \ rather than piped into stdin - found and
                            \ fixed as a real, pre-existing bug:
                            \ without this, DO-IF/DO-WHILE always read
                            \ from the real stdin regardless of where
                            \ the script actually came from, so "if"'s
                            \ own "then"/body/"fi" lines were never
                            \ seen at all when invoked this way, and
                            \ the whole command was silently a no-op.



\ ==================================================================
\ Replaying a stored body as an INPUT SOURCE
\ ==================================================================
\ A while/for body and a function body are both stored as raw,
\ NUL-separated lines and replayed later. Replay used to dispatch each
\ stored line directly (RUN-STORED-LINE), which meant a nested
\ multi-line construct inside such a body did not work at all: DO-IF's
\ own search for "then"/"fi" reads a NEW line, and that read went to
\ the real process input rather than to the next stored line. So the
\ nested if's body ran unconditionally and its "fi" was never seen -
\ the limitation carried since Iteration 23 and since noted against
\ three separate features.
\
\ The fix is to make replay a third INPUT SOURCE, alongside the real
\ stdin and an open script file, rather than a separate dispatch path.
\ Everything that reads a line already funnels through
\ READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE, so once replay is one of its cases, every
\ construct nested inside a body reads from the body automatically,
\ with no construct needing to know about replay at all.
\
\ REPLAY-SRC/LEN/POS are declared as locals by each replaying word, so
\ replays nest: an inner body's replay saves and restores the outer
\ one's position, and a construct that CAPTURES lines while a replay
\ is active (a nested while reading its own body, say) consumes them
\ from the outer body and leaves the position correctly advanced.

\ Whether a replay is in progress. A separate flag rather than
\ "REPLAY-SRC is non-zero", because REPLAY-SRC became an arena OFFSET
\ and the very first allocation legitimately has offset 0 - so the
\ outermost loop in a script silently did not replay at all.
\ True when REPLAY-SRC is an OFFSET into the body arena (a while/for
\ body), false when it is a plain address (a function body, which
\ lives in a function's own heap block). The distinction is needed
\ because the arena can be RESIZEd - and so relocated - part way
\ through a replay: a nested loop inside the body being replayed
\ allocates its own storage before it reads a single line. Resolving
\ the address per line rather than caching it is what makes that safe.
VARIABLE RNL-OFF
VARIABLE RNL-BODY
VARIABLE RNL-CUR
VARIABLE RNL-LEN

VARIABLE INPUT-EOF?
\ True when the current input source has genuinely run out, as opposed
\ to having produced an empty line - the two are reported identically
\ by READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE's own u2, which is why every capture loop in
\ this file used to spin forever on an unterminated construct rather
\ than diagnosing one (Iteration 107).
\
\ Only the two sources that can actually tell set it: a replay knows
\ its own length, and READ-LINE returns a flag. ACCEPT cannot
\ distinguish them, so the interactive path is left alone - a real
\ shell prompts for continuation there indefinitely too.

: REPLAY-NEXT-LINE ( off body --- u2 )
  SHADOW{ RNL-OFF RNL-BODY | RNL-CUR RNL-LEN }
  REPLAY-POS @ REPLAY-LEN @ < 0= IF -1 INPUT-EOF? ! 0 EXIT THEN
  REPLAY-ARENA? @ IF REPLAY-SRC @ BODY@ ELSE REPLAY-SRC @ THEN
  REPLAY-POS @ + RNL-CUR !
  RNL-CUR @ CSTRLEN RNL-LEN !
  REPLAY-POS @ RNL-LEN @ + 1+ REPLAY-POS !
  RNL-OFF @ RNL-LEN @ + 1+ RNL-BODY @ BUF-ENSURE
  RNL-CUR @ RNL-BODY @ @ RNL-OFF @ + RNL-LEN @ MOVE
  RNL-LEN @ ;

: READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE ( off body --- u2 )
  \ Reads one line of "current input" - from the real process stdin
  \ via ACCEPT (preserving its backspace/delete editing, which matters
  \ for a genuinely interactive session) when not running via
  \ SH-FILE, or from the open script file via READ-LINE (discarding
  \ its own flag/ior - EOF just gives u2=0, the same as running out
  \ of lines already meant here) when SHFILE-ACTIVE? is true.
  0 INPUT-EOF? !  0 LONG-LINE? !
  REPLAY-ACTIVE? @ IF REPLAY-NEXT-LINE EXIT THEN
  SHFILE-ACTIVE? @ IF
    SHFILE-FID @ READ-LINE-INTO        ( u2 flag )
    0= IF -1 INPUT-EOF? ! THEN
  ELSE
    ACCEPT-INTO
  THEN ;

' READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE IS READ-INPUT-CALL

\ ==================================================================
\ Operator normalization: ';', '|', '&', '<', '>' (and their doubled
\ forms '&&', '||', '>>') are, in real POSIX shells, self-delimiting
\ operator characters - "true;echo" and "a>file" parse correctly with
\ no whitespace needed around the operator at all. Every operator
\ this shell has implemented so far (Iterations 7, 18, 19) instead
\ took the shortcut of requiring surrounding whitespace, a deliberate,
\ consistently-documented scope limit - but re-reading mrsh's own test
\ files directly showed this blocking real progress: "if true; then",
\ "( echo hi )" written as "(echo hi)", and similar fused-operator
\ style are the norm in real scripts, not the exception.
\
\ Rather than rewrite SCAN-TOKEN/TOKENIZE's own in-place compaction
\ logic to recognize operators mid-scan (which turned out to risk a
\ real hazard: an unquoted word's NUL-termination write lands exactly
\ where a following fused operator character would sit, destroying it
\ before it could ever be read as its own token), this runs as a
\ pre-pass over the raw line text, inserting a literal space before
\ and after every *unquoted* operator character found (tracking
\ single/double quote state so an operator character *inside* a quote
\ is left untouched - quoted operators are literal text, not
\ operators, matching real shells) - "true;echo" becomes "true ; echo"
\ before TOKENIZE (entirely unmodified) ever sees it. Re-normalizing
\ an already-spaced-out operator is harmless (just adds a redundant
\ extra space either side, which SKIP-WS already tolerates fine) -
\ relevant since a while loop's condition gets re-tokenized fresh every
\ iteration, and would otherwise go through this twice.
\
\ Deliberately out of scope for now: '(', ')', '{', '}'. Unlike the
\ five operators above, blindly spacing every unquoted paren/brace
\ would break $(...) command substitution outright - EXPAND-VAR's own
\ detection needs "$(" with no space in between - and doing this
\ correctly needs tracking "am I currently inside a $(...) construct"
\ during this same pre-pass, which is real additional complexity left
\ for its own future iteration rather than risking this one.
\ ==================================================================

: OP-CHAR? ( c --- f )
  DUP 59 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN    ( ; )
  DUP 124 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN   ( | )
  DUP 38 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN    ( & )
  DUP 60 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN    ( < )
  DUP 62 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN    ( > )
  DUP 40 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN    ( ( )
  DUP 41 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN    \ )  - a backslash comment: "( ) )"
                                   \ relied on PARSE skipping a leading )
  DROP 0 ;

: NORM-DOUBLED-OP? ( c --- f )  \ one of &, |, >, ; - the four
                                  \ operator characters with a
                                  \ two-character doubled form (&&,
                                  \ ||, >>, ;; - the last being case's
                                  \ own "end of arm" marker, needed as
                                  \ its own single token rather than
                                  \ two separate ';' ones)
  DUP 38 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP 124 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP 62 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP 60 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN    ( < , for "<<" here-documents )
  59 = ;

VARIABLE NORM-LEN
VARIABLE NORM-I
VARIABLE NORM-U
VARIABLE NORM-IN-SQ?
VARIABLE NORM-IN-DQ?
VARIABLE NORM-C
VARIABLE UNTERMINATED-QUOTE?
VARIABLE UNTERMINATED-CMDSUB?  \ a "$(" or backquote still open at end
                                \ of line - the command text continues
                                \ on the next one, exactly as an open
                                \ quote does (Iteration 105)
VARIABLE NORM-IN-CMDSUB?   \ true while copying through a $(...)
                            \ region verbatim. '(' and ')' are
                            \ self-delimiting operators everywhere
                            \ else now, but inside a command
                            \ substitution they are part of the
                            \ substituted text - and "$(" must stay
                            \ fused for EXPAND-VAR to recognise it at
                            \ all. Same shape as NORM-IN-ARITH? below.
VARIABLE NORM-IN-BQ?
VARIABLE NORM-CMDSUB-DEPTH \ nested "(" inside the substitution, so a
                            \ "(" in the command text is not mistaken
                            \ for its closing ")"
VARIABLE NORM-IN-ARITH?    \ true while copying through a $((...))
                            \ region verbatim - operators like <, >,
                            \ &, | are meaningful to the arithmetic
                            \ grammar itself (e.g. "<=", "&&") and
                            \ must not get shell-level spacing
                            \ inserted around them, the same reason
                            \ quoted regions are already copied
                            \ through untouched rather than normalized.
VARIABLE NORM-ARITH-DEPTH  \ how many extra, unmatched "(" are open
                            \ within the arithmetic expression itself,
                            \ so a nested "(...)" inside it isn't
                            \ mistaken for the expansion's own closing
                            \ "))" - mirrors EXPAND-ARITH's own,
                            \ separate depth tracking exactly, since
                            \ this is the same problem solved once
                            \ more, earlier in the pipeline.

: NORM-COMMENT-START? ( --- f )
  \ True if the '#' at the current position begins a comment: POSIX
  \ says '#' introduces one only at the start of a word, so it must be
  \ the first character of the line or follow whitespace. That is
  \ exactly what keeps "$#", "${#VAR}", "${VAR#pat}" and a literal
  \ "a#b" working - in all of those the '#' follows a non-blank.
  NORM-LEN @ 0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  NORM-BUF NORM-LEN @ 1- + C@ WS? ;

: NORM-CLOSE ( --- )
  \ Ends the word currently being emitted, if any.
  \
  \ Word boundaries are recorded HERE, by the pass that already knows
  \ where they are. NORMALIZE-OPERATORS tracks single quotes, double
  \ quotes, $(...), backquotes and $((...)) because it must not space
  \ out operators inside them - which is the same question as "does
  \ this whitespace separate two words". Deriving it a second time in
  \ the tokenizer would be two answers to one question, and
  \ FORTH-STYLE.md section 11 is about what happens next.
  NORM-WSTART @ -1 = IF EXIT THEN
  NORM-WCOUNT @ MAX-ARGS 1- < IF
    NORM-WSTART @                    NORM-WORDS NORM-WCOUNT @ CELLS + !
    NORM-LEN @ NORM-WSTART @ -       NORM-WLENS NORM-WCOUNT @ CELLS + !
    NORM-WCOUNT @ 1+ NORM-WCOUNT !
  ELSE
    \ This, not the tokenizer, is where a too-long line actually loses
    \ its words: NORM-WORDS is MAX-ARGS cells, so NORM-WCOUNT is
    \ already capped by the time TOKENIZE-RAW reads it, and a check
    \ there can never see the overflow.
    -1 ARGS-OVERFLOW? !
  THEN
  -1 NORM-WSTART ! ;

: NORM-EMIT ( c --- )
  NORM-LEN @ BUFFER-SIZE NORM-BUF 1- < IF
    \ A word opens at the first non-blank emitted while none is open.
    \ Blanks never open one, so the separators - both the line's own
    \ and the ones inserted around an operator - fall between words
    \ rather than starting a new one. A blank INSIDE a word (quoted,
    \ or within a $(...)) arrives with the word already open and
    \ leaves it open, which is exactly right.
    DUP WS? 0= NORM-WSTART @ -1 = AND IF NORM-LEN @ NORM-WSTART ! THEN
    NORM-BUF NORM-LEN @ + C!
    NORM-LEN @ 1+ NORM-LEN !
  ELSE
    DROP        ( buffer full - the character was being left on the
                  data stack until Iteration 112 )
  THEN ;

: NORM-PEEK ( offset --- c )  \ byte at LINE-BUF[NORM-I + offset], or
                                \ 0 if that would be beyond NORM-U
  NORM-I @ + DUP NORM-U @ < IF
    LINE-BUF SWAP + C@
  ELSE
    DROP 0
  THEN ;

: NORM-AT-CMDSUB-START? ( --- f )  \ "$(" but not "$((" ?
  NORM-C @ 36 = 1 NORM-PEEK 40 = AND 2 NORM-PEEK 40 <> AND ;

: NORM-AT-ARITH-START? ( --- f )  \ is "$((" starting right at the
                                     \ current position?
  NORM-C @ 36 = 1 NORM-PEEK 40 = AND 2 NORM-PEEK 40 = AND ;

VARIABLE NORM-SKIPD

: NORM-SKIP-CMDSUB ( --- )
  \ Copies a "$(...)" region verbatim from INSIDE double quotes,
  \ counting parens. The double-quote branch below does not otherwise
  \ recognise a command substitution, so the quotes inside one were
  \ taken for the enclosing quote's closer: `"cmd: $(echo "a b")"`
  \ left this pass believing the string ended after `echo `.
  \
  \ That was harmless while nothing acted on where a word ended.
  \ Iteration 112 made this pass the authority on that, and the
  \ boundary landed in the middle of the substitution.
  NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
  1 NORM-PEEK NORM-EMIT
  NORM-I @ 2 + NORM-I !
  0 NORM-SKIPD !
  BEGIN
    NORM-I @ NORM-U @ < IF
      0 NORM-PEEK 41 = NORM-SKIPD @ 0= AND 0=
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    0 NORM-PEEK 40 = IF NORM-SKIPD @ 1+ NORM-SKIPD ! THEN
    0 NORM-PEEK 41 = IF NORM-SKIPD @ 1- NORM-SKIPD ! THEN
    0 NORM-PEEK NORM-EMIT
    NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
  REPEAT
  NORM-I @ NORM-U @ < IF
    41 NORM-EMIT
    NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
  THEN ;

: NORM-SKIP-BQ ( --- )
  \ The same for a backquoted region inside double quotes.
  NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
  NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
  BEGIN
    NORM-I @ NORM-U @ < IF 0 NORM-PEEK 96 <> ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    0 NORM-PEEK NORM-EMIT
    NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
  REPEAT
  NORM-I @ NORM-U @ < IF
    96 NORM-EMIT
    NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
  THEN ;

: NORMALIZE-OPERATORS ( u --- u2 )  \ LINE-BUF holds u raw bytes;
                                      \ result (u2 bytes) is in
                                      \ NORM-BUF, not LINE-BUF itself -
                                      \ the caller copies it back.
  SHADOW{ NORM-U | NORM-LEN NORM-IN-SQ? NORM-IN-DQ? NORM-IN-ARITH? NORM-ARITH-DEPTH NORM-IN-BQ? NORM-IN-CMDSUB? NORM-CMDSUB-DEPTH NORM-I NORM-C }
  0 NORM-WCOUNT !  -1 NORM-WSTART !
  \ Spacing the operators at most triples the text.
  NORM-U @ 3 * 8 + ENSURE-BUFFER NORM-BUF
  BEGIN NORM-I @ NORM-U @ < WHILE
    0 NORM-PEEK NORM-C !
    NORM-IN-ARITH? @ IF
      NORM-C @ 40 = IF NORM-ARITH-DEPTH @ 1+ NORM-ARITH-DEPTH ! THEN
      NORM-C @ 41 = NORM-ARITH-DEPTH @ 0 = AND 1 NORM-PEEK 41 = AND IF
        \ The closing "))" is consumed as a UNIT. Leaving arith-mode on
        \ the first ')' and letting the second fall through was
        \ harmless until Iteration 48 made ')' a self-delimiting
        \ operator - after which the second one got a space inserted
        \ before it, turning "$((x*2))" into "$((x*2) )" and breaking
        \ every arithmetic expansion. Found by run-arith.
        0 NORM-IN-ARITH? !
        41 NORM-EMIT  41 NORM-EMIT
        NORM-I @ 2 + NORM-I !
      ELSE
        NORM-C @ 41 = NORM-ARITH-DEPTH @ 0 > AND IF
          NORM-ARITH-DEPTH @ 1- NORM-ARITH-DEPTH !
        THEN
        NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
        NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
      THEN
    ELSE NORM-IN-BQ? @ IF
      NORM-C @ 96 = IF 0 NORM-IN-BQ? ! THEN
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
    ELSE NORM-IN-CMDSUB? @ IF
      NORM-C @ 40 = IF NORM-CMDSUB-DEPTH @ 1+ NORM-CMDSUB-DEPTH ! THEN
      NORM-C @ 41 = IF
        NORM-CMDSUB-DEPTH @ 0 > IF
          NORM-CMDSUB-DEPTH @ 1- NORM-CMDSUB-DEPTH !
        ELSE
          0 NORM-IN-CMDSUB? !
        THEN
      THEN
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
    ELSE NORM-IN-SQ? @ IF
      NORM-C @ 39 = IF 0 NORM-IN-SQ? ! THEN
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
    ELSE NORM-IN-DQ? @ NORM-AT-CMDSUB-START? AND IF
      NORM-SKIP-CMDSUB
    ELSE NORM-IN-DQ? @ NORM-C @ 96 = AND IF
      NORM-SKIP-BQ
    ELSE NORM-IN-DQ? @ IF
      \ A backslash inside double quotes escapes the next character,
      \ so an escaped quote must not be counted as the closing one.
      \ This tracking used to be approximate and it did not matter,
      \ because nothing acted on the state; once an unterminated quote
      \ became a diagnosed syntax error, `echo "a\"b"` started
      \ reporting one. The actual unescaping still happens later, in
      \ COPY-DOUBLE-QUOTED - here both characters are simply copied.
      NORM-C @ 92 = NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-U @ < AND IF
        NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
        1 NORM-PEEK NORM-EMIT
        NORM-I @ 2 + NORM-I !
      ELSE
        NORM-C @ 34 = IF 0 NORM-IN-DQ? ! THEN
        NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
        NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
      THEN
    ELSE NORM-C @ 96 = IF
      \ A backquoted region is copied verbatim for the same reason a
      \ $(...) one is: the operators inside belong to the substituted
      \ command, not to this line.
      -1 NORM-IN-BQ? !
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
    ELSE NORM-C @ 39 = IF
      -1 NORM-IN-SQ? !
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
    ELSE NORM-C @ 34 = IF
      -1 NORM-IN-DQ? !
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
    ELSE NORM-C @ 92 = IF
      ( a backslash outside quotes escapes the next character -
        copied verbatim, including if it happens to be an operator
        character, so it stays unspaced/literal like everywhere else
        this shell already treats a backslash-escaped char )
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
      NORM-I @ NORM-U @ < IF
        0 NORM-PEEK NORM-EMIT
        NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
      THEN
    ELSE NORM-C @ 35 = NORM-COMMENT-START? AND IF
      \ A comment runs to end of line: stop copying entirely by
      \ jumping the read position to the end. Quoted '#' never gets
      \ here (the quote branches above consume it), and neither does
      \ a backslash-escaped one.
      NORM-CLOSE
      NORM-U @ NORM-I !
    ELSE NORM-AT-CMDSUB-START? IF
      \ "$(" starting here: copy both characters verbatim and switch
      \ into cmdsub-mode, so the parens belonging to the substitution
      \ are not spaced out as operators.
      -1 NORM-IN-CMDSUB? !
      0 NORM-CMDSUB-DEPTH !
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      1 NORM-PEEK NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 2 + NORM-I !
    ELSE NORM-AT-ARITH-START? IF
      \ "$((" starting here - copy all three characters verbatim and
      \ switch into arith-mode, so operators meaningful to the
      \ arithmetic grammar itself ("<=", "&&", etc.) inside it don't
      \ get shell-level spacing inserted around them - see
      \ NORM-IN-ARITH?'s own comment above.
      -1 NORM-IN-ARITH? !
      0 NORM-ARITH-DEPTH !
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      1 NORM-PEEK NORM-EMIT
      2 NORM-PEEK NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 3 + NORM-I !
    ELSE NORM-C @ OP-CHAR? IF
      NORM-CLOSE
      32 NORM-EMIT
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
      NORM-C @ NORM-DOUBLED-OP? IF
        0 NORM-PEEK NORM-C @ = IF
          NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
          NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
        THEN
      THEN
      \ ">&" and "<&" are single redirection operators (fd duplication)
      \ and must not be split into '>' and '&' - '&' on its own means
      \ something quite different.
      NORM-C @ 62 = NORM-C @ 60 = OR IF
        0 NORM-PEEK 38 = IF
          38 NORM-EMIT
          NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
        THEN
      THEN
      \ "<<-" is its own operator (strip leading tabs), so the '-'
      \ stays attached to the "<<" that was just emitted.
      NORM-C @ 60 = IF
        0 NORM-PEEK 45 = IF
          45 NORM-EMIT
          NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
        THEN
      THEN
      NORM-CLOSE
      32 NORM-EMIT
    ELSE
      NORM-C @ WS? IF NORM-CLOSE THEN
      NORM-C @ NORM-EMIT
      NORM-I @ 1+ NORM-I !
    THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN
  REPEAT
  NORM-CLOSE
  \ A quote still open at end of line is a syntax error, not something
  \ to paper over. POSIX: single quotes cannot contain single quotes,
  \ so "'''" is an empty string followed by an unterminated quote -
  \ which is exactly what the 2.2.2 conformance case checks.
  \ This shell has no line continuation, so end of line really is the
  \ end of the word; a multi-line quoted string is not supported and
  \ would be reported here rather than silently mis-parsed.
  NORM-IN-SQ? @ NORM-IN-DQ? @ OR UNTERMINATED-QUOTE? !
  \ Likewise for a command substitution: "$(" or a backquote still
  \ open means the command text continues on the next line, which is
  \ what JOIN-OPEN-QUOTES acts on.
  \
  \ NORM-IN-CMDSUB?/NORM-CMDSUB-DEPTH are scratch locals now. They
  \ were plain globals and were not reset on entry, so a line ending
  \ inside a $(...) left the flag set into the NEXT call, which would
  \ have copied that whole line through verbatim. Nothing had reached
  \ it because nothing acted on the state at end of line - the same
  \ shape as the quote tracking before Iteration 60.
  NORM-IN-CMDSUB? @ NORM-IN-BQ? @ OR UNTERMINATED-CMDSUB? !
  NORM-LEN @ ;

VARIABLE JQ-LEN
VARIABLE JQ-N
VARIABLE JQ-GUARD
VARIABLE NO-MORE-INPUT?   \ set by SH-C: the command string is all there is

: JOIN-OPEN-QUOTES ( u --- u2 )
  \ LINE-BUF holds u bytes. A quote still open at end of line means the
  \ string continues on the next one:
  \
  \     echo "a
  \     b"
  \
  \ so read further lines, joined by the newline that separated them,
  \ until the quote closes. The same applies to a "$(" or backquote
  \ still open, whose command text continues the same way - the
  \ newline is kept, and CMDSUB-SPLIT-LINES turns it back into a line
  \ boundary for the substituted text (Iteration 105). Iteration 60 made an open quote a syntax
  \ error, which is right at end of INPUT and wrong at end of line -
  \ that check now only fires when this word gives up.
  \
  \ NORMALIZE-OPERATORS is re-run purely to test: it is a pure
  \ function of LINE-BUF into NORM-BUF and sets UNTERMINATED-QUOTE?,
  \ so calling it twice costs a pass over the line and nothing else.
  \ JQ-GUARD stops a runaway at EOF, where READ-INPUT-CALL reports an
  \ empty line and end-of-input identically.
  JQ-LEN !
  0 JQ-GUARD !
  \ In -c mode the command string is the whole input, so there is
  \ nothing to continue onto and asking would block on ACCEPT reading
  \ the terminal. An open quote there is a genuine syntax error.
  \ Still normalizes once on the way out: this word's callers rely on
  \ NORM-BUF and the word spans being valid when it returns, and skip
  \ normalizing again themselves (Iteration 119).
  NO-MORE-INPUT? @ IF JQ-LEN @ NORMALIZE-OPERATORS DROP JQ-LEN @ EXIT THEN
  BEGIN
    JQ-LEN @ NORMALIZE-OPERATORS DROP
    UNTERMINATED-QUOTE? @ UNTERMINATED-CMDSUB? @ OR
    JQ-GUARD @ 4096 < AND
    JQ-LEN @ LINE-HARD-MAX < AND
  WHILE
    JQ-GUARD @ 1+ JQ-GUARD !
    JQ-LEN @ 2 + ENSURE-BUFFER LINE-BUF
    10 LINE-BUF JQ-LEN @ + C!
    JQ-LEN @ 1+ JQ-LEN !
    JQ-LEN @ BUFFER-BODY LINE-BUF READ-INPUT-CALL JQ-N !
    LONG-LINE? @ IF 0 EXIT THEN        \ nothing of it may run
    JQ-N @ 0= IF JQ-LEN @ EXIT THEN
    JQ-LEN @ JQ-N @ + JQ-LEN !
  REPEAT
  JQ-LEN @ ;

: NORM-TOKENIZE ( u --- )
  \ LINE-BUF holds u raw bytes: normalize the operators, copy the
  \ result back over LINE-BUF and tokenize it.
  \
  \ This shape appeared verbatim in four places and, in a fifth, did
  \ not appear where it should have: DO-WHILE re-tokenized its stored
  \ condition text with no normalization at all, because
  \ SAVE-WHILE-COND stores the line as it was typed. So `while a;b`
  \ in a condition never had its ';' spaced out - a latent bug nobody
  \ had hit, found by scoping Stage 1b of PARSE-EXPAND-PLAN.md rather
  \ than by anything failing (Iteration 110).
  \
  \ `TOKENIZE` reads `NORM-BUF` directly rather than having it copied
  \ back over `LINE-BUF` first. That copy needed a fallback for when
  \ the normalized text did not fit `LINE-BUF` - `NORM-MAX` is 512
  \ and `LINE-MAX` is 256 - in which case the ORIGINAL line was
  \ tokenized instead, silently, with its operators unspaced. Reading
  \ `NORM-BUF` where it already is removes the copy, the ceiling and
  \ the fallback together.
  NORMALIZE-OPERATORS TOKENIZE ;

: READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV ( --- )  \ reads one line from stdin, tokenizes
                                 \ it into ARGV/ARGC - no redirect/pipe
                                 \ parsing or dispatch, just enough to
                                 \ inspect ARGV[0] for a keyword.
                                 \ Also stashes a raw (pre-tokenize,
                                 \ pre-expansion) copy in RAW-LINE-BUF,
                                 \ needed by DO-WHILE below to capture
                                 \ a loop's condition/body text so it
                                 \ can be re-expanded fresh on every
                                 \ iteration rather than frozen at its
                                 \ first reading.
  0 BUFFER-BODY LINE-BUF READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE
  \ The newline is the echo of an INTERACTIVELY typed line. A replayed
  \ line - a loop body, a function body, a command substitution - was
  \ never typed, and echoing one printed a blank line before every
  \ iteration of a one-line loop under `-c` (Iteration 117). It never
  \ showed before because `-c` had no way to run a loop at all.
  SHFILE-ACTIVE? @ 0= REPLAY-ACTIVE? @ 0= AND IF CR THEN
  JOIN-CONTINUATIONS
  JOIN-OPEN-QUOTES
  DUP RAW-LINE-LEN !
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER RAW-LINE-BUF
  LINE-BUF RAW-LINE-BUF RAW-LINE-LEN @ MOVE
  \ TOKENIZE, not NORM-TOKENIZE: JOIN-OPEN-QUOTES has just normalized
  \ this exact line - that is how it decides whether a quote is still
  \ open - so NORM-BUF and the word spans are already there.
  \ Normalizing a second time was pure repeated work on every line of
  \ every loop body (Iteration 119).
  TOKENIZE
  \ Checked AFTER normalizing, since NORMALIZE-OPERATORS is what sets
  \ the flag - JOIN-OPEN-QUOTES gives up without normalizing in -c
  \ mode, so the flag can otherwise be a stale one from the previous
  \ line. Tokenizing an unterminated line first is harmless: an
  \ unclosed quote just consumes to end of input, and nothing runs.
  UNTERMINATED-QUOTE? @ IF
    ." shell: syntax error: unterminated quote" CR
    2 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

\ Moved down here in Iteration 105: it now installs the substituted
\ text as a replay input source and reads it through
\ READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV, so it must come after that word and after the
\ REPLAY-*/SHFILE-ACTIVE? declarations. Reaching it from
\ EXPAND-CMDSUB, far above, is what RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD-CALL is for.

: RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD ( --- )
  \ Runs the substituted text inside the child EXPAND-CMDSUB forked,
  \ so clobbering the global ARGV/ARGC/LINE-BUF is safe.
  \
  \ The text is installed as a replay input source and read line by
  \ line through READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV, exactly as a loop body is. That
  \ is what gives a $(...) the whole language rather than a private
  \ subset: several commands, ';', '&&', pipes, redirection,
  \ if/while/for, functions, and nested $(...) - the inner one is
  \ expanded by the ordinary tokenizer running here.
  \
  \ It costs one extra fork for a plain external command, which used
  \ to be exec'd directly from this child. Correctness over the fork:
  \ the alternative is keeping a second, weaker parser to avoid it.
  \ SHFILE-ACTIVE? only decides whether READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV echoes a
  \ newline for an interactive session. This child's stdout is the
  \ capture pipe, so that echo would be captured as output - set here
  \ rather than tested for, since a command substitution is never
  \ interactive.
  -1 SHFILE-ACTIVE? !
  CMDSUB-LINES REPLAY-SRC !  0 REPLAY-ARENA? !  -1 REPLAY-ACTIVE? !
  CMDSUB-LINES-LEN @ REPLAY-LEN !  0 REPLAY-POS !
  BEGIN REPLAY-POS @ REPLAY-LEN @ < WHILE
    READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV
    RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
  REPEAT
  LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT ;
' RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD IS RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD-CALL

\ ==================================================================
\ Same-line 'then'/'fi'/'else' continuation: "if true; then echo hi;
\ fi" all on one line now tokenizes correctly (';' has been a
\ self-delimiting operator since Iteration 24), but DO-IF still needs
\ its own redesign to actually look for "then"/"fi"/"else" in the
\ *remainder of the current line* before falling back to reading a
\ whole new one - getting the tokenization right was necessary but
\ not sufficient, and this is that redesign, for "if" only (while/for
\ still require "do" on its own separate line - left for their own
\ future work, given the added complexity of also needing this for
\ their own body-replay buffers).
\
\ SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD scans the *global* ARGV for the first unquoted
\ token matching a given keyword ("then", "else", or "fi") - if found,
\ ARGV/ARGC are truncated to just what came before it (the "before"
\ content, e.g. the condition, or a body line - may be empty), and
\ everything after the keyword becomes the new PENDING-REMAINDER for
\ READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE to pick up next, rather than reading an
\ actual new line of input. If not found, ARGV/ARGC are left
\ completely untouched and no pending remainder is set - DO-IF then
\ falls back to READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV exactly as before, so "then"/
\ "else"/"fi" on their own separate line still works exactly as it
\ always has.
\
\ Content after the *final* "fi" on the same line (e.g.
\ "if x; then y; fi; z") is a documented, deliberate scope limit: "z"
\ is silently dropped rather than handed back to whatever called
\ DO-IF - preserving it correctly would mean propagating a pending
\ remainder *up* the call stack, a bigger change than this one.
\ ==================================================================

MAX-ARGS CELLS BUFFER: ARGV-PENDING
MAX-ARGS BUFFER: ARGQ-PENDING   \ each pending token's quoted flag
VARIABLE ARGC-PENDING
VARIABLE PENDING-REMAINDER?

: READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE ( --- )
  \ Either continues where a previous same-line split left off, or
  \ genuinely reads a new line of input - transparently, so DO-IF's
  \ own then/else/fi search doesn't need to know or care which.
  PENDING-REMAINDER? @ IF
    0 PENDING-REMAINDER? !
    \ COPY-ARGV-Q, not COPY-ARGV: the pending remainder must carry its
    \ tokens' quoted flags with it, or a stale flag left at the same
    \ index by an earlier expansion hides a real operator. Exactly the
    \ hazard Iteration 28 found and fixed for the ';'/'&&' splitters -
    \ this call site was missed, and it surfaced as
    \ "if ...; then echo two; elif ..." echoing a literal ";".
    ARGV-PENDING ARGQ-PENDING ARGC-PENDING @ COPY-ARGV-Q
  ELSE
    READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV
  THEN ;

VARIABLE SAK-I
VARIABLE SAK-J

VARIABLE SAEK-DEPTH
VARIABLE SAEK-WHICH


VARIABLE SAEK-KW1-ADDR
VARIABLE SAEK-KW1-LEN
VARIABLE SAEK-KW2-ADDR
VARIABLE SAEK-KW2-LEN
VARIABLE SAEK-KW3-ADDR
VARIABLE SAEK-KW3-LEN

: SPLIT-AT-3 ( a1 u1 a2 u2 a3 u3 --- which )
  \ Like SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD, but scans for *either* of two keywords in
  \ one pass, splitting at whichever one is actually found first
  \ (which = 1 for the first keyword, 2 for the second, 0 if neither
  \ is present at all - same truncate/pending-remainder side effects
  \ as SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD either way, and the same "if"/"fi" nesting-
  \ depth tracking). DO-IF needs this rather than two separate
  \ SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD calls, since "else" and "fi" can both be present
  \ in the same remaining line (e.g. "then a; else b; fi") - checking
  \ for "fi" first would find the *later* one, incorrectly swallowing
  \ a real "else" in between as if it were ordinary body text (found
  \ by testing this exact case directly: both branches of an if/else
  \ on one line silently failed to run at all).
  SHADOW{ SAEK-KW1-ADDR SAEK-KW1-LEN SAEK-KW2-ADDR SAEK-KW2-LEN SAEK-KW3-ADDR SAEK-KW3-LEN | SAEK-DEPTH SAK-I SAK-J SAEK-WHICH }
  BEGIN SAK-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    0 SAEK-WHICH !
    SAK-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@ 0= IF
      SAK-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" if" STR= IF
        SAEK-DEPTH @ 1+ SAEK-DEPTH !
      ELSE
        SAEK-DEPTH @ 0 = IF
          SAK-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN SAEK-KW1-ADDR @ SAEK-KW1-LEN @ STR=
          IF 1 SAEK-WHICH !
          ELSE
            SAK-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN SAEK-KW2-ADDR @ SAEK-KW2-LEN @ STR=
            IF 2 SAEK-WHICH !
            ELSE
              SAK-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN SAEK-KW3-ADDR @ SAEK-KW3-LEN @ STR=
              IF 3 SAEK-WHICH ! THEN
            THEN
          THEN
        ELSE
          SAK-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" fi" STR= IF
            SAEK-DEPTH @ 1- SAEK-DEPTH !
          THEN
        THEN
      THEN
    THEN
    SAEK-WHICH @ 0 <> IF
      0 ARGC-PENDING !
      SAK-I @ 1+ SAK-J !
      BEGIN SAK-J @ ARGC @ < WHILE
        SAK-J @ ARGV@ ARGV-PENDING ARGC-PENDING @ CELLS + !
        SAK-J @ ARGV-QUOTED@ ARGQ-PENDING ARGC-PENDING @ + C!
        ARGC-PENDING @ 1+ ARGC-PENDING !
        SAK-J @ 1+ SAK-J !
      REPEAT
      0 ARGV-PENDING ARGC-PENDING @ CELLS + !
      -1 PENDING-REMAINDER? !
      SAK-I @ ARGC !
      0 ARGV SAK-I @ CELLS + !
      SAEK-WHICH @ EXIT
    THEN
    SAK-I @ 1+ SAK-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ;

: SPLIT-AT-EITHER-KEYWORD ( c-addr1 u1 c-addr2 u2 --- which )
  \ The two-alternative case of SPLIT-AT-3, passing the second keyword
  \ twice; a match on the duplicate reports 3, which means the same
  \ thing as 2 here.
  2DUP SPLIT-AT-3 DUP 3 = IF DROP 2 THEN ;

: SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD ( c-addr u --- f )
  \ The one-keyword case of SPLIT-AT-EITHER-KEYWORD above, which it
  \ now simply defers to by passing the same keyword as both
  \ alternatives - the two words previously carried ~35 lines of
  \ identical scanning, splitting and if/fi depth-tracking logic
  \ between them. f = -1 if the keyword was found (ARGV truncated to
  \ what came before it, everything after it left in
  \ ARGV-PENDING/PENDING-REMAINDER? for READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE), 0 if
  \ not (nothing touched). With both alternatives identical the
  \ underlying word can only ever answer 0 or 1, so 0= 0= is just
  \ that normalized to this word's own -1/0 convention.
  2DUP SPLIT-AT-EITHER-KEYWORD 0= 0= ;

VARIABLE COND-TRUE?
VARIABLE IF-ELIF-SKIP

: DO-IF ( --- )
  SHADOW{ | IF-ELIF-SKIP }
  \ ARGV[0] is "if"; the rest of the line is the condition command,
  \ up to an optional unquoted "then" on the *same* line (checked via
  \ SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD - see the comment above it for the full design).
  \ Supports nesting, including inside a branch that itself doesn't
  \ execute: COND-TRUE? and SUPPRESS-EXEC? are both saved on the
  \ return stack at entry (COND-TRUE? first, then SUPPRESS-EXEC? on
  \ top of it) and restored right before every exit. Every body line
  \ is always run through RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL regardless of whether
  \ this branch should actually execute anything - what changes is
  \ SUPPRESS-EXEC?'s own value beforehand, which DO-ASSIGN and
  \ RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE both check before actually doing anything.
  \ This is what lets a nested if/while inside a *skipped* branch
  \ still correctly consume its own then/body/fi (so its "fi" is never
  \ mistaken for this if's own) without actually running anything
  \ inside it - unlike only gating whether RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL itself
  \ gets called, which is what an earlier version of this fix did
  \ before testing directly surfaced that it broke exactly this case.
  \ (while/do/done does NOT support nesting yet - its condition/body
  \ are buffered as raw text across possibly many buffer-sized values,
  \ not simple scalars, so nesting it would need considerably more
  \ than this; left for its own future iteration.)
  COND-TRUE? @ >R
  SUPPRESS-EXEC? @ >R
  SHIFT-ARGV-DOWN
  ARGV-SHIFT ARGQ-SHIFT ARGC-SHIFT @ COPY-ARGV-Q
  S" then" SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD DROP
  SUPPRESS-EXEC? @ IF
    0 COND-TRUE? !
  ELSE
    RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
    LAST-STATUS @ 0 = COND-TRUE? !
  THEN
  PENDING-REMAINDER? @ 0= IF
    READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
    S" then" LINE-IS? 0= IF
      ." if: expected 'then'" CR
      R> SUPPRESS-EXEC? ! R> COND-TRUE? ! EXIT
    THEN
  THEN
  R@ COND-TRUE? @ 0= OR SUPPRESS-EXEC? !
  \ One flat loop over the remaining branches. COND-TRUE? means "some
  \ branch has been taken", not just "the first condition was true" -
  \ that is what lets elif chain: each elif evaluates its condition
  \ only if nothing has been taken yet, and "else" runs only if
  \ nothing has. The nested else-loop the previous version used exists
  \ in this shape as an ordinary iteration.
  BEGIN
    READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
    INPUT-EOF? @ IF
      ." shell: syntax error: unexpected end of input, expected 'fi'" CR
      2 LAST-STATUS !
      R> SUPPRESS-EXEC? ! R> COND-TRUE? ! EXIT
    THEN
    S" elif" S" else" S" fi" SPLIT-AT-3
    DUP 3 = IF                          ( fi )
      DROP
      RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
      \ POSIX: if no branch was taken, the if exits 0 - not with the
      \ failed condition's status. Read COND-TRUE? ("some branch has
      \ been taken") before restoring it. This only became visible
      \ once something looked at the status afterwards, which is what
      \ "fi && echo m" does.
      COND-TRUE? @ 0= IF 0 LAST-STATUS ! THEN
      R> SUPPRESS-EXEC? ! R> COND-TRUE? !
      \ Anything after the "fi" belongs to the enclosing line, not to
      \ the if: "if ...; fi && echo m" must still run the "&& echo m".
      \ It used to be discarded here. Run through RUN-TOKENIZED like
      \ any line, so a leading "&&" splits with an empty left-hand
      \ side and therefore tests the if's own exit status - which is
      \ exactly the semantics wanted. Groups got this in Iteration 49
      \ via AT-GROUP-END?; the compound commands never did.
      PENDING-REMAINDER? @ ARGC-PENDING @ 0 > AND IF
        READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
        RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
      ELSE
        0 PENDING-REMAINDER? !
      THEN
      EXIT
    THEN
    DUP 2 = IF                          ( else )
      DROP
      RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
      R@ COND-TRUE? @ OR SUPPRESS-EXEC? !
    ELSE DUP 1 = IF                     ( elif COND [; then] )
      DROP
      RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL                \ body text preceding the elif
      R@ COND-TRUE? @ OR IF-ELIF-SKIP !
      READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE            \ the condition
      S" then" SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD DROP
      IF-ELIF-SKIP @ IF
        \ A branch was already taken, or the whole if is suppressed:
        \ the condition is still consumed - it may span constructs -
        \ but must not run, and must not change COND-TRUE?.
        -1 SUPPRESS-EXEC? !
        RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
      ELSE
        0 SUPPRESS-EXEC? !
        RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
        LAST-STATUS @ 0 = IF -1 COND-TRUE? ! THEN
      THEN
      PENDING-REMAINDER? @ 0= IF
        READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
        S" then" LINE-IS? 0= IF
          ." if: expected 'then' after 'elif'" CR
          R> SUPPRESS-EXEC? ! R> COND-TRUE? ! EXIT
        THEN
      THEN
      IF-ELIF-SKIP @ IF
        \ A branch was already taken: this elif's body is suppressed
        \ whatever COND-TRUE? says. Deriving suppression from
        \ COND-TRUE? alone here was wrong - it is true precisely
        \ *because* an earlier branch ran, so the body ran too.
        -1 SUPPRESS-EXEC? !
      ELSE
        R@ COND-TRUE? @ 0= OR SUPPRESS-EXEC? !
      THEN
    ELSE                                ( ordinary body line )
      DROP
      RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
    THEN THEN
  0 UNTIL ;

\ ==================================================================
\ while/do/done
\ Unlike if, a loop body has to run more than once - stdin is a
\ one-pass stream, so it can't just be read-and-discarded like if's
\ body could be. This buffers the RAW (pre-tokenize, pre-expansion)
\ text of both the condition and the body once, the first time
\ through, then re-copies and re-tokenizes that saved text into
\ LINE-BUF fresh on every iteration. Re-tokenizing fresh each time -
\ rather than reusing the already-tokenized ARGV from the first
\ reading - matters: it's what lets $?/$$/$VAR in the condition
\ reflect each iteration's own current state rather than a value
\ frozen from before the loop even started (e.g. "while test $? -eq
\ 0" needs $? to update every time to be meaningful at all).
\ Same v0.5 limitations as if: no nesting, no ";"-separated forms.
\ ==================================================================

\ ==================================================================
\ Per-invocation storage for captured bodies
\ ==================================================================
\ A while/for body, a for word-list and a function-definition body are
\ all captured as raw text before being replayed. They used to live in
\ single shared buffers, which is why a loop nested directly inside
\ another loop produced no output at all: the inner loop's capture
\ overwrote the outer's body. Iteration 42 made replay a real input
\ source, which was the prerequisite; this is the other half.
\
\ A bump arena, carved by BODY-ALLOC. Nothing is ever freed
\ explicitly: DO-WHILE/DO-FOR/DO-FUNCDEF each declare BODY-ARENA-TOP
\ as a LOCAL, so the bump pointer is restored when the construct ends
\ - on every exit path, including an early EXIT - which gives exactly
\ the stack discipline nested constructs need, for free.
\
\ Note the idiom for that, since a "|" scratch local is ZEROED rather
\ than preserved:
\
\     BODY-ARENA-TOP @  SHADOW{ BODY-ARENA-TOP | ... }
\
\ pushing the current value and taking it straight back as an argument
\ local. Result: saved on entry, unchanged during the body, restored
\ on exit.
\
\ The arena itself is a BUFFER: (pool.4), so it costs nothing in the
\ image and nothing at runtime until the first loop or function is
\ actually seen.

4096 CONSTANT WHILE-BODY-MAX
VARIABLE WHILE-COND-BUF     \ pointers into the arena, not buffers
VARIABLE WHILE-COND-LEN
VARIABLE WHILE-BODY-BUF
VARIABLE WHILE-BODY-LEN
VARIABLE RAW-SKIP-I

VARIABLE RLS-ADDR
VARIABLE RLS-WANT
VARIABLE RLS-LEN
VARIABLE RLS-I
VARIABLE RLS-POS
VARIABLE RLS-SQ?
VARIABLE RLS-DQ?

: SAME-LINE-DO? ( --- f )
  \ Is the last token of the current line an unquoted "do"? That is
  \ the "while COND; do" / "for X in W; do" shape. Checking the LAST
  \ token specifically, rather than "is there a do anywhere", so a
  \ condition that merely mentions do (`while grep do file`) is not
  \ mistaken for it.
  ARGC @ 2 < IF 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGC @ 1- DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" do" STR= ;

: RAW-LAST-CHAR ( c-addr u c --- pos | -1 )
  \ Offset of the last UNQUOTED ';' in raw text, or -1. Needed because
  \ a while condition is stored as raw text (so it can be re-expanded
  \ fresh each iteration), not as tokens - so the "; do" tail has to be
  \ trimmed off the raw text, not off ARGV. The last one rather than
  \ the first, so "while a; b; do" keeps the whole compound condition.
  SHADOW{ RLS-ADDR RLS-LEN RLS-WANT | RLS-I RLS-POS RLS-SQ? RLS-DQ? }
  -1 RLS-POS !
  BEGIN RLS-I @ RLS-LEN @ < WHILE
    RLS-ADDR @ RLS-I @ + C@
    DUP 39 = RLS-DQ? @ 0= AND IF RLS-SQ? @ 0= RLS-SQ? ! THEN
    DUP 34 = RLS-SQ? @ 0= AND IF RLS-DQ? @ 0= RLS-DQ? ! THEN
    RLS-WANT @ = RLS-SQ? @ 0= AND RLS-DQ? @ 0= AND IF RLS-I @ RLS-POS ! THEN
    RLS-I @ 1+ RLS-I !
  REPEAT
  RLS-POS @ ;

: FOR-AT-SEMI? ( i --- f )
  DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" ;" STR= ;

: SAVE-WHILE-COND ( --- )
  \ RAW-LINE-BUF/RAW-LINE-LEN holds the raw "while ..." line just
  \ read. Skips its own leading whitespace, the literal "while" (5
  \ characters), and more whitespace, then saves whatever remains
  \ into WHILE-COND-BUF (NUL-terminated) - the condition text, with
  \ no expansion applied yet (that happens fresh each iteration when
  \ it's re-tokenized).
  0 RAW-SKIP-I !
  BEGIN
    RAW-SKIP-I @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ < IF RAW-LINE-BUF RAW-SKIP-I @ + C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE RAW-SKIP-I @ 1+ RAW-SKIP-I ! REPEAT
  RAW-SKIP-I @ 5 + RAW-SKIP-I !
  BEGIN
    RAW-SKIP-I @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ < IF RAW-LINE-BUF RAW-SKIP-I @ + C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE RAW-SKIP-I @ 1+ RAW-SKIP-I ! REPEAT
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ RAW-SKIP-I @ > IF
    RAW-LINE-LEN @ RAW-SKIP-I @ - WHILE-COND-LEN !
    RAW-LINE-BUF RAW-SKIP-I @ + WHILE-COND-BUF @ BODY@ WHILE-COND-LEN @ MOVE
  ELSE
    0 WHILE-COND-LEN !
  THEN
  SAME-LINE-DO? IF
    WHILE-COND-BUF @ BODY@ WHILE-COND-LEN @ 59 RAW-LAST-CHAR
    DUP -1 <> IF WHILE-COND-LEN ! ELSE DROP THEN
  THEN
  0 WHILE-COND-BUF @ BODY@ WHILE-COND-LEN @ + C! ;

VARIABLE WHILE-BODY-CAP   \ this body's current arena capacity

: BODY-ENSURE ( n --- ok? )
  \ Make room for n more bytes in the body being captured, growing it
  \ if needed. A body can be extended in place exactly when it is the
  \ TOPMOST arena allocation, which during a capture it always is:
  \ nothing else allocates while lines are being appended (a nested
  \ loop inside the body is still just text at this point; it does not
  \ allocate until it runs). So bumping the arena extends this body.
  \
  \ BODY-ALLOC may RESIZE and therefore relocate the arena. Harmless:
  \ WHILE-BODY-BUF holds an offset, not a pointer.
  WHILE-BODY-LEN @ + 1+ WHILE-BODY-CAP @ > IF
    WHILE-BODY-BUF @ WHILE-BODY-CAP @ + BODY-ARENA-TOP @ = IF
      WHILE-BODY-MAX BODY-ALLOC DROP
      WHILE-BODY-CAP @ WHILE-BODY-MAX + WHILE-BODY-CAP !
      -1
    ELSE
      0
    THEN
  ELSE
    -1
  THEN ;

: APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY ( --- )
  \ Appends the current RAW-LINE-BUF/RAW-LINE-LEN (NUL-terminated) to
  \ the end of the body being captured, growing the body as needed so
  \ there is no fixed maximum body size.
  \
  \ It used to drop any line that would not fit in a fixed 4096-byte
  \ buffer, silently and with no error - which showed up as a script
  \ producing quietly WRONG output rather than failing, once a body
  \ was large enough. Found by diffing a generated 60-loop script
  \ against bash. If a body somehow still cannot be grown (it is not
  \ the topmost allocation, which should not happen during a capture)
  \ that is now reported rather than passed over in silence.
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ BODY-ENSURE 0= IF
    ." shell: loop body too large to store" CR
    EXIT
  THEN
  RAW-LINE-BUF WHILE-BODY-BUF @ BODY@ WHILE-BODY-LEN @ + RAW-LINE-LEN @ MOVE
  WHILE-BODY-LEN @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ + WHILE-BODY-LEN !
  0 WHILE-BODY-BUF @ BODY@ WHILE-BODY-LEN @ + C!
  WHILE-BODY-LEN @ 1+ WHILE-BODY-LEN ! ;


VARIABLE BODY-DEPTH

: CAPTURE-CONTINUE? ( --- f )
  \ Called once per line while capturing a while/for body. True if the
  \ line belongs to the body, false if it is the "done" that closes
  \ THIS loop.
  \
  \ The depth count is what makes a loop nested inside another loop
  \ work: without it the outer capture stopped at the INNER loop's
  \ "done", so the outer body was truncated and the outer "done" was
  \ left to execute as a stray top-level line. Same shape as
  \ SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD's own if/fi depth tracking, and found the same
  \ way - by testing a nested loop directly rather than by
  \ inspection. Only "while"/"for" open a "done"; "do" and "if" do
  \ not, so they are not counted.
  \
  \ BODY-DEPTH is declared as a local by DO-WHILE/DO-FOR, so captures
  \ nest too.
  INPUT-EOF? @ IF
    ." shell: syntax error: unexpected end of input, expected 'done'" CR
    2 LAST-STATUS !
    0 EXIT
  THEN
  S" done" LINE-IS? IF
    BODY-DEPTH @ 0 > IF BODY-DEPTH @ 1- BODY-DEPTH ! -1 ELSE 0 THEN
  ELSE
    S" while" LINE-IS? S" for" LINE-IS? OR IF
      BODY-DEPTH @ 1+ BODY-DEPTH !
    THEN
    -1
  THEN ;

DEFER RUN-LINE-CALL
\ RUN-LINE is the last word in the file; the suffix after a "done" or
\ "esac" is run as an ordinary line, so this reaches it the usual way.

VARIABLE SUF-BUF        \ arena offset of the saved suffix text
VARIABLE SUF-LEN
VARIABLE SUF-KWLEN
VARIABLE SUF-I
VARIABLE LOOP-RAN?      \ did this loop execute its body even once?

: SAVE-COMPOUND-SUFFIX ( kwlen --- )
  \ RAW-LINE-BUF holds the line that closes a while/for/case -
  \ "done && echo m", say. CAPTURE-CONTINUE? and DO-CASE both detect
  \ that line with LINE-IS?, which looks only at ARGV[0], so
  \ everything after the keyword used to be dropped in silence.
  \
  \ The RAW text is saved, not the tokens. ARGV entries point into
  \ LINE-BUF, and running the loop reads lines - which overwrites it.
  \ Copying the pointer array the way RT-SAVE-REST does would not
  \ help here: that solves a shared-array problem, not a text-lifetime
  \ one. This is the distinction Iteration 101 recorded and is why
  \ this was not a mechanical repeat of 92's fix for "fi": DO-IF
  \ reaches its "fi" AFTER the body has run, and a loop reaches its
  \ "done" before.
  SHADOW{ SUF-KWLEN | SUF-I }
  0 SUF-LEN !
  BEGIN
    SUF-I @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ < IF RAW-LINE-BUF SUF-I @ + C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE SUF-I @ 1+ SUF-I ! REPEAT
  SUF-I @ SUF-KWLEN @ + SUF-I !
  BEGIN
    SUF-I @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ < IF RAW-LINE-BUF SUF-I @ + C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE SUF-I @ 1+ SUF-I ! REPEAT
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ SUF-I @ > IF
    RAW-LINE-LEN @ SUF-I @ - SUF-LEN !
    SUF-LEN @ 1+ BODY-ALLOC SUF-BUF !
    RAW-LINE-BUF SUF-I @ + SUF-BUF @ BODY@ SUF-LEN @ MOVE
  THEN ;

: RUN-COMPOUND-SUFFIX ( --- )
  \ Runs whatever followed the terminator, once the construct itself
  \ has finished, as an ordinary line. A leading "&&" then splits with
  \ an empty left-hand side and so tests the construct's own exit
  \ status - the same thing DO-IF does after "fi" (Iteration 92).
  SUF-LEN @ 0= IF EXIT THEN
  SUF-BUF @ BODY@ LINE-BUF SUF-LEN @ MOVE
  SUF-LEN @  0 SUF-LEN !
  RUN-LINE-CALL ;

VARIABLE JT-FROM
VARIABLE JT-TO
VARIABLE JT-I
VARIABLE JT-LEN
VARIABLE RA-A
VARIABLE RA-U
VARIABLE TE-I
VARIABLE TE-A
VARIABLE TE-U
VARIABLE OL-DO
VARIABLE OL-DONE
VARIABLE OL-I
VARIABLE OL-END

: TOK-EQ? ( i c-addr u --- f )
  \ Is ARGV[i] this exact unquoted word? LINE-IS? asks the same
  \ question but only ever of ARGV[0].
  SHADOW{ TE-I TE-A TE-U }
  TE-I @ ARGC @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  TE-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@ IF 0 EXIT THEN
  TE-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN TE-A @ TE-U @ STR= ;

: RAW-APPEND ( c-addr u --- )
  \ Appends one word and a separating space at JT-LEN, which is
  \ JOIN-ARGV-TO-RAW's local and so belongs to the join in progress.
  SHADOW{ RA-A RA-U }
  RA-U @ JT-LEN @ + 2 + ENSURE-BUFFER RAW-LINE-BUF
  RA-U @ JT-LEN @ + LINE-HARD-MAX < IF
    RA-A @ RAW-LINE-BUF JT-LEN @ + RA-U @ MOVE
    JT-LEN @ RA-U @ + JT-LEN !
    32 RAW-LINE-BUF JT-LEN @ + C!
    JT-LEN @ 1+ JT-LEN !
  THEN ;

: JOIN-ARGV-TO-RAW ( from to --- )
  \ Rebuilds RAW-LINE-BUF from ARGV[from..to), one space between
  \ words. The tokens are raw - quotes and '$' still in them, since
  \ Iteration 114 - so the result is a line that means exactly what
  \ that part of the original line meant. Original spacing is lost,
  \ which nothing depends on.
  \
  \ Safe to overwrite RAW-LINE-BUF here: every caller has already
  \ taken what it needs out of it.
  SHADOW{ JT-FROM JT-TO | JT-I JT-LEN }
  JT-FROM @ JT-I !
  BEGIN JT-I @ JT-TO @ < JT-I @ ARGC @ < AND WHILE
    JT-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN RAW-APPEND
    JT-I @ 1+ JT-I !
  REPEAT
  JT-LEN @ 0 > IF JT-LEN @ 1- JT-LEN ! THEN   ( the trailing space )
  JT-LEN @ RAW-LINE-LEN !
  0 RAW-LINE-BUF JT-LEN @ + C! ;

: ONE-LINE-LOOP? ( --- f )
  \ Is the whole loop on this one line - "for i in 1 2; do echo $i;
  \ done"? True when there is an unquoted "do" that is not the last
  \ token and an unquoted "done" after it.
  \
  \ The LAST "done" is taken, so a one-line loop nested inside another
  \ belongs to the outer one; the inner is then a body line and is
  \ recognised again when it runs.
  SHADOW{ | OL-I }
  -1 OL-DO !  -1 OL-DONE !
  BEGIN OL-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    OL-DO @ -1 = IF
      OL-I @ S" do" TOK-EQ? IF OL-I @ OL-DO ! THEN
    ELSE
      OL-I @ S" done" TOK-EQ? IF OL-I @ OL-DONE ! THEN
    THEN
    OL-I @ 1+ OL-I !
  REPEAT
  OL-DO @ ARGC @ 1- = IF -1 OL-DO ! THEN
  OL-DO @ -1 <> OL-DONE @ -1 <> AND ;

: CAPTURE-ONE-LINE-LOOP ( --- )
  \ Splits a one-line loop into the three pieces the multi-line path
  \ produces by reading further lines: the body, the suffix after
  \ "done", and a header left in ARGV.
  \
  \ The body is stored as text, exactly as APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY
  \ stores a body line read from input, so everything downstream -
  \ DO-WHILE-BODY, nesting, break/continue - is unchanged and unaware.
  \
  \ ARGC is cut back to the header before the caller expands anything:
  \ the body's words must not be expanded now, when the loop is being
  \ set up, but each time the body runs. Without that,
  \ "for i in 1 2; do echo $i; done" expands $i once, before i exists.
  0 WHILE-BODY-LEN !
  OL-DO @ 1+ OL-DONE @ JOIN-ARGV-TO-RAW
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ 0 > IF APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY THEN
  OL-DONE @ ARGC @ JOIN-ARGV-TO-RAW
  4 SAVE-COMPOUND-SUFFIX                  ( "done" )
  OL-DO @ ARGC !
  0 ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + ! ;

: JOIN-WHILE-COND ( --- )
  \ Rebuilds RAW-LINE-BUF as just "while COND", so SAVE-WHILE-COND can
  \ read it the way it reads any while line. A trailing ";" before the
  \ "do" is dropped - it separated the condition from the "do" and is
  \ not part of the condition.
  OL-DO @ OL-END !
  OL-END @ 1- 0 > IF
    OL-END @ 1- S" ;" TOK-EQ? IF OL-END @ 1- OL-END ! THEN
  THEN
  0 OL-END @ JOIN-ARGV-TO-RAW ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Shell functions: name() { <body> } - definition, invocation,
\ redefinition, and recursion. Each function's body is stored as raw,
\ NUL-separated lines (the same format while/for's own bodies use),
\ but persistently, in its own dedicated table slot keyed by name -
\ unlike while/for's shared WHILE-BODY-BUF, since a function's body
\ needs to survive and be replayed on every future call, not just
\ once immediately after being read.
\ ==================================================================


\ Each function's body is a heap block of its own, sized to fit
\ (Iteration 249); until then they were 2048-byte slots and a longer
\ body was cut short without a word. A redefined function's old block
\ is RETIRED, not freed: the function may be redefining itself while
\ its old body is being replayed. The top-level loop frees it.
MAX-FUNCS CELLS BUFFER: FUNC-BODY-PTRS
MAX-FUNCS BUFFER: FUNC-SUBSH
VARIABLE FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL?   \ set by DO-FUNCDEF, read by SET-FUNC
VARIABLE FD-DEPTH            \ nested "{" depth while capturing a body
MAX-FUNCS CELLS BUFFER: FUNC-BODY-LENS

: FUNC-BODY-SLOT ( i --- addr )  CELLS FUNC-BODY-PTRS + @ ;
: FUNC-BODY-LEN-SLOT ( i --- addr )  CELLS FUNC-BODY-LENS + ;
: FUNC-SUBSH-SLOT  ( i --- addr )  FUNC-SUBSH + ;

VARIABLE SF-NAME
VARIABLE SF-BODY-ADDR
VARIABLE SF-BODY-LEN
VARIABLE SF-I

: SET-FUNC ( name-addr body-addr body-len --- )
  \ Defines (or, if name-addr already names a function, redefines) a
  \ function - a new definition with the same name simply overwrites
  \ the existing table slot in place, the same "update if found,
  \ append if not" shape SET-SHVAR already uses for shell variables.
  \ body-addr/body-len is raw, NUL-separated line data (not a C
  \ string - it can and does contain embedded NULs as line
  \ separators), so it's copied via a length-bounded MOVE, not
  \ SAFE-CSTR-COPY/SAFE-COPY-NUL (which assume a single trailing NUL
  \ terminator, the wrong shape for this data).
  SF-BODY-LEN ! SF-BODY-ADDR ! SF-NAME !
  SF-NAME @ FIND-FUNC
  DUP -1 = IF
    DROP
    FUNC-COUNT @ MAX-FUNCS < 0= IF
      ." shell: too many functions" CR
      EXIT
    THEN
    FUNC-COUNT @ SF-I !
    FUNC-COUNT @ 1+ FUNC-COUNT !
  ELSE
    SF-I !
  THEN
  SF-NAME @ SF-I @ FUNC-NAME-SLOT FUNC-NAME-MAX SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  SF-BODY-LEN @ SF-I @ FUNC-BODY-LEN-SLOT !
  FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? @ SF-I @ FUNC-SUBSH-SLOT C!
  SF-I @ FUNC-BODY-SLOT ?DUP IF BUF-RETIRE THEN
  SF-BODY-LEN @ 1+ ALLOCATE ABORT" shell: out of memory"
  DUP SF-I @ CELLS FUNC-BODY-PTRS + !
  SF-BODY-ADDR @ SWAP SF-BODY-LEN @ MOVE ;

VARIABLE FUNC-CUR-I

: RUN-FUNC-BODY ( i --- )
  \ i is the function's own table index. Installs its stored body as
  \ the current input source and reads lines the ordinary way, exactly
  \ as DO-WHILE-BODY does - so a nested if/while/for inside a function
  \ body works, and so does recursion: REPLAY-SRC/LEN/POS and
  \ FUNC-CUR-I are locals, so an inner invocation cannot disturb an
  \ outer, still-in-progress one's position.
  \
  \ FUNC-DEPTH tracks call nesting so "return" outside any function
  \ can be diagnosed. The loop stops as soon as RETURN-PENDING? is set
  \ (by a "return" somewhere in this invocation's body, possibly
  \ several ';'/'&&'/'||'-chained segments deep - see
  \ RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE), which is then reset before returning, so
  \ an outer caller never sees a stale, already-consumed return. It
  \ also stops as soon as LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? is set (a
  \ "break"/"continue" meant for a while/for loop further out that
  \ called this function from its body) - but unlike RETURN-PENDING?
  \ that flag is deliberately left untouched here, so it keeps
  \ propagating outward until DO-WHILE-BODY consumes it.
  \
  \ ARGV[1..] - the call arguments, still the live global ARGV at this
  \ point - become this invocation's $1.. ; the caller's own
  \ positional parameters are saved keyed by FUNC-DEPTH and restored
  \ when this returns, so recursive and nested calls each see only
  \ their own.
  SHADOW{ FUNC-CUR-I | REPLAY-ACTIVE? REPLAY-SRC REPLAY-ARENA? REPLAY-LEN REPLAY-POS }
  FUNC-CUR-I @ FUNC-SUBSH-SLOT C@ IF
    \ "f() ( ... )": run the whole body in a child, so nothing it does
    \ reaches this shell. Recursing here rather than duplicating the
    \ loop below - the child clears its own flag first so it does not
    \ fork again on the way in.
    FORK DUP 0 = IF
      DROP
      0 FUNC-CUR-I @ FUNC-SUBSH-SLOT C!
      FUNC-CUR-I @ RECURSE
      LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT
    THEN
    WAITPID DROP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND LAST-STATUS !
    EXIT
  THEN
  \ SAVE-POS-PARAMS indexes POS-PARAMS-SAVE by depth with no bound of
  \ its own: past MAX-POS-PARAM-DEPTH it would MOVE a full parameter
  \ block past the end of that buffer. Nothing had hit it because
  \ shadow.4's own save stack overflows at a similar depth first - but
  \ that is an accident of two unrelated limits, and its ABORT kills
  \ the shell rather than reporting a shell error.
  FUNC-DEPTH @ MAX-POS-PARAM-DEPTH < 0= IF
    ." shell: function recursion too deep" CR
    1 LAST-STATUS !
    EXIT
  THEN
  FUNC-DEPTH @ SAVE-POS-PARAMS
  SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-ARGV
  FUNC-DEPTH @ 1+ FUNC-DEPTH !
  FUNC-CUR-I @ FUNC-BODY-SLOT REPLAY-SRC !  -1 REPLAY-ACTIVE? !
  FUNC-CUR-I @ FUNC-BODY-LEN-SLOT @ REPLAY-LEN !
  BEGIN
    REPLAY-POS @ REPLAY-LEN @ <
    RETURN-PENDING? @ 0= AND
    LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? @ 0= AND
  WHILE
    READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV
    RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
  REPEAT
  0 RETURN-PENDING? !
  FUNC-DEPTH @ 1- FUNC-DEPTH !
  FUNC-DEPTH @ RESTORE-POS-PARAMS ;
' RUN-FUNC-BODY IS RUN-FUNC-BODY-CALL

64 BUFFER: FUNCDEF-NAME-BUF
VARIABLE FBC-I
VARIABLE FBC-D
VARIABLE FD-CLOSE

: FIND-BODY-CLOSE ( --- idx | -1 )
  \ ARGV[0] should be the body's opening "{" (or "("); returns the
  \ index of the brace that closes it, or -1 if it is not there.
  \
  \ By depth rather than "the last token": a definition nested inside
  \ another, or anything following the closing brace on the same
  \ line, both leave tokens after it.
  SHADOW{ | FBC-I FBC-D }
  0 FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? @ IF S" (" ELSE S" {" THEN TOK-EQ? 0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  BEGIN FBC-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    FBC-I @ FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? @ IF S" (" ELSE S" {" THEN TOK-EQ? IF
      FBC-D @ 1+ FBC-D !
    THEN
    FBC-I @ FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? @ IF S" )" ELSE S" }" THEN TOK-EQ? IF
      FBC-D @ 1- FBC-D !
      FBC-D @ 0= IF FBC-I @ EXIT THEN
    THEN
    FBC-I @ 1+ FBC-I !
  REPEAT
  -1 ;

VARIABLE FDL-I

: FD-OPENS-BRACE? ( --- f )
  \ Does this body line open a nested brace block? A nested function
  \ header ("func_c() {") ends with "{", as does a multi-line group.
  ARGC @ 0 = IF 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGC @ 1- DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" {" STR= ;

: FD-BODY-END? ( --- f )
  \ True only at the "}" that closes THIS function. Without the depth
  \ count a nested definition's own "}" ended the outer body early,
  \ truncating it - the "nested function definitions are not
  \ supported" limit recorded in Iteration 43.
  INPUT-EOF? @ IF
    ." shell: syntax error: unexpected end of input, expected '}'" CR
    2 LAST-STATUS !
    -1 EXIT
  THEN
  S" }" LINE-IS? IF
    FD-DEPTH @ 0 > IF FD-DEPTH @ 1- FD-DEPTH ! 0 ELSE -1 THEN
  ELSE
    FD-OPENS-BRACE? IF FD-DEPTH @ 1+ FD-DEPTH ! THEN
    0
  THEN ;

VARIABLE FDC-DEPTH
VARIABLE FDC-I

: FDC-OPENER? ( i --- f )
  DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN
  2DUP S" if"    STR= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" while" STR= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" for"   STR= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
       S" case"  STR= ;

: FDC-CLOSER? ( i --- f )
  DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN
  2DUP S" fi"   STR= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" done" STR= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
       S" esac" STR= ;

: FDC-BALANCED? ( --- f )
  \ Is the compound command on this line already complete? "f() if
  \ true; then x; fi" is; "f() if true" continues on later lines.
  SHADOW{ | FDC-I FDC-DEPTH }
  BEGIN FDC-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    FDC-I @ FDC-OPENER? IF FDC-DEPTH @ 1+ FDC-DEPTH ! THEN
    FDC-I @ FDC-CLOSER? IF FDC-DEPTH @ 1- FDC-DEPTH ! THEN
    FDC-I @ 1+ FDC-I !
  REPEAT
  FDC-DEPTH @ 1 < ;

: FDC-LINE-END? ( --- f )   \ closes the compound body, counting nesting
  INPUT-EOF? @ IF
    ." shell: syntax error: unexpected end of input" CR
    2 LAST-STATUS !
    -1 EXIT
  THEN
  S" fi" LINE-IS? S" done" LINE-IS? OR S" esac" LINE-IS? OR IF
    FDC-DEPTH @ 0 > IF FDC-DEPTH @ 1- FDC-DEPTH ! 0 ELSE -1 THEN
  ELSE
    S" if" LINE-IS? S" while" LINE-IS? OR
    S" for" LINE-IS? OR S" case" LINE-IS? OR IF
      FDC-DEPTH @ 1+ FDC-DEPTH !
    THEN
    0
  THEN ;

: FD-RAW-AFTER-PAREN ( --- )
  \ Shift RAW-LINE-BUF left so it starts just past the header's ")",
  \ leaving the compound command's own text as if it were a line of
  \ its own - APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY then stores it unchanged. Safe
  \ to clobber RAW-LINE-BUF here: the header tokens have already been
  \ read out of ARGV.
  0 FDC-I !
  BEGIN
    FDC-I @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ < IF RAW-LINE-BUF FDC-I @ + C@ 41 <> ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE FDC-I @ 1+ FDC-I ! REPEAT
  FDC-I @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ < IF FDC-I @ 1+ FDC-I ! THEN
  BEGIN
    FDC-I @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ < IF RAW-LINE-BUF FDC-I @ + C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE FDC-I @ 1+ FDC-I ! REPEAT
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ FDC-I @ - 0 > IF
    RAW-LINE-BUF FDC-I @ + RAW-LINE-BUF RAW-LINE-LEN @ FDC-I @ - MOVE
    RAW-LINE-LEN @ FDC-I @ - RAW-LINE-LEN !
  ELSE
    0 RAW-LINE-LEN !
  THEN ;

: DO-FUNCDEF ( --- )
  \ ARGV[0] ends with "()" - a function definition header. Requires
  \ "{" later on the same line (the common "name() {" style), on its
  \ own separate line, or with the WHOLE body on one line -
  \ "f() { echo hi; }" - which FIND-BODY-CLOSE handles by locating
  \ the matching brace among the tokens (Iteration 118). Stores the captured body (raw text, NUL-separated lines, the
  \ same shape while/for's own bodies use) via SET-FUNC, keyed by the
  \ name extracted from ARGV[0] - a later definition with the same
  \ name simply replaces it.
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @
  SHADOW{ BODY-ARENA-TOP | WHILE-BODY-BUF WHILE-BODY-LEN WHILE-BODY-CAP }
  WHILE-BODY-MAX BODY-ALLOC WHILE-BODY-BUF !  WHILE-BODY-MAX WHILE-BODY-CAP !
  0 ARGV@ FUNCDEF-NAME-BUF 64 SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP   \ ARGV[0] is now the bare name
  \ POSIX allows a function body to be any compound command, so
  \ "f() ( ... )" is as valid as "f() { ... }" - and it means something
  \ different: the body runs in a subshell, so its assignments and cd
  \ do not affect the caller. Which form was used is recorded per
  \ function and honoured by RUN-FUNC-BODY.
  0 FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? !
  \ Drop the three header tokens - name, "(" and ")" - before looking
  \ for the body opener. Scanning the whole line instead finds the
  \ parameter list's own "(" at index 1 and mistakes every brace
  \ function for a subshell one, which is exactly what happened.
  ARGC @ 3 > IF
    ARGC @ 3 - 0 DO
      I 3 + CELLS ARGV + @    I CELLS ARGV + !
      I 3 + ARGV-QUOTED + C@  I ARGV-QUOTED + C!
    LOOP
    ARGC @ 3 - ARGC !
  ELSE
    0 ARGC !
  THEN
  0 ARGC @ CELLS ARGV + !
  ARGC @ 0 > IF
    S" (" 0 ARGV@ STR0= IF -1 FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? ! THEN
  THEN
  \ POSIX allows ANY compound command as a body, not only { } or ( ).
  \ "f() if true; then x; fi" is valid, and is what function.sh uses.
  \ Detected here, before the brace/paren path: the rest of the line
  \ is stored as the body's first line and further lines are read
  \ until the construct's own terminator.
  ARGC @ 0 > FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? @ 0= AND IF
    0 FDC-OPENER? IF
      0 WHILE-BODY-LEN !
      FDC-BALANCED? 0= FDC-DEPTH !
      FD-RAW-AFTER-PAREN
      APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY
      FDC-DEPTH @ IF
        \ Depth counts constructs opened INSIDE the body; the outer
        \ one's own terminator is what ends the capture, so this
        \ starts at 0 rather than 1.
        0 FDC-DEPTH !
        BEGIN
          READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
          FDC-LINE-END? 0=
        WHILE
          ARGC @ 0 > IF APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY THEN
        REPEAT
        APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY
      THEN
      FUNCDEF-NAME-BUF WHILE-BODY-BUF @ BODY@ WHILE-BODY-LEN @
      SUPPRESS-EXEC? @ 0= IF SET-FUNC ELSE DROP 2DROP THEN
      EXIT
    THEN
  THEN
  \ The whole body on one line - "f() { echo hi; }". The opener is
  \ ARGV[0] and its closer is the last token, so the body is simply
  \ the tokens between them, joined back into a line and stored the
  \ way a body line read from input is stored. Same shape as
  \ Iteration 117's one-line loop, and possible for the same reason:
  \ since Iteration 114 the tokens are still the words as written, so
  \ nothing in the body has been expanded yet.
  \
  \ The LAST closer, so a definition nested inside another belongs to
  \ the outer one.
  FIND-BODY-CLOSE DUP 0 > IF
    FD-CLOSE !
    0 WHILE-BODY-LEN !
    1 FD-CLOSE @ JOIN-ARGV-TO-RAW
    RAW-LINE-LEN @ 0 > IF APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY THEN
    FUNCDEF-NAME-BUF WHILE-BODY-BUF @ BODY@ WHILE-BODY-LEN @
    SUPPRESS-EXEC? @ 0= IF SET-FUNC ELSE DROP 2DROP THEN
    \ Anything after the closing brace is the rest of the line and
    \ still has to run - "outer() { inner() { echo deep; }; inner; }"
    \ stores the inner definition and then calls it, both from the
    \ outer body's single stored line.
    FD-CLOSE @ 1+ ARGC @ < IF
      FD-CLOSE @ 1+ ARGC @ JOIN-ARGV-TO-RAW
      RAW-LINE-BUF LINE-BUF RAW-LINE-LEN @ MOVE
      RAW-LINE-LEN @ RUN-LINE-CALL
    THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  DROP
  FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? @ IF S" (" ELSE S" {" THEN SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD DROP
  PENDING-REMAINDER? @ 0= IF
    READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
    S" {" LINE-IS? IF 0 FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? ! ELSE
      S" (" LINE-IS? IF -1 FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? ! ELSE
        ." function: expected '{' or '('" CR EXIT
      THEN
    THEN
  THEN
  0 WHILE-BODY-LEN !
  0 FD-DEPTH !
  BEGIN
    READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
    FUNCDEF-SUBSHELL? @ IF S" )" LINE-IS? ELSE FD-BODY-END? THEN 0= WHILE
    ARGC @ 0 > IF APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY THEN
  REPEAT
  FUNCDEF-NAME-BUF WHILE-BODY-BUF @ BODY@ WHILE-BODY-LEN @
  SUPPRESS-EXEC? @ 0= IF SET-FUNC ELSE DROP 2DROP THEN ;

VARIABLE WHILE-BODY-I
VARIABLE WHILE-BODY-CUR

: DO-WHILE-BODY ( --- )
  \ Runs each stored body line once, in order, by installing the body
  \ as the current input source and then reading lines the ordinary
  \ way - so a nested if/while/for inside the body reads its own
  \ continuation lines from the body too. Stops early if
  \ LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? is set (by "break"/"continue", run either
  \ directly here or by a function called from within - see
  \ RUN-FUNC-BODY on why that word checks but never resets this flag).
  \ Either way this word - and only this word - resets it before
  \ returning: the iteration it was scoped to has now ended.
  \ LOOP-BREAK? is deliberately left alone; that is the outer loop's
  \ own check, made right after this returns.
  SHADOW{ | REPLAY-ACTIVE? REPLAY-SRC REPLAY-ARENA? REPLAY-LEN REPLAY-POS }
  WHILE-BODY-BUF @ REPLAY-SRC !  -1 REPLAY-ARENA? !  -1 REPLAY-ACTIVE? !
  WHILE-BODY-LEN @ REPLAY-LEN !
  BEGIN
    REPLAY-POS @ REPLAY-LEN @ <
    LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? @ 0= AND
    \ A "return" inside a loop inside a function ends the body too,
    \ and must keep propagating - RUN-FUNC-BODY is what consumes it.
    \ Without this, "while : ; do return; done" spun forever: the
    \ return set RETURN-PENDING?, nothing here looked at it, and the
    \ condition was ":".
    RETURN-PENDING? @ 0= AND
  WHILE
    READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV
    RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
  REPEAT
  0 LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? ! ;

: DO-WHILE ( --- )
  \ ARGV[0] is "while"; RAW-LINE-BUF holds the raw line just read.
  \ The condition text and the body get their own arena storage for
  \ this invocation, so a while nested inside another loop's body no
  \ longer overwrites the outer one's. BODY-ARENA-TOP is taken as an
  \ argument local (see BODY-ALLOC's comment) so it is restored on
  \ every exit path, releasing both automatically.
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @
  \ One physical line: SHADOW{ ... } cannot span lines (see shadow.4).
  SHADOW{ BODY-ARENA-TOP | WHILE-COND-BUF WHILE-COND-LEN WHILE-BODY-BUF WHILE-BODY-LEN WHILE-BODY-CAP BODY-DEPTH SUF-BUF SUF-LEN LOOP-RAN? }
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ 1+ BODY-ALLOC WHILE-COND-BUF !
  WHILE-BODY-MAX BODY-ALLOC WHILE-BODY-BUF !  WHILE-BODY-MAX WHILE-BODY-CAP !
  0 WHILE-BODY-LEN !
  0 BODY-DEPTH !
  ONE-LINE-LOOP? IF
    \ Body and suffix first: both rebuild RAW-LINE-BUF, and
    \ SAVE-WHILE-COND reads it.
    CAPTURE-ONE-LINE-LOOP
    JOIN-WHILE-COND
    SAVE-WHILE-COND
  ELSE
    SAVE-WHILE-COND
    SAME-LINE-DO? 0= IF
      READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV
      S" do" LINE-IS? 0= IF
        ." while: expected 'do'" CR EXIT
      THEN
    THEN
    BEGIN
      READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV
      CAPTURE-CONTINUE?
    WHILE
      APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY
    REPEAT
    \ An unterminated body is a syntax error: report it and run
    \ nothing, rather than executing however much of it was read.
    INPUT-EOF? @ IF EXIT THEN
    4 SAVE-COMPOUND-SUFFIX                ( "done" )
  THEN
  \ LOOP-DEPTH lets break/continue outside any loop be diagnosed;
  \ LOOP-BREAK? is reset fresh for this loop, then checked *before*
  \ each re-evaluation of the real condition below - if the previous
  \ iteration's own DO-WHILE-BODY call set it (via "break"), the real
  \ condition is skipped entirely rather than needlessly re-run, and
  \ this Forth-level WHILE simply stops.
  LOOP-DEPTH @ 1+ LOOP-DEPTH !
  0 LOOP-BREAK? !
  BEGIN
    LOOP-BREAK? @ 0= RETURN-PENDING? @ 0= AND IF
      WHILE-COND-BUF @ BODY@ LINE-BUF WHILE-COND-LEN @ MOVE
      WHILE-COND-LEN @ NORM-TOKENIZE
      RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
      LAST-STATUS @ 0 =
    ELSE
      0
    THEN
  WHILE
    -1 LOOP-RAN? !
    DO-WHILE-BODY
  REPEAT
  0 LOOP-BREAK? !
  LOOP-DEPTH @ 1- LOOP-DEPTH !
  \ POSIX: a loop's status is that of the last body it ran, or zero if
  \ it ran none - NOT the status of the condition that stopped it. The
  \ same rule Iteration 92 applied to an untaken "if", and invisible
  \ for the same reason until something looked at the status
  \ afterwards, which "done && echo m" now does.
  LOOP-RAN? @ 0= IF 0 LAST-STATUS ! THEN
  RUN-COMPOUND-SUFFIX ;

\ ==================================================================
\ for VAR in word1 word2 ... / do / <body> / done
\ Requires "do" on its own, separate line, matching if/while's own
\ established convention here ("for VAR in ...; do" on one line isn't
\ supported, same as "if cond; then" isn't). Reuses while's own
\ body-capture/replay machinery (WHILE-BODY-BUF/APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-
\ BODY/DO-WHILE-BODY) - the mechanics of "store body lines, then
\ replay them" are identical; only the iteration control differs (a
\ fixed word list, expanded once here at the "for ... in ..." line
\ itself, matching POSIX - not re-evaluated each iteration the way
\ while's own condition is). Does NOT support nesting a for/while
\ inside a for's own body (the same shared buffer would be clobbered,
\ the same underlying reason while doesn't support nesting either).
\ Does not validate that ARGV[2] is actually "in" (matching this
\ shell's sparse error handling elsewhere) - it's simply skipped over.
\ ==================================================================

256 CONSTANT FOR-WORDS-MAX
VARIABLE FOR-WORDS-BUF   \ pointer into the arena
VARIABLE FOR-WORDS-LEN
VARIABLE FOR-VARNAME-BUF \ pointer into the arena
VARIABLE FW-I
VARIABLE FW-WORDLEN

: SAVE-FOR-WORDS ( --- )
  \ ARGV[1] (the loop variable name) into FOR-VARNAME-BUF; ARGV[3..]
  \ (the word list, skipping ARGV[2]'s assumed "in") into
  \ FOR-WORDS-BUF/FOR-WORDS-LEN.
  ARGV 1 CELLS + @ FOR-VARNAME-BUF @ BODY@ 64 SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  0 FOR-WORDS-LEN !
  3 FW-I !
  BEGIN
    FW-I @ ARGC @ < IF FW-I @ FOR-AT-SEMI? 0= ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    FW-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN FW-WORDLEN !
    FW-WORDLEN @
    FOR-WORDS-BUF @ BODY@ FOR-WORDS-LEN @ +
    FOR-WORDS-MAX FOR-WORDS-LEN @ -
    SAFE-COPY-NUL IF
      FOR-WORDS-LEN @ FW-WORDLEN @ + 1+ FOR-WORDS-LEN !
    THEN
    FW-I @ 1+ FW-I !
  REPEAT ;

VARIABLE FOR-WORD-I
VARIABLE FOR-WORD-CUR

: DO-FOR-ITERATE ( --- )
  \ For each stored word (NUL-separated in FOR-WORDS-BUF/
  \ FOR-WORDS-LEN), sets the loop variable (SET-SHVAR) and replays the
  \ stored body (DO-WHILE-BODY, shared with while - see above). A
  \ no-op if SUPPRESS-EXEC? is set - this whole for loop sits inside a
  \ skipped if/else branch, so do/body/done were still correctly
  \ consumed above, but nothing should actually run. LOOP-DEPTH/
  \ LOOP-BREAK? mirror DO-WHILE's own use of them exactly - see its
  \ comment for why LOOP-BREAK? is checked before each word rather
  \ than only at the very end.
  SUPPRESS-EXEC? @ IF EXIT THEN
  0 FOR-WORD-I !
  LOOP-DEPTH @ 1+ LOOP-DEPTH !
  0 LOOP-BREAK? !
  BEGIN
    FOR-WORD-I @ FOR-WORDS-LEN @ <
    LOOP-BREAK? @ 0= AND
    RETURN-PENDING? @ 0= AND
  WHILE
    FOR-WORDS-BUF @ BODY@ FOR-WORD-I @ + FOR-WORD-CUR !
    FOR-VARNAME-BUF @ BODY@ FOR-WORD-CUR @ SET-SHVAR
    -1 LOOP-RAN? !
    DO-WHILE-BODY
    FOR-WORD-I @ FOR-WORD-CUR @ CSTRLEN + 1+ FOR-WORD-I !
  REPEAT
  0 LOOP-BREAK? !
  LOOP-DEPTH @ 1- LOOP-DEPTH !
  LOOP-RAN? @ 0= IF 0 LAST-STATUS ! THEN ;

: DO-FOR ( --- )
  \ ARGV[0] is "for"; ARGV[1] is the loop variable name; ARGV[3..] is
  \ the word list (ARGV[2], assumed "in", is skipped over). Body, word
  \ list and variable name all get their own arena storage for this
  \ invocation, same as DO-WHILE - and DO-FOR-ITERATE, called below,
  \ reads these same names, which works because a local IS the
  \ variable and holds this invocation's value for the whole call.
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @
  SHADOW{ BODY-ARENA-TOP | WHILE-BODY-BUF WHILE-BODY-LEN WHILE-BODY-CAP FOR-WORDS-BUF FOR-WORDS-LEN FOR-VARNAME-BUF FOR-WORD-I FOR-WORD-CUR BODY-DEPTH SUF-BUF SUF-LEN LOOP-RAN? }
  WHILE-BODY-MAX BODY-ALLOC WHILE-BODY-BUF !  WHILE-BODY-MAX WHILE-BODY-CAP !
  FOR-WORDS-MAX  BODY-ALLOC FOR-WORDS-BUF !
  64             BODY-ALLOC FOR-VARNAME-BUF !
  \ The word list is expanded before it is saved: `for i in $list`
  \ iterates over the words $list expands to. DO-FOR reads ARGV
  \ directly and never reaches RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE, so it asks for
  \ expansion itself.
  0 WHILE-BODY-LEN !
  0 BODY-DEPTH !
  ONE-LINE-LOOP? IF
    CAPTURE-ONE-LINE-LOOP
    EXPAND-WORDS
    SAVE-FOR-WORDS
  ELSE
    EXPAND-WORDS
    SAVE-FOR-WORDS
    SAME-LINE-DO? 0= IF
      READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV
      S" do" LINE-IS? 0= IF
        ." for: expected 'do'" CR EXIT
      THEN
    THEN
    BEGIN
      READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV
      CAPTURE-CONTINUE?
    WHILE
      APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY
    REPEAT
    INPUT-EOF? @ IF EXIT THEN
    4 SAVE-COMPOUND-SUFFIX                ( "done" )
  THEN
  DO-FOR-ITERATE
  RUN-COMPOUND-SUFFIX ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Glob-pattern matching (*, ?, [...] with a-z ranges and [!...]/[^...]
\ negation) - needed by case/esac below for matching its own patterns
\ against the case word, the same "pattern matching notation" POSIX
\ pathname expansion also uses. The classic iterative two-pointer
\ algorithm (no recursion needed): '*' remembers the pattern/text
\ positions it was seen at, initially matching zero characters; if a
\ later mismatch is hit, backtrack to the most recent '*' and let it
\ consume one more character of text instead, retrying from there.
\ Bracket expressions are handled as a single, variable-length unit
\ within this scan (BRACKET-END finds where one ends in the pattern
\ text; BRACKET-MATCHES? checks membership, including ranges and
\ negation) rather than needing their own separate backtrack state.
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE GM-PATTERN
VARIABLE GM-PLEN
VARIABLE GM-TEXT
VARIABLE GM-TLEN
VARIABLE GM-P
VARIABLE GM-S
VARIABLE GM-STAR-P
VARIABLE GM-STAR-S
VARIABLE GM-HAS-STAR?

VARIABLE BM-NEG?
VARIABLE BM-I
VARIABLE BM-CHAR
VARIABLE BM-END
VARIABLE BM-MATCHED?

: BRACKET-END ( p --- p2 )
  \ GM-PATTERN[p] is '[' - returns the index right after the matching
  \ ']'. A leading '!'/'^' (negation) and a ']' immediately after
  \ that (or right after the '[' itself) are skipped as not being the
  \ closer - POSIX: "[]abc]" and "[!]abc]" both treat that first ']'
  \ as a literal member, not the end of the expression.
  1+
  DUP GM-PLEN @ < IF
    DUP GM-PATTERN @ + C@ 33 = OVER GM-PATTERN @ + C@ 94 = OR IF
      1+
    THEN
  THEN
  DUP GM-PLEN @ < IF
    DUP GM-PATTERN @ + C@ 93 = IF
      1+
    THEN
  THEN
  BEGIN
    DUP GM-PLEN @ < IF DUP GM-PATTERN @ + C@ 93 <> ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    1+
  REPEAT
  DUP GM-PLEN @ < IF 1+ THEN ;

: BRACKET-MATCHES? ( p p2 char --- f )
  \ p is GM-PATTERN's index of the opening '[', p2 the index right
  \ after the matching ']' (from BRACKET-END). Checks whether char is
  \ a member of this set - a leading '!'/'^' negates the whole result,
  \ and "x-y" (three characters, a literal '-' in the middle) is a
  \ range rather than three separate members.
  SHADOW{ BM-I BM-END BM-CHAR | BM-NEG? BM-MATCHED? }
  BM-I @ 1+ BM-I !
  BM-I @ GM-PATTERN @ + C@ 33 = BM-I @ GM-PATTERN @ + C@ 94 = OR IF
    -1 BM-NEG? !
    BM-I @ 1+ BM-I !
  THEN
  BEGIN BM-I @ BM-END @ 1- < WHILE
    BM-I @ 1+ BM-END @ 1- < IF
      BM-I @ 1+ GM-PATTERN @ + C@ 45 =
      BM-I @ 2 + BM-END @ 1- < AND
    ELSE 0 THEN
    IF
      BM-CHAR @ BM-I @ GM-PATTERN @ + C@ < 0=
      BM-I @ 2 + GM-PATTERN @ + C@ BM-CHAR @ < 0=
      AND IF -1 BM-MATCHED? ! THEN
      BM-I @ 3 + BM-I !
    ELSE
      BM-I @ GM-PATTERN @ + C@ BM-CHAR @ = IF -1 BM-MATCHED? ! THEN
      BM-I @ 1+ BM-I !
    THEN
  REPEAT
  BM-MATCHED? @ BM-NEG? @ IF 0= ELSE THEN ;

VARIABLE GCM-P
VARIABLE GCM-CHAR
VARIABLE GCM-BEND

: GLOB-CHAR-MATCHES? ( p-idx char --- match? p-idx-after )
  \ Does GM-PATTERN[p-idx] (a '?', a bracket expression, or a single
  \ literal character) match char? p-idx-after is where to resume
  \ pattern scanning next, regardless of match/no-match - a bracket
  \ expression can span many pattern characters, unlike '?' or a
  \ literal, which only ever advance by one.
  SHADOW{ GCM-P GCM-CHAR | GCM-BEND }
  GCM-P @ GM-PATTERN @ + C@
  DUP 63 = IF
    DROP
    -1 GCM-P @ 1+
  ELSE
    91 = IF
      GCM-P @ BRACKET-END GCM-BEND !
      GCM-P @ GCM-BEND @ GCM-CHAR @ BRACKET-MATCHES?
      GCM-BEND @
    ELSE
      GCM-P @ GM-PATTERN @ + C@ GCM-CHAR @ =
      GCM-P @ 1+
    THEN
  THEN ;

: GLOB-MATCH ( pattern-addr pattern-len text-addr text-len --- f )
  SHADOW{ GM-PATTERN GM-PLEN GM-TEXT GM-TLEN | GM-P GM-S GM-STAR-P GM-STAR-S GM-HAS-STAR? }
  BEGIN GM-S @ GM-TLEN @ < WHILE
    GM-P @ GM-PLEN @ < IF
      GM-P @ GM-PATTERN @ + C@ 42 =
    ELSE 0 THEN
    IF
      -1 GM-HAS-STAR? !
      GM-P @ GM-STAR-P !
      GM-S @ GM-STAR-S !
      GM-P @ 1+ GM-P !
    ELSE
      GM-P @ GM-PLEN @ < IF
        GM-P @ GM-TEXT @ GM-S @ + C@ GLOB-CHAR-MATCHES?
      ELSE 0 0 THEN
      OVER IF
        SWAP DROP GM-P !
        GM-S @ 1+ GM-S !
      ELSE
        DROP DROP
        GM-HAS-STAR? @ IF
          GM-STAR-P @ 1+ GM-P !
          GM-STAR-S @ 1+ DUP GM-STAR-S !
          GM-S !
        ELSE
          0 EXIT
        THEN
      THEN
    THEN
  REPEAT
  BEGIN
    GM-P @ GM-PLEN @ < IF GM-P @ GM-PATTERN @ + C@ 42 = ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    GM-P @ 1+ GM-P !
  REPEAT
  GM-P @ GM-PLEN @ = ;

\ ==================================================================
\ ${VAR%word}/${VAR%%word}/${VAR#word}/${VAR##word} - suffix/prefix
\ removal, the real implementation behind the deferred TRIM-PARAM-CALL
\ EXPAND-BRACED-VAR uses (see its own comment for why this is
\ deferred: GLOB-MATCH, needed here, is defined much later in the file
\ than EXPAND-BRACED-VAR is). Each of the four modes searches for the
\ shortest or longest prefix/suffix of the variable's own value that
\ GLOB-MATCH accepts as a whole match against the pattern, by trying
\ candidate lengths one at a time - 0,1,2,...,value-len for shortest,
\ value-len,value-len-1,...,0 for longest - stopping at the first
\ length that matches. If nothing matches at any length (including 0
\ and the full value-len), the value is emitted unchanged, per POSIX.
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE FPM-PAT-ADDR
VARIABLE FPM-PAT-LEN
VARIABLE FPM-VAL-ADDR
VARIABLE FPM-VAL-LEN
VARIABLE FPM-SUFFIX?
VARIABLE FPM-LONGEST?
VARIABLE FPM-I

: FPM-CANDIDATE ( --- c-addr u )
  \ The candidate substring of the value that is FPM-I characters long:
  \ its first FPM-I characters when trimming a prefix, its last FPM-I
  \ when trimming a suffix. Reads FIND-TRIM-LEN's own locals, which
  \ hold this invocation's values for as long as the call is running.
  FPM-SUFFIX? @ IF
    FPM-VAL-ADDR @ FPM-VAL-LEN @ FPM-I @ - +
  ELSE
    FPM-VAL-ADDR @
  THEN
  FPM-I @ ;

: FPM-MATCHES? ( --- f )
  FPM-PAT-ADDR @ FPM-PAT-LEN @ FPM-CANDIDATE GLOB-MATCH ;

: FIND-TRIM-LEN ( pat-addr pat-len val-addr val-len suffix? longest? --- match-len | -1 )
  \ One word replacing what were four near-identical ones
  \ (FIND-SHORTEST/LONGEST-PREFIX/SUFFIX-LEN). They only ever differed
  \ in two things: which end of the value the candidate is taken from,
  \ and whether candidate lengths are tried upward from 0 or downward
  \ from the full length. Both are now parameters. Returns the length
  \ of the first candidate GLOB-MATCH accepts, or -1 if no length
  \ matches at all - including 0 and the full value length - in which
  \ case POSIX says the value is emitted unchanged.
  SHADOW{ FPM-PAT-ADDR FPM-PAT-LEN FPM-VAL-ADDR FPM-VAL-LEN FPM-SUFFIX? FPM-LONGEST? | FPM-I }
  FPM-LONGEST? @ IF FPM-VAL-LEN @ FPM-I ! THEN
  BEGIN
    FPM-MATCHES? 0=
    FPM-LONGEST? @ IF FPM-I @ 0 > ELSE FPM-I @ FPM-VAL-LEN @ < THEN
    AND
  WHILE
    FPM-LONGEST? @ IF FPM-I @ 1- ELSE FPM-I @ 1+ THEN FPM-I !
  REPEAT
  FPM-MATCHES? IF FPM-I @ ELSE -1 THEN ;

: TYPE-N-TO-TOK ( addr n --- )
  \ Like TYPE0-TO-TOK, but for a known-length span rather than a
  \ NUL-terminated C string - the remaining portion after a prefix/
  \ suffix match isn't itself NUL-terminated at the right point
  \ The zero guard is not optional: this kernel's DO with start = limit
  \ runs the whole unsigned range rather than zero iterations
  \ (FORTH-STYLE.md section 12). n IS zero whenever a trim consumes the
  \ entire value - "${p%%/*}", "${p##*}" - and every one of those
  \ segfaulted.
  DUP 0 > IF
    0 DO
      DUP I + C@ EMIT-TOK-CHAR
    LOOP
  ELSE
    DROP
  THEN
  DROP ;

VARIABLE TP-VAL-ADDR
VARIABLE TP-VAL-LEN
VARIABLE TP-MATCH-LEN

: TRIM-PARAM ( --- )
  \ VARNAME-BUF names the variable; PEWORD-BUF/PEWORD-LEN holds the
  \ already-read pattern; PEW-SUFFIX?/PEW-LONGEST? (set by
  \ EXPAND-BRACED-VAR before calling TRIM-PARAM-CALL) select which of
  \ the four removal modes applies. An unset variable expands to
  \ empty (nothing to trim).
  SHADOW{ | TP-VAL-ADDR TP-VAL-LEN TP-MATCH-LEN }
  VARNAME-BUF LOOKUP-VAR
  DUP 0= IF DROP EXIT THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN TP-VAL-LEN ! TP-VAL-ADDR !
  PEWORD-BUF PEWORD-LEN @ TP-VAL-ADDR @ TP-VAL-LEN @
  PEW-SUFFIX? @ PEW-LONGEST? @ FIND-TRIM-LEN
  TP-MATCH-LEN !
  TP-MATCH-LEN @ -1 = IF
    TP-VAL-ADDR @ TP-VAL-LEN @ TYPE-N-TO-TOK
  ELSE
    PEW-SUFFIX? @ IF
      TP-VAL-ADDR @ TP-VAL-LEN @ TP-MATCH-LEN @ - TYPE-N-TO-TOK
    ELSE
      TP-VAL-ADDR @ TP-MATCH-LEN @ + TP-VAL-LEN @ TP-MATCH-LEN @ - TYPE-N-TO-TOK
    THEN
  THEN ;
' TRIM-PARAM IS TRIM-PARAM-CALL

\ ==================================================================
\ case WORD in / PATTERN1) / <body> / ;; / PATTERN2|PATTERN3) / ... /
\ esac
\ Requires each pattern arm on its own line, and ';;' (or the arm
\ ending directly at "esac", for the last item, per POSIX) on its own
\ line too - no same-line support yet, matching while/for. Each
\ pattern (or '|'-separated alternative) is glob-matched against the
\ case word saved when "case WORD in" was first read; the first arm
\ with any matching alternative has its body run, and no further arms
\ are even inspected afterward - unlike a C switch, there's no
\ fall-through. A pattern arm's own trailing ')' isn't its own token
\ (unlike '('/')' used for command grouping, ')' here isn't spaced
\ out by NORMALIZE-OPERATORS - it stays fused to the last character of
\ whatever precedes it, e.g. "he*)" tokenizes as one token), so
\ CASE-ARM-MATCHES? strips it from the *last* ARGV token specifically
\ before testing it as a pattern.
\ ==================================================================

256 BUFFER: CASE-WORD-BUF
VARIABLE CASE-WORD-LEN
256 BUFFER: CASE-PATLAST-BUF
VARIABLE CASE-PATLAST-LEN
VARIABLE CA-I
VARIABLE CASE-MATCHED?

: CASE-ARM-MATCHES? ( --- f )
  \ ARGV/ARGC hold the current pattern-arm line (e.g. ["hello)"] or
  \ ["world","|","hello)"]). The last token's own trailing ')' is
  \ stripped into CASE-PATLAST-BUF; every non-'|' token (including
  \ that stripped last one) is tried as an independent alternative
  \ against the saved CASE-WORD-BUF - any one matching is enough to
  \ select this arm, matching real shells' "PAT1|PAT2|..." semantics.
  \ No trailing-')' stripping any more: DO-CASE splits the arm at its
  \ ')' before calling this, and Iteration 48 made ')' its own token
  \ in any case, so every remaining token is a whole pattern.
  SHADOW{ | CA-I }
  BEGIN CA-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    \ '|' separates alternatives; ')' is now its own token too, since
    \ Iteration 48 made parens self-delimiting - before that it stayed
    \ fused to the last pattern and was stripped below. Both are
    \ skipped rather than treated as patterns; without this the ')'
    \ would be tried as an empty pattern, which would wrongly match a
    \ case word that happened to be empty.
    CA-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@ 0= IF
      CA-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" |" STR=
      CA-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" )" STR= OR
    ELSE 0 THEN
    0= IF
      CA-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN
      CASE-WORD-BUF CASE-WORD-LEN @ GLOB-MATCH IF -1 EXIT THEN
    THEN
    CA-I @ 1+ CA-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ;

: DO-CASE ( --- )
  \ ARGV[0] is "case"; ARGV[1] is the case word (already $VAR-
  \ expanded during tokenizing); ARGV[2] is skipped, assumed "in" (not
  \ validated, matching for's own convention). Respects an enclosing
  \ SUPPRESS-EXEC? throughout - a matched arm's body only actually runs
  \ if the outer context wasn't already suppressed either. Once one arm
  \ has matched, every later one is skipped without even being tested,
  \ via CASE-MATCHED? - real case statements never fall through the
  \ way a C switch can (found and fixed as a real bug: an earlier
  \ version set CASE-MATCHED? but never actually checked it, so a
  \ later arm - even a non-matching one - kept right on being tested
  \ and, if it matched, running its body too).
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @  SHADOW{ BODY-ARENA-TOP | SUF-BUF SUF-LEN }
  SUPPRESS-EXEC? @ >R
  \ Same as DO-FOR: the case word is read straight out of ARGV, so
  \ this path asks for expansion itself.
  EXPAND-WORDS
  ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN
  DUP CASE-WORD-LEN !
  CASE-WORD-BUF 256 SAFE-COPY-NUL DROP
  0 CASE-MATCHED? !
  BEGIN
    READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
    INPUT-EOF? @ IF
      ." shell: syntax error: unexpected end of input, expected 'esac'" CR
      2 LAST-STATUS !
      R> SUPPRESS-EXEC? ! EXIT
    THEN
    S" esac" LINE-IS? 0=
  WHILE
    \ "pat) body ;;" all on one line: split at the ')' so ARGV holds
    \ only the patterns and the body becomes the pending remainder,
    \ exactly as DO-IF handles a same-line "then".
    S" )" SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD DROP
    \ The patterns are expanded, the body is not - the body goes back
    \ through RUN-TOKENIZED, which expands each of its commands at the
    \ point that command runs. Splitting at the ')' first is what
    \ separates the two, and is why this call is here rather than at
    \ the top of the loop.
    EXPAND-WORDS
    CASE-MATCHED? @ 0= CASE-ARM-MATCHES? AND IF
      -1 CASE-MATCHED? !
      R@ SUPPRESS-EXEC? !
    ELSE
      -1 SUPPRESS-EXEC? !
    THEN
    BEGIN
      READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
      INPUT-EOF? @ IF
        ." shell: syntax error: unexpected end of input, expected 'esac'" CR
        2 LAST-STATUS !
        R> SUPPRESS-EXEC? ! EXIT
      THEN
      S" esac" LINE-IS? IF
        4 SAVE-COMPOUND-SUFFIX
        R> SUPPRESS-EXEC? ! RUN-COMPOUND-SUFFIX EXIT
      THEN
      S" ;;" LINE-IS? IF -1 ELSE
        \ ";;" may end this arm from within a body line rather than
        \ having a line of its own.
        S" ;;" SPLIT-AT-KEYWORD IF
          RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
          ARGC-PENDING @ 0= IF 0 PENDING-REMAINDER? ! THEN
          -1
        ELSE
          RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
          0
        THEN
      THEN
    UNTIL
  REPEAT
  4 SAVE-COMPOUND-SUFFIX                  ( "esac" )
  R> SUPPRESS-EXEC? !
  RUN-COMPOUND-SUFFIX ;

VARIABLE FD-ADDR
VARIABLE FD-LEN

: FUNCDEF-NAME? ( --- f )
  \ Is this line a function-definition header?
  \
  \ Since Iteration 48 made '(' and ')' self-delimiting, "name() {"
  \ tokenizes as four tokens - name, "(", ")", "{" - not as a single
  \ "name()" token with the parens fused on, which is what this used
  \ to look for. Checking the three-token shape instead. (Missing this
  \ is what broke every function test when parens became operators.)
  ARGC @ 3 < IF 0 EXIT THEN
  0 ARGV-QUOTED@ IF 0 EXIT THEN
  1 ARGV-QUOTED@ IF 0 EXIT THEN
  2 ARGV-QUOTED@ IF 0 EXIT THEN
  1 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" (" STR= 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" )" STR= 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  0 ARGV@ CSTRLEN 0 > ;

VARIABLE RT-REST
VARIABLE RT-RESTQ
VARIABLE RT-RESTC
VARIABLE RT-I

: RT-SAVE-REST ( --- )
  \ Copy the ';'-remainder out of the shared ARGV-SEMI-REST arrays into
  \ per-invocation arena storage, BEFORE running the part before the
  \ ';'.
  \
  \ Those arrays are globals, and running the left-hand part can
  \ recurse all the way back into SPLIT-SEMI - a brace group does
  \ exactly that, since its body is run through RUN-TOKENIZED. The
  \ remainder was then overwritten and silently dropped:
  \ "{ echo x; { echo y; }; echo z; }" printed x and y but never z.
  \ Pre-existing; it needed a group nested inside a ';' list to show.
  ARGC-SEMI-REST @ RT-RESTC !
  MAX-ARGS CELLS BODY-ALLOC RT-REST !
  MAX-ARGS       BODY-ALLOC RT-RESTQ !
  0 RT-I !
  BEGIN RT-I @ RT-RESTC @ < WHILE
    ARGV-SEMI-REST RT-I @ CELLS + @  RT-REST  @ BODY@ RT-I @ CELLS + !
    ARGQ-SEMI-REST RT-I @ +      C@  RT-RESTQ @ BODY@ RT-I @ +       C!
    RT-I @ 1+ RT-I !
  REPEAT ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Aliases
\ ==================================================================
\ Textual substitution of the first word of a command, done before the
\ line is interpreted - "completely lexical", as Ramey puts it, which
\ is why an alias can introduce operators and even change the grammar
\ of the line it appears in. The expansion re-normalizes and
\ re-tokenizes, so "alias ll='ls -l'" and "alias e='echo a;echo b'"
\ both behave.
\
\ ALIAS-DEPTH stops "alias ls='ls -l'" from recursing forever. Real
\ shells suppress re-expansion of the alias currently being expanded;
\ a depth cap is cruder but needs no per-expansion state, and the
\ failure mode is a diagnosed stop rather than a hang.

: AL-APPEND ( c-addr u --- )
  SHADOW{ AL-NAME AL-LEN }
  ALIAS-LINE-LEN @ AL-LEN @ + 2 + ENSURE-BUFFER ALIAS-LINE
  ALIAS-LINE-LEN @ AL-LEN @ + LINE-HARD-MAX < IF
    AL-NAME @ ALIAS-LINE ALIAS-LINE-LEN @ + AL-LEN @ MOVE
    ALIAS-LINE-LEN @ AL-LEN @ + ALIAS-LINE-LEN !
    32 ALIAS-LINE ALIAS-LINE-LEN @ + C!
    ALIAS-LINE-LEN @ 1+ ALIAS-LINE-LEN !
  THEN ;

: TRY-ALIAS ( --- f )
  \ f = -1 if ARGV[0] was an alias and the rebuilt line has been run.
  ARGC @ 0 = IF 0 EXIT THEN
  0 ARGV-QUOTED@ IF 0 EXIT THEN
  ALIAS-DEPTH @ 8 > IF
    ." alias: expansion too deep" CR
    0 EXIT
  THEN
  ARGV @ FIND-ALIAS DUP -1 = IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  0 ALIAS-LINE-LEN !
  ALIAS-VALUE-SLOT DUP CSTRLEN AL-APPEND
  1 AL-I !
  BEGIN AL-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    AL-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN AL-APPEND
    AL-I @ 1+ AL-I !
  REPEAT
  ALIAS-LINE LINE-BUF ALIAS-LINE-LEN @ MOVE
  ALIAS-DEPTH @ 1+ ALIAS-DEPTH !
  ALIAS-LINE-LEN @ NORM-TOKENIZE
  RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
  ALIAS-DEPTH @ 1- ALIAS-DEPTH !
  -1 ;

: DO-ALIAS ( --- )
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  1 AL-I !
  BEGIN AL-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    AL-I @ ARGV@ ASSIGNMENT-EQPOS
    DUP -1 = IF
      DROP
    ELSE
      AL-I @ ARGV@ SWAP SPLIT-ASSIGN-AT
      ASSIGN-NAME-BUF ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF SET-ALIAS
    THEN
    AL-I @ 1+ AL-I !
  REPEAT ;

: DO-UNALIAS ( --- )
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  1 AL-I !
  BEGIN AL-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    AL-I @ ARGV@ FIND-ALIAS DUP -1 <> IF
      ALIAS-COUNT @ 1- OVER <> IF
        ALIAS-COUNT @ 1- ALIAS-NAME-SLOT  OVER ALIAS-NAME-SLOT  ALIAS-NAME-MAX  MOVE
        ALIAS-COUNT @ 1- ALIAS-VALUE-SLOT OVER ALIAS-VALUE-SLOT ALIAS-VALUE-MAX MOVE
      THEN
      DROP ALIAS-COUNT @ 1- ALIAS-COUNT !
    ELSE DROP THEN
    AL-I @ 1+ AL-I !
  REPEAT ;

' DO-ALIAS   S" alias"   BUILTIN
' DO-UNALIAS S" unalias" BUILTIN

VARIABLE MG-SUB?
VARIABLE MG-DEPTH

: MG-END? ( --- f )
  \ True at the ")" or "}" that closes THIS group, counting nesting -
  \ the same shape as FD-BODY-END? and CAPTURE-CONTINUE?.
  INPUT-EOF? @ IF
    ." shell: syntax error: unexpected end of input, unterminated group" CR
    2 LAST-STATUS !
    -1 EXIT
  THEN
  MG-SUB? @ IF S" )" LINE-IS? ELSE S" }" LINE-IS? THEN IF
    MG-DEPTH @ 0 > IF MG-DEPTH @ 1- MG-DEPTH ! 0 ELSE -1 THEN
  ELSE
    MG-SUB? @ IF S" (" LINE-IS? ELSE FD-OPENS-BRACE? THEN IF
      MG-DEPTH @ 1+ MG-DEPTH !
    THEN
    0
  THEN ;

: DO-MULTILINE-GROUP ( --- )
  \ A group whose opener is alone on its line:
  \
  \     (
  \         echo a
  \     )
  \
  \ SPLIT-GROUP only handles a group contained in one line, so this
  \ captures the body the way DO-WHILE and DO-FUNCDEF do and replays
  \ it through DO-WHILE-BODY. A "(" group runs in a fork, a "{" group
  \ in this shell - the same distinction SPLIT-GROUP's two callers
  \ make.
  \
  \ Known wart: DO-WHILE-BODY clears LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? on the way
  \ out, so a "break" inside a multi-line group nested in a loop is
  \ swallowed rather than propagating. Reusing it is still better than
  \ a second copy of the replay loop; the flag handling wants
  \ factoring out when something needs it.
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @
  SHADOW{ BODY-ARENA-TOP | WHILE-BODY-BUF WHILE-BODY-LEN WHILE-BODY-CAP MG-DEPTH }
  WHILE-BODY-MAX BODY-ALLOC WHILE-BODY-BUF !  WHILE-BODY-MAX WHILE-BODY-CAP !
  0 WHILE-BODY-LEN !
  BEGIN
    READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
    MG-END? 0=
  WHILE
    ARGC @ 0 > IF APPEND-RAW-LINE-TO-BODY THEN
  REPEAT
  INPUT-EOF? @ IF EXIT THEN
  MG-SUB? @ IF
    FORK DUP 0 = IF
      DROP
      DO-WHILE-BODY
      LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT
    THEN
    WAITPID DROP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND LAST-STATUS !
  ELSE
    DO-WHILE-BODY
  THEN ;

: (DO-MULTILINE-GROUP) ( sub? --- )
  \ The DEFER'd form DISPATCH-GROUP calls. It takes the subshell flag
  \ on the stack rather than having each caller store MG-SUB? itself,
  \ so that variable stays private to this end of the file.
  MG-SUB? ! DO-MULTILINE-GROUP ;

' (DO-MULTILINE-GROUP) IS DO-MULTILINE-GROUP-CALL

VARIABLE PP-I
VARIABLE PP-BAR

: PP-COMPOUND-AFTER-PIPE? ( --- f )
  \ Scanned from ARGV, before SPLIT-PIPE has run: is the token after
  \ the last unquoted "|" a multi-line compound keyword?
  -1 PP-BAR !  0 PP-I !
  BEGIN PP-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    PP-I @ ARGV-QUOTED@ 0= IF
      PP-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN S" |" STR= IF PP-I @ PP-BAR ! THEN
    THEN
    PP-I @ 1+ PP-I !
  REPEAT
  PP-BAR @ -1 = IF 0 EXIT THEN
  PP-BAR @ 1+ DUP ARGC @ < 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN
  2DUP S" while" STR= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" for"   STR= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" if"    STR= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
       S" case"  STR= ;

: PB-APPEND ( --- )
  PIPE-BODY-LEN @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ + 1+ 8192 < IF
    RAW-LINE-BUF PIPE-BODY PIPE-BODY-LEN @ + RAW-LINE-LEN @ MOVE
    PIPE-BODY-LEN @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ + PIPE-BODY-LEN !
    0 PIPE-BODY PIPE-BODY-LEN @ + C!
    PIPE-BODY-LEN @ 1+ PIPE-BODY-LEN !
  THEN ;

: PP-TRIM-TO-STAGE ( --- )
  RAW-LINE-BUF RAW-LINE-LEN @ 124 RAW-LAST-CHAR
  DUP -1 = IF DROP EXIT THEN
  1+ PLS-I !
  BEGIN
    PLS-I @ RAW-LINE-LEN @ < IF RAW-LINE-BUF PLS-I @ + C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE PLS-I @ 1+ PLS-I ! REPEAT
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ PLS-I @ - 0 > IF
    RAW-LINE-BUF PLS-I @ + RAW-LINE-BUF RAW-LINE-LEN @ PLS-I @ - MOVE
    RAW-LINE-LEN @ PLS-I @ - RAW-LINE-LEN !
  ELSE
    0 RAW-LINE-LEN !
  THEN ;

: PREPARE-COMPOUND-PIPE ( --- )
  0 PIPE-HAVE-BODY !
  PP-COMPOUND-AFTER-PIPE? 0= IF EXIT THEN
  \ If the construct is already complete on this line there is nothing
  \ to capture - and capturing anyway would swallow the NEXT line as
  \ body. A fully one-line "while ...; do ...; done" is complete, and
  \ DO-WHILE handles it directly since Iteration 117.
  FDC-BALANCED? IF EXIT THEN
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER PIPE-RAW-FULL
  RAW-LINE-BUF PIPE-RAW-FULL RAW-LINE-LEN @ MOVE
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ PIPE-RAW-FULL-LEN !
  0 PIPE-BODY-LEN !
  0 FDC-DEPTH !
  BEGIN
    READ-NEXT-LOGICAL-LINE
    FDC-LINE-END? 0=
  WHILE
    ARGC @ 0 > IF PB-APPEND THEN
  REPEAT
  PB-APPEND
  PIPE-RAW-FULL RAW-LINE-BUF PIPE-RAW-FULL-LEN @ MOVE
  PIPE-RAW-FULL-LEN @ RAW-LINE-LEN !
  PP-TRIM-TO-STAGE
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER PIPE-RAW
  RAW-LINE-BUF PIPE-RAW RAW-LINE-LEN @ MOVE
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ PIPE-RAW-LEN !
  PIPE-RAW-FULL RAW-LINE-BUF PIPE-RAW-FULL-LEN @ MOVE
  PIPE-RAW-FULL-LEN @ RAW-LINE-LEN !
  PIPE-RAW-FULL LINE-BUF PIPE-RAW-FULL-LEN @ MOVE
  PIPE-RAW-FULL-LEN @ NORM-TOKENIZE
  -1 PIPE-HAVE-BODY ! ;

' PREPARE-COMPOUND-PIPE IS PREPARE-PIPE-CALL

: RUN-TOKENIZED ( --- )
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @
  SHADOW{ BODY-ARENA-TOP | RT-REST RT-RESTQ RT-RESTC RT-I }
  \ Alias expansion first: it is textual, and the rebuilt line must
  \ go through every check below as if it had been typed.
  TRY-ALIAS IF EXIT THEN
  \ Acts on the currently-tokenized ARGV/ARGC: '(' / '{' group
  \ detection first of all (see DO-SUBSHELL/DO-BRACE-GROUP - a
  \ group's own ';'/'&&'/'||' must stay scoped to its body rather than
  \ being split as if it belonged to the outer line, so this has to
  \ run before any of that), then control-structure keywords (for the
  \ same reason as groups - "if true; then echo hi; fi" needs "if" to
  \ see its own ';'/"then"/"fi" intact, not have the outer ';'-split
  \ carve it up first; while/for don't support same-line "do" yet, but
  \ moving their own detection here too avoids a *worse*, different
  \ misbehavior than simply keeping "do" on its own line already was -
  \ "while true; do" was already broken before this reordering too),
  \ then ';' splitting (so each ';'-separated piece gets the full
  \ treatment below on its own - see SPLIT-SEMI), then assignment
  \ detection, then &&/|| splitting (see SPLIT-ANDOR/RUN-AND-OR-CHAIN
  \ - POSIX gives '&&'/'||' equal, left-to-right precedence, tighter
  \ than ';' but looser than '|'), then a plain pipeline/simple command
  \ (RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE, which is what each &&/||-separated piece
  \ above also runs through). Shared by RUN-LINE (after its own
  \ TOKENIZE call, below) and DO-IF's own body-line handling above (via
  \ RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL, patched to this word's xt right after it's
  \ defined).
  \ A group is handled here only when it IS the entire line. If
  \ anything follows its closing ')'/'}' - a pipe, a '&&', a ';' -
  \ fall through to the splitters instead, which now know not to
  \ split inside the group (GRP-DEPTH), and the group ends up as one
  \ segment handled by RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE below. Before this, a
  \ trailing pipe or operator after a group was silently dropped -
  \ the scope limit recorded in Iteration 20.
  DISPATCH-GROUP IF EXIT THEN
  S" if" LINE-IS? IF DO-IF EXIT THEN
  S" while" LINE-IS? IF DO-WHILE EXIT THEN
  S" for" LINE-IS? IF DO-FOR EXIT THEN
  S" case" LINE-IS? IF DO-CASE EXIT THEN
  FUNCDEF-NAME? IF DO-FUNCDEF EXIT THEN
  SPLIT-SEMI IF
    RT-SAVE-REST
    ARGV-SEMI-LEFT ARGQ-SEMI-LEFT ARGC-SEMI-LEFT @ COPY-ARGV-Q
    RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
    RT-REST @ BODY@ RT-RESTQ @ BODY@ RT-RESTC @ COPY-ARGV-Q
    RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL
    EXIT
  THEN
  SPLIT-ANDOR IF
    RUN-AND-OR-CHAIN
    EXIT
  THEN
  RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE ;
' RUN-TOKENIZED IS RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL


: RUN-LINE ( u --- )  \ LINE-BUF already holds u bytes: tokenize, then
                        \ act on it via RUN-TOKENIZED.
                        \ Shared by the interactive loop (SH1) and -c
                        \ mode (SH-C) below, so both go through
                        \ identical logic. Note: redirection only
                        \ applies to external commands (see
                        \ APPLY-REDIRECTIONS) - "pwd > file" doesn't
                        \ redirect the builtin's own output in v0.2.
                        \ Also stashes a raw copy, same reason as
                        \ READ-LINE-INTO-ARGV above - taken *before*
                        \ operator normalization, so it holds the
                        \ line exactly as given, unmodified.
  JOIN-CONTINUATIONS
  JOIN-OPEN-QUOTES
  DUP RAW-LINE-LEN !
  RAW-LINE-LEN @ 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER RAW-LINE-BUF
  LINE-BUF RAW-LINE-BUF RAW-LINE-LEN @ MOVE
  \ TOKENIZE, not NORM-TOKENIZE: JOIN-OPEN-QUOTES has just normalized
  \ this exact line - that is how it decides whether a quote is still
  \ open - so NORM-BUF and the word spans are already there.
  \ Normalizing a second time was pure repeated work on every line of
  \ every loop body (Iteration 119).
  TOKENIZE
  UNTERMINATED-QUOTE? @ IF
    ." shell: syntax error: unterminated quote" CR
    2 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
  THEN
  \ A line with more words than ARGV holds is refused rather than run
  \ short. Running it would execute a command the script did not
  \ write, which is strictly worse than not running it - and the old
  \ behaviour did exactly that, silently. Reported on fd 2, like the
  \ other Iteration 154 diagnostic and unlike the older ones above.
  ARGS-OVERFLOW? @ IF
    0 ARGS-OVERFLOW? !
    S" shell: too many arguments" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
    1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
  THEN
  RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL ;
' RUN-LINE IS RUN-LINE-CALL

: SH1 ( --- )  \ process one interactively-read line; returns either way
  \ Nothing can point into a retired buffer between commands.
  BUF-FREE-RETIRED
  SH-PROMPT
  0 BUFFER-BODY LINE-BUF ACCEPT-INTO
  \ Terminates the prompt line, so it belongs with the prompt on fd 2
  \ rather than on stdout. As a CR it put a stray newline into the
  \ output of every piped script.
  SH-NL 1 2 WRITE-FILE DROP
  RUN-LINE ;

: SH ( --- )
  BEGIN SH1 0 UNTIL ;

: SH-FILE ( c-addr --- )  \ c-addr: a NUL-terminated file path (from
                            \ SYS-ARG). Opens it read-only, runs each
                            \ line through RUN-LINE the same way SH1
                            \ does for one interactively-read line
                            \ (READ-LINE's own u2 lines up exactly with
                            \ what RUN-LINE expects), and exits with
                            \ the last command's status once EOF is
                            \ reached - matching `sh script.sh`. An
                            \ "exit" mid-script still exits immediately
                            \ via SYS-EXIT inside RUN-LINE's own
                            \ DISPATCH, same as everywhere else. No
                            \ error message on a missing/unreadable
                            \ file, just a nonzero exit status -
                            \ matching this shell's existing sparse
                            \ error handling elsewhere (see
                            \ APPLY-REDIRECTIONS).
  R/O OPEN-FOR 0= IF 127 SYS-EXIT THEN
  SHFILE-FID !
  -1 SHFILE-ACTIVE? !
  BEGIN
    BUF-FREE-RETIRED   \ between commands: see SH1
    0 BUFFER-BODY LINE-BUF SHFILE-FID @ READ-LINE-INTO
  WHILE
    RUN-LINE
  REPEAT
  DROP
  SHFILE-FID @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
  LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT ;

VARIABLE SHC-LEN

: SH-C ( c-addr --- )  \ c-addr: a NUL-terminated command string (from
                        \ SYS-ARG). Runs it once and exits with its status -
                        \ never returns. The same one logical line SH1
                        \ would read - ';'/'&&'/'||' chaining all work
                        \ (RUN-LINE/RUN-TOKENIZED handle those, not
                        \ TOKENIZE itself), but no if/while/for, which
                        \ need multiple lines.
  -1 NO-MORE-INPUT? !
  DUP CSTRLEN SHC-LEN !
  \ Grown to fit first: until Iteration 249 a -c string longer than
  \ LINE-BUF was copied past its end.
  SHC-LEN @ LINE-HARD-MAX > IF DROP LINE-TOO-LONG LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT THEN
  SHC-LEN @ 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER LINE-BUF
  LINE-BUF SHC-LEN @ MOVE
  SHC-LEN @ RUN-LINE
  LAST-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT ;

: MAIN ( --- )  \ entry point once shell.4 is loaded: `relf kernel.img -c
                  \ "cmd"` (via SYS-ARGC/SYS-ARG, exposing relf's own argv
                  \ beyond the image path) runs one command non-interactively
                  \ and exits with its status - matching invoking any real
                  \ shell binary as `sh -c "cmd"`. `relf kernel.img
                  \ script.sh` (via relfsh) runs script.sh's contents line
                  \ by line and exits with the last command's status -
                  \ matching `sh script.sh` (see SH-FILE). Any further
                  \ arguments beyond the script path are currently ignored
                  \ (shell.4 has no positional-parameter support yet).
                  \ No args at all falls through to the ordinary
                  \ interactive loop.
  \ The body arena is heap memory, so its base address is meaningless
  \ after an image is saved and reloaded. Reset here, at the boot
  \ word, rather than relying on it happening to be zero because no
  \ loop ran during the image build.
  \ Every pool.4 buffer's pointer was zeroed by RESET-BUFFERS before
  \ this image was written, because a heap address means nothing after
  \ a save and reload. Nothing below may touch a buffer before this
  \ runs, so it is the first thing in the boot word.
  ALLOC-BUFFERS
  0 BODY-ARENA-BASE !  0 BODY-ARENA-SIZE !  0 BODY-ARENA-TOP !
  \ A turnkey image boots straight here from COLD, so WARM and QUIT -
  \ which normally set up the search order and the input source -
  \ never run. Nothing in the shell needed them until the `forth`
  \ builtin, whose EVALUATE calls FIND and segfaulted on an empty
  \ search order. Doing that setup here rather than in COLD keeps the
  \ kernel unchanged and keeps it where the reason for it lives.
  2 #ORDER !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CONTEXT !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CONTEXT CELL+ !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CURRENT !
  TIB SRC !  0 SID !
  INCLUDE-BUFFER INCLUDE-POINTER !
  SYS-ARGC 2 = IF
    S" -c" 0 SYS-ARG STR0= IF
      1 SYS-ARG SH-C
    THEN
  THEN
  SYS-ARGC 0 > IF
    SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-SYS-ARGS
    0 SYS-ARG SH-FILE
  THEN
  SH ;
